window.BS_DEEP={"foxo4-dri":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and what the original paper actually claimed","where":"animals","body":["It came out of one 2017 paper from a Dutch group, which identified FOXO4 as what they called a pivot in senescent cell viability, designed a peptide to break the FOXO4 to p53 interaction, and showed that this pushes p53 out of the nucleus in senescent cells and triggers their self-destruction [1] (verified).","The paper's three in-vivo claims are worth stating precisely, because they are usually compressed into \"it rejuvenated old mice\". It neutralised the tissue damage caused by doxorubicin, a chemotherapy drug; and in both fast-ageing XpdTTD mice and naturally aged mice it restored fitness, fur density and kidney function [1] (verified). The abstract reports no effect sizes, and the full paper could not be opened for this review, so the magnitudes behind those three words are not quoted here.","The design is the cleverest part. It is a retro-inverso peptide: mirror-image D-amino acids in reversed order, which keeps the shape that binds p53 while making it unreadable to the enzymes that would otherwise digest it, plus a stretch of positively charged residues to carry it into cells [2] (verified from the published sequence). That is why it is injected rather than infused as a protein, and why it is not comparable to ordinary peptides on stability."]},{"h":"The selectivity argument, and the one study that tested it carefully","where":"cells","body":["The claim that healthy cells are spared rests on them depending less on FOXO4 to keep p53 in check. A 2021 study from a Pittsburgh group is the most careful public test of that in human cells, and it is worth reading because it both supports and limits the claim.","Human chondrocytes from eight donors were expanded in culture until over 40% were senescent by beta-galactosidase staining, against under 17% in minimally expanded controls. Treatment at 25 micromolar for five days removed more than half the heavily expanded cells while leaving the minimally expanded population essentially unchanged, with caspase staining confirming apoptosis. Senescent cells fell to under 5%, and p16, p21 and p53 protein dropped [3] (verified, human cells).","Then the part that is rarely quoted. The surviving cells did not regain their ability to make cartilage: in three-dimensional pellet culture, pretreatment did not increase matrix deposition or cartilage gene expression, and it reduced SOX9 and COL2, two central cartilage genes. p21 protein rose in pretreated groups, which the authors read as possible stress on the remaining cells. They concluded that removing senescent cells was not enough to restore function and that other strategies would be needed [3] (verified).","That is the most informative result in the whole literature for anyone thinking about repair: clearance worked, and clearance alone did not restore the tissue's job."]},{"h":"What else has been published since","where":"animals","body":["The peptide has been used as a research tool across a scattered set of animal and cell models: senescence in kidney transplantation, Leydig cells and testosterone in aged mice, radiosensitisation and radiation-induced lung fibrosis, bleomycin lung fibrosis, spermatogenesis in aged mice, keloid fibroblasts, and the p53 transactivation domain as a target [2] (verified that these studies exist; their results are not summarised here because the primary texts were not opened).","A 2025 study reported that injections suppressed aortic ageing and improved aortic function in naturally aged and progeroid mice, and reduced senescence in endothelial cells deprived of oxygen and glucose, with the proposed chain running from blocked FOXO4 binding to nuclear exclusion of phosphorylated p53 to BAX and caspase-3 activation [4] (reported; dose and route were not stated in the accessible summary).","What is missing from this list is a large independent replication of the 2017 ageing result by a group with no connection to the original, and any human study of any kind."]},{"h":"Safety: what is known, and the structural reason to be careful","where":"animals","body":["The 2017 paper described the peptide as well tolerated in vivo under the conditions tested [1] (verified as stated, with those conditions unspecified in the abstract). That is the extent of the published safety record. No repeat-dose toxicology, genotoxicity, reproductive or carcinogenicity studies have been published, and no pharmacokinetic study in any species was found for this review.","The mechanism itself sets the main caution, and it is worth being explicit rather than vague. The peptide works by freeing p53 to kill a cell. p53 is the body's main brake on cancer and also a central damage-response switch in healthy tissue. The selectivity argument is that healthy cells do not need FOXO4 to restrain p53, which the chondrocyte work supports in one cell type at one concentration [3]. Whether that holds across tissues, over repeated courses, and in people with existing damage has not been tested (inferred).","The chondrocyte study's incidental finding points the same way: the cells that survived treatment showed raised p21, a stress marker [3] (verified, cells). Not a harm, but not nothing either.","There is no established human dose. Figures circulating in self-experimentation circles are extrapolations from a mouse dose, and nothing in the published record supports any of them."]},{"h":"Who is developing it, and why that has stalled","where":"lab","body":["A company was formed to commercialise the Dutch group's work and is still described as a preclinical operation, with its focus reported as the FOXO4 to p53 interaction more broadly rather than this particular peptide, and candidate indications under selection across chronic diseases, a rare disease and resistant tumours [4,5] (reported).","The commentary around it makes an economic point that explains the plateau better than any scientific one: a peptide whose sequence is public and hard to protect struggles to attract the money a proper trial costs, so it stays a tool compound while a patentable successor is sought [4] (reported). Nine years after the original paper, that is still where it sits."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Not approved anywhere, no investigational new drug programme announced, no registered clinical trial found. Material sold under this name is a research chemical.","It is not named on the WADA Prohibited List; as a substance with no approval for human therapeutic use it falls under the S0 class, prohibited at all times (inferred from the class definition)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does clearance help, if the cells left behind do not work better? The chondrocyte result says removal and restoration are different problems.","Does selectivity hold outside a dish and outside one cell type? This is the question the entire safety case depends on, and it has one careful test behind it.","What happens with repeated courses? No repeat-dose study in any species has been published, in a mechanism that deliberately promotes cell death.","What are the pharmacokinetics? A D-amino acid retro-inverso peptide should be unusually long-lived, which cuts both ways, and nobody has published the numbers.","Why has nobody replicated the 2017 ageing result independently? Nine years is a long time for a widely cited finding to stand on one paper."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Baar et al., Cell 2017, targeted apoptosis of senescent cells restores tissue homeostasis in response to chemotoxicity and aging","url":"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4j8893w1","year":2017},{"n":2,"t":"Wikipedia, FOXO4-DRI (sequence, chemistry, index of subsequent studies)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FOXO4-DRI","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Huang et al., Front Bioeng Biotechnol 2021, FOXO4-DRI selectively removes senescent cells from expanded human chondrocytes","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fbioe.2021.677576/pdf","year":2021},{"n":4,"t":"Fight Aging, people are still working on the senolytic peptide FOXO4-DRI (reporting the 2025 aortic ageing study and company status)","url":"https://www.fightaging.org/archives/2026/02/people-are-still-working-on-the-senolytic-peptide-foxo4-dri/","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"Lifespan.io rejuvenation roadmap entry, Cleara Biotech and FOXO4-DRI","url":"https://www.lifespan.io/road-maps/the-rejuvenation-roadmap/cleara-biotech-foxo4-dri","year":2026}]},"nadplus":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Not a peptide, not a drug, and not a thing the body lets in","where":"mixed","body":["NAD+ is a coenzyme every cell already makes and constantly recycles. That makes it unlike everything else in this library: there is no question of whether it is biologically important, and no question of whether a cell can use it. The whole question is whether putting it into the bloodstream, as a large charged dinucleotide, raises anything inside the cells that need it.","The honest framing is that NAD+ is not a deficiency state waiting to be corrected like iron. It is a flux: made from precursors, consumed by PARP enzymes repairing DNA, by sirtuins, and by CD38 on cell surfaces, which rises with age and inflammation (verified as the accepted biochemistry). Measuring a level tells you very little about the rate, and all the human product claims are about levels.","One structural point explains most of what follows. The molecule carries two phosphates and is large by cellular standards. There is no established plasma membrane transporter that brings intact NAD+ into cells in people, which is why the field works with precursors, nicotinamide riboside and nicotinamide mononucleotide, that have known entry routes (verified as the state of the field)."]},{"h":"The one human pharmacokinetic study, and what it found","where":"people","body":["There is a single published human study tracking NAD+ in blood and urine during administration, and it is small: 11 healthy men aged 30 to 55, eight given 750 mg of NAD+ intravenously in saline over six hours at roughly 2 mg per minute, three given saline alone [1] (verified).","The central finding is the one most often skipped. Plasma NAD+ did not rise at all for the first hours; it was described as rapidly and completely removed from circulation for at least the first two hours, and only at the six-hour mark, the end of the infusion, was it about 398% above baseline [1] (verified). In other words, a slow infusion is almost entirely cleared as fast as it goes in, until the clearance routes saturate.","Where it went is partly visible in the metabolites: nicotinamide rose 409%, ADP-ribose 393% and methylnicotinamide 350% at six hours, with nicotinamide mononucleotide rising 472% only at eight hours [1]. Urinary NAD+ excretion rose 538% at six hours, while urinary nicotinamide did not change significantly, so intact NAD+ was appearing in urine [1] (verified). The authors attribute the disappearance to tissue uptake and to breakdown by the surface enzymes CD38, CD157 and CD203a, and state plainly that tissue uptake was not measured [1] (inferred by the authors, not demonstrated).","So the only human kinetic data show rapid destruction and urinary loss of a large fraction, with the rise in blood appearing only once the system is overwhelmed. Nothing in it shows that intracellular NAD+ in any tissue increased. The dose was chosen empirically from clinic practice, and the study was funded by a company running NAD+ infusion clinics, with one author a director of it and another receiving consulting fees [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"What raising blood NAD+ has and has not achieved in trials","where":"people","body":["The precursors have been tested properly, which makes them the best available guide to whether the premise works. Nicotinamide riboside reliably raises measured NAD+ in blood or muscle, and the functional results have been consistently null.","Twenty-four adults aged 55 to 79 took 1,000 mg daily for six weeks with no significant group difference in vascular function after correction; 40 men took 2,000 mg daily for 12 weeks with no difference from placebo in metabolic outcomes, and muscle sampling from the same cohort found no mitochondrial change; 12 older men took 1,000 mg daily for 21 days with no change in how muscle mitochondria used oxygen; 30 adults took 2,000 mg daily for 12 weeks with no significant difference in walk distance or quality of life [2] (verified as reported, with trial sizes of 12 to 40 and durations of three weeks to three months).","That pattern, the biomarker moves and the outcome does not, is the most important fact in this entry. It is also the pattern that an injected or infused form inherits, since raising the biomarker is precisely what it is sold to do (inferred).","A separate and more basic gap: there is no published human study of subcutaneous or intramuscular NAD+ at all. Every human number above comes from intravenous infusion or oral precursors (verified as absent). The commonly quoted half-life figures for injected NAD+ have no human source found in this review."]},{"h":"Tolerability, measured honestly for once","where":"people","body":["The infusion experience is usually described as flushing or discomfort that passes. A 2026 retrospective review from a commercial clinic puts numbers on it, and they are not mild. Fourteen clients received 500 mg daily for four consecutive days in 500 mL of saline, six as NAD+ and eight as nicotinamide riboside [3] (verified).","All six NAD+ clients, six of six, reported moderate to severe abdominal cramping, diarrhoea, nausea, vomiting, raised heart rate, throat pain, congestion and chest pressure during the infusion, resolving when it stopped. Five of eight NR clients reported minor tingling in the tongue, jaw and arm with minor cramping [3] (verified).","The mechanism of the dose schedule is visible in the timing. Clients controlled their own flow rate by tolerance, and the NAD+ group slowed theirs so much that average infusion time was about 97 minutes against 37 for NR [3] (verified). The long slow drip is not a pharmacological choice; it is what the symptoms force.","The limits of that study are substantial and the authors state them: retrospective, 14 people, no control group, four days only, whole-blood NAD+ assays unable to detect changes, and all seven authors employed by the clinic that funded it, with the NR donated by its manufacturer [3] (verified). It is cited here not as efficacy evidence but because the symptom list is concrete and comes from the setting where this is actually used."]},{"h":"Product reality","where":"lab","body":["NAD+ is chemically unstable in ways that matter for a product. It hydrolyses in solution, is sensitive to pH and heat, and the oxidised and reduced forms are different molecules with different stability, which is why the plus sign in the name is chemistry rather than branding.","Because it is sold variously as a supplement ingredient, a compounded injectable and a research powder, there is no single quality framework behind a given vial. A compounded sterile injection from a licensed pharmacy carries beyond-use dating, sterility and endotoxin requirements; a research powder carries none of those, and the molecule's instability means that content at manufacture and content at use are different questions (inferred).","One practical consequence of the infusion data: because clearance saturates, the rate of administration changes exposure more than the nominal amount does [1]. Two vials of the same size given at different rates are not the same exposure, which makes nominal figures a poor description of what someone received (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["This is a muddle rather than a position. NAD+ itself is sold as a supplement ingredient and as a compounded injectable in the US, and is not an approved drug for any anti-ageing or energy indication (verified). It has not been through the peptide compounding review process that covers the injectable peptides elsewhere in this library, because it is not a peptide.","The precursor side shows how fast the ground can move. In 2022 the FDA concluded that beta-nicotinamide mononucleotide cannot be marketed as a dietary supplement, on the basis that it had already been authorised for investigation as a new drug with substantial public clinical investigation, reversing its own earlier acceptance of another filing; enforcement has not followed, and the agency does not disclose the relevant authorisation date [4] (verified). Nicotinamide riboside was not affected by that decision [4].","In sport, NAD+ is not named on the 2026 Prohibited List. The constraint that does apply is the method prohibition on intravenous infusions totalling more than 100 mL per 12 hours outside hospital treatment, which a 500 mL drip plainly exceeds [5] (verified). Note also that the related mitochondrial peptide MOTS-c is named on the list as an AMPK activator, so metabolic agents in this neighbourhood are not uniformly outside the rules [5].","Does intracellular NAD+ in any human tissue rise after infusion? The one kinetic study measured plasma and urine and inferred tissue uptake without measuring it [1].","Why is the biomarker-to-outcome link so consistently empty? Every well-conducted precursor trial raised NAD+ and changed nothing measurable, which is either a dose, duration, population or premise problem, and nobody has distinguished which.","Is injecting the intact coenzyme better than giving a precursor the body has transporters for? No study has compared them on any outcome other than infusion tolerability, where the precursor was clearly easier [3].","What is the cost of flooding a saturable clearance system repeatedly? Methylnicotinamide rose 350% in six hours [1], which consumes methyl groups, and nothing has examined what weekly or more frequent loading does to methyl balance (speculated)."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Grant et al., Frontiers in Aging Neuroscience, plasma and urine NAD+ metabolome during a 6-hour intravenous NAD+ infusion","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/aging-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnagi.2019.00257/pdf","year":2019},{"n":2,"t":"Compilation of nicotinamide riboside human trials (Martens 2018, Dollerup 2018 and 2020, Elhassan 2019, Wang 2022) with endpoints and sample sizes","url":"https://verifiedsupplementdata.com/nr/for-nad-elevation/","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Reyna et al., Frontiers in Aging, intravenous NAD+ versus nicotinamide riboside, retrospective tolerability pilot","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12907335/","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Lifespan.io, FDA concludes beta-NMN is excluded from the dietary supplement definition under drug preclusion","url":"https://lifespan.io/news/fda-purges-nmn-from-the-list-of-supplements/","year":2022},{"n":5,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List, including M2.2 intravenous infusion limit","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"retatrutide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What the pivotal trial actually measured","where":"people","body":["TRIUMPH-1 randomised 2,339 adults with obesity, or overweight plus at least one weight-related condition, and without diabetes, one-to-one-to-one-to-one across three doses and placebo, dosed weekly for 80 weeks [1] (verified). A pre-specified extension carried 532 participants with a starting body mass index of 35 or above out to 104 weeks.","The primary measure was percent body weight change at 80 weeks. Results ran 19.0% at the low dose, 25.9% at the middle dose and 28.3% at the top dose, against 2.2% on placebo [1]. In the extension, the top dose reached 30.3% at 104 weeks, and participants switched from placebo onto drug reached 19.2% [1] (verified).","Two secondary measures are more informative than the headline. Waist circumference fell 16.3, 21.8 and 24.1 centimetres across the three doses against 3.6 on placebo, which matters because waist tracks visceral fat more closely than total weight does [1]. And the proportion losing at least 30% of starting body weight was 15.3%, 37.9% and 45.3% against 0.5% on placebo [1] (verified). That last figure is the one that moved clinical conversation, because losing 30% of body weight was previously the territory of surgery.","What the trial did not measure: it was not a cardiovascular outcome trial, it excluded diabetes, and at 80 weeks weight was still falling, so the plateau has not been located."]},{"h":"The dose-response is the real finding, in both directions","where":"people","body":["A clean dose-response is what separates a drug effect from noise, and this one is clean. Weight loss, waist reduction and the proportion hitting large thresholds all rose in order across the three doses [1].","Tolerability moved in the same order. Discontinuation for adverse events ran 4.1%, 6.9% and 11.3% across the three doses against 4.9% on placebo [1] (verified). So at the top dose, roughly one in nine participants stopped because of side effects, and the placebo rate tells you some of that would have happened anyway.","The gastrointestinal pattern scaled too: nausea 28.6%, 38.4% and 42.4% against 14.8% on placebo; vomiting 10.6%, 22.8% and 25.3% against 4.8% [1] (verified).","Read together, those two gradients are the practical substance of the trial. The top dose bought roughly 9 percentage points more weight loss than the low dose and roughly tripled the chance of stopping. That is a genuine trade-off rather than a free gain, and it is the kind of detail that disappears when a compound is described by its best number alone."]},{"h":"One signal that is not in the class playbook","where":"people","body":["Dysesthesia, meaning altered or unpleasant skin sensation, appeared in 5.1%, 12.3% and 12.5% of the three dose groups against 0.9% on placebo [1] (verified). That is a roughly thirteen-fold difference from placebo at the higher doses, with a dose relationship.","This is worth flagging because it is not a familiar feature of the established incretin drugs, so it is plausibly tied to the third receptor arm rather than to the shared mechanism (inferred). The trial sponsor described these events as generally mild to moderate, with most resolving during treatment [1] (reported).","Heart rate is the other arm-specific question. Glucagon receptor activation raises energy expenditure, and it also raises resting heart rate; Phase 2 work reported an increase of roughly 3 to 5 beats per minute [2] (reported). The pivotal release did not report heart rate figures [1], which is a gap rather than a reassurance.","Neither point argues against the compound. Both argue that a third receptor means a third side-effect profile, and that the class-wide intuitions people carry over from older drugs will be incomplete here."]},{"h":"The other three trials, and what a hazard ratio of 0.82 does and does not show","where":"people","body":["All four Phase 3 trials had reported topline results by October 2026 [2] (reported).","In obesity with type 2 diabetes, results reported in July 2026 showed up to 20.8% weight loss with HbA1c falling by as much as 1.6 percentage points [2]. Weight loss is reliably smaller in diabetes across this whole drug class, so the gap from the non-diabetes trial is expected rather than disappointing.","In obesity with knee osteoarthritis, results reported in December 2025 showed 28.7% weight loss at 68 weeks alongside significant improvement in knee pain [2] (reported).","The cardiovascular trial, in people with a body mass index of 35 or above and established cardiovascular disease, reported up to 22.6% weight loss and a pre-specified composite cardiovascular hazard ratio of 0.82 against placebo [2] (reported). That number deserves careful handling. A hazard ratio of 0.82 points in the right direction, but a pre-specified analysis inside a weight-loss trial is not the same as a dedicated, adequately powered cardiovascular outcome trial, and nothing in the topline establishes that it crossed a significance threshold with a trial designed to test it. Treat it as encouraging and not as established (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where approval stands, and why that matters here more than usual","where":"people","body":["As of October 2026 the compound was not approved anywhere, and no marketing application had been submitted. The developer has indicated an intent to file in the first quarter of 2027, after completing manufacturing data, with any decision following well after that [2] (reported).","That timeline creates a situation worth naming plainly. The efficacy evidence here is unusually strong for an unapproved compound: four Phase 3 trials, thousands of participants, a clean dose-response, publication in a major journal [1]. The manufacturing and quality evidence for anything circulating outside that programme is entirely separate from it, and none of the trial data speaks to what is in an unapproved vial.","For every other compound in this catalog, weak trial evidence and uncertain product quality travel together. Here they come apart: the molecule has been tested properly, and the supply has not. Those are two different questions and conflating them in either direction produces a wrong conclusion (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Where does weight loss plateau? It was still falling at 80 weeks and reached 30.3% by 104 weeks in the extension, so the ceiling has not been observed.","What happens on stopping? No published data address regain after this compound specifically, and the class pattern suggests it should be expected.","Is the dysesthesia signal durable, dose-limiting or mechanistic? It is dose-related and roughly thirteen-fold over placebo, and its cause has not been established.","What does the glucagon arm do to the heart over years? Resting heart rate rose in Phase 2 and the pivotal release did not report it. A long cardiovascular trial is the only way to settle this.","Does the cardiovascular signal hold up in a trial built to test it? A pre-specified composite inside a weight trial is a reason to run that trial, not a substitute for it.","How much muscle comes off at 28 to 30% total weight loss? Body composition is the obvious question at this magnitude of loss and is not in the topline reporting."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Eli Lilly, TRIUMPH-1 Phase 3 topline results in obesity","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lillys-triple-agonist-retatrutide-delivered-powerful-weight-loss","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Compound Review, retatrutide Phase 3 programme tracker, all four TRIUMPH trials","url":"https://compoundreview.com/guides/retatrutide-phase-3","year":2026}]},"cagrilintide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What the pivotal trial measured, and what it did not include","where":"people","body":["REDEFINE 1 randomised 3,417 adults with obesity, or overweight plus at least one related condition, and without type 2 diabetes, to the combination product or placebo, dosed weekly for 68 weeks under double-blind conditions [1] (verified).","Weight reduction at 68 weeks was 22.7% against 2.3% on placebo under the estimand that assumes adherence, and 20.4% against 3.0% under the estimand that counts everyone regardless of adherence [1]. That gap between the two figures, roughly 2 percentage points, is the cost of real-world adherence and is usually the more honest number to carry forward.","The detail that matters for reading this compound on its own: the published pivotal release reports the combination against placebo and does not report separate single-agent arms for the amylin analogue alone or the established incretin alone [1] (verified). So the trial establishes what the pair does. It does not quantify, in that trial, how much the amylin half contributed.","That is the central evidence problem for this entry. This compound is nearly always studied and used as half of a pair, and the pivotal evidence is for the pair. Its solo Phase 2 work exists but is far smaller, and no large trial has isolated its independent contribution at the doses used in combination (verified as absent).","Tolerability: gastrointestinal events in 79.6% against 39.9% on placebo, with nausea 55% against 12.6%, constipation 30.7% against 11.6% and vomiting 26.1% against 4.1%. Discontinuation for adverse events was 6% against 3.7% [1] (verified). A nausea rate above half is high for this class."]},{"h":"The head-to-head that did not go as expected","where":"people","body":["REDEFINE 4 compared the combination directly against the leading dual-receptor drug: 809 adults with obesity and at least one related condition, no diabetes, 84 weeks, open-label [2] (reported).","Weight loss came in at 20.2% against 23.6% under the treatment-regimen estimand, and 23.0% against 25.5% under the efficacy estimand. Non-inferiority was not demonstrated [2] (reported). Since non-inferiority failed, no superiority test for the comparator was formally reported, so the correct statement is that the combination did not prove itself as good as the comparator, rather than that the comparator was proven better.","The timing is worth noting for anyone tracking the regulatory story. The marketing application was filed in December 2025, before these head-to-head results were released in February 2026, and the filing rests on the placebo-controlled trials rather than on the comparison [2] (reported).","This is a useful corrective to a common assumption. Adding a second mechanism does not automatically beat a well-optimised dual-receptor molecule. Two targets chosen well can outperform two targets combined, and the arithmetic of mechanisms is not additive in practice (inferred)."]},{"h":"Why the amylin pathway is a reasonable bet anyway","where":"mixed","body":["The pharmacological case for this target does not rest on the head-to-head result, and it is worth separating the two.","Amylin signalling reaches satiety circuits in the hindbrain that are partly distinct from the incretin circuits, which is the mechanistic basis for expecting additive effects rather than overlapping ones. In the placebo-controlled trial, the pair did produce more weight loss than the established incretin alone achieves in its own trials, which is consistent with genuine addition even though cross-trial comparison is weak evidence (inferred).","There is also a precedent with a cautionary edge. The first amylin-based drug reached approval years earlier for use alongside insulin, and it carries a boxed warning for severe hypoglycaemia in that setting and is contraindicated in gastroparesis (verified, as reflected in the card's cautions). That history is the best available guide to what this receptor family does when pushed, and it points at the stomach and at glucose control rather than at anything exotic.","The practical consequence of the mechanism is that gastric emptying slows, and the pivotal trial's nausea and vomiting rates are consistent with that. For a compound whose action is partly to make meals end sooner, gastrointestinal effects are not a side issue; they are the mechanism being felt (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where approval stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["The combination product's marketing application was filed in December 2025 and, as of late October 2026, had not been approved. The developer guided toward a decision in the final quarter of 2026, with no public decision date identified [2] (reported).","The single agent is not separately filed or approved anywhere. Anyone encountering it outside a trial is encountering an unapproved compound whose pivotal evidence belongs to a two-drug product it was studied inside (verified).","That has a specific consequence for interpretation. Reported effects from combination use cannot be attributed to this compound, and the trial that would settle its independent contribution at combination doses has not been run (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What does this compound do on its own at the doses used in the combination? The pivotal programme did not isolate it, which leaves its independent effect size genuinely unknown.","Why did the head-to-head fail non-inferiority when the placebo-controlled result looked so strong? Population, open-label design, titration and dose optimisation are all candidates, and none has been established.","Does the amylin pathway add anything that matters beyond weight, such as a different pattern of regain or a different effect on muscle? Untested.","How does a nausea rate above half interact with long-term adherence? The two estimands in the pivotal trial already show adherence costing about 2 percentage points of effect over 68 weeks.","Does the insulin interaction seen with the earlier amylin drug carry over? The mechanism suggests caution with glucose-lowering treatment, and no trial has characterised it for this molecule.","If approved, does the combination earn a place against an already-superior single molecule? That is now a commercial and clinical question rather than a pharmacological one."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Novo Nordisk, REDEFINE 1 results published in NEJM (Garvey et al.)","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/cagrisema-2-4-mg--2-4-mg-demonstrated-22-7-mean-weight-reduction-in-adults-with-overweight-or-obesity-in-redefine-1--published-in-nejm-302487770.html","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"Remy Research, REDEFINE 4 head-to-head results and filing status","url":"https://remyresearch.com/research/cagrisema-vs-tirzepatide","year":2026}]},"mk-677":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from: a Merck drug that never found its indication","where":"people","body":["Merck designed it in the mid-1990s as L-163,191, later MK-0677, with a tentative brand name of Oratrope [1] (verified). The goal was a pill that would do what growth hormone injections do for frail older people, by persuading the pituitary to release more of its own hormone rather than replacing it. That framing explains the shape of the human record: the trials were in older adults, hip fracture patients, people with Alzheimer's disease and children with short stature, not in healthy young adults.","Merck ran it through several indications and stopped each one, for reasons covered below. The molecule then passed through licensees and is now back in development under a new code, LUM-201, with the original company still listed as originator [2] (verified). So the compound sold as a gym product and the one in a 2026 phase 3 for children are the same molecule with very different paperwork behind them."]},{"h":"Mechanism: why it raises growth hormone all day rather than in a pulse","where":"people","body":["It binds the ghrelin receptor, the same receptor the hunger hormone uses, which is why appetite and growth hormone release travel together on this drug. Natural ghrelin is a peptide with a short life in blood. This is a small, orally absorbed molecule with a much longer one, so instead of brief spikes the receptor is pushed for most of the day [1] (verified).","The useful detail is the gap between drug half-life and effect. In dogs the molecule itself has an elimination half-life of about four to six hours, yet in humans IGF-1 stays elevated for up to 24 hours after a single dose [1] (verified, animal half-life; human effect duration). Human plasma half-life figures were not found in an accessible primary source for this review. What matters for interpreting the trials is that daily dosing produces a sustained IGF-1 rise, and IGF-1, not growth hormone, is what most of the downstream effects and side effects track.","Because it acts on the body's own pituitary, the growth hormone it produces still comes in pulses, just larger and more frequent ones; the 1990s work described amplitude rising rather than a flat plateau (inferred from the studies of the class; the Nass trial reported pulsatile secretion restored to young-adult levels [3])."]},{"h":"The human record: what each trial measured and found","where":"people","body":["Healthy older adults, the best trial. Sixty-five healthy adults aged 60 to 81 took 25 mg or placebo daily for a year. Growth hormone and IGF-1 rose to young-adult levels. Fat-free mass rose 1.1 kg on drug and fell 0.5 kg on placebo, a 1.6 kg difference [3,4] (verified). Strength and physical function did not change [4] (reported). Fasting glucose rose by about 0.3 mmol per litre and insulin sensitivity fell [3,4] (verified). This is the source of the claim that it builds lean mass, and the same paper is the source of the claim that the lean mass did not translate into function.","Hip fracture recovery. A Merck phase 2b randomised 123 elderly hip fracture patients to 25 mg or placebo for 24 weeks. IGF-1 rose by about 51 ng per mL over placebo. Gait speed improved slightly, stair-climbing power and other functional measures did not significantly change, and falls trended lower without reaching significance. The trial was stopped early for a safety signal of congestive heart failure in a small number of patients, and the authors called the safety profile unfavourable in that population [5] (verified).","Alzheimer's disease. A Merck-funded year-long trial in people with mild to moderate disease raised IGF-1 as expected and did nothing to slow progression [6,7] (verified). Coverage at the time put enrolment at 416 [7] (reported). It is the cleanest demonstration in the record that raising IGF-1 is not the same as producing a clinical benefit.","Sleep. One small study: eight young men on 5 mg, 25 mg and placebo for a week each, and six older adults on 2 mg then 25 mg for two weeks each. In the young group, stage IV sleep lasted about 50% longer and REM rose over 20% at the high dose; in the older group REM rose nearly 50% and REM latency fell [8] (verified). The older arm had no placebo period, and fourteen people in total is a thin base for the sleep reputation the compound has.","Children with growth hormone deficiency, the live programme. Phase 2 studies reportedly met their endpoints, one enrolling 104 children, and a 12-month placebo-controlled phase 3 in treatment-naive prepubertal children is recruiting, with a long-term extension planned [2] (reported)."]},{"h":"Safety: what the trials actually showed","where":"people","body":["Glucose is the most consistent signal. Every longer trial in the record moved fasting glucose up and insulin sensitivity down [3,4] (verified). The mechanism is ordinary growth hormone physiology, which opposes insulin action, so this is expected rather than idiosyncratic, and it is dose- and duration-dependent rather than a rare event (inferred).","The heart failure signal is the one most often left out. It appeared in frail elderly patients recovering from surgery, a population prone to fluid overload, and the published abstract does not give the case count by arm [5]. It cannot be generalised to young healthy users, and it cannot be dismissed either: fluid retention is a known effect of raising growth hormone, and the drug did enough of it in that group to stop a trial (verified that the trial stopped; inferred on mechanism).","Appetite rises early and, in the older adult trial, settled in most people within about three months [3] (reported). Cortisol rises acutely with dosing [1] (reported).","What has not been studied: no trial has looked at healthy adults under 60 for more than weeks, none has examined cancer incidence despite the sustained IGF-1 rise, and none has followed users beyond about two years."]},{"h":"What is actually in the bottle","where":"lab","body":["It is a small molecule, not a peptide, so the identity and stability problems that dominate peptide products are less severe here: it is a stable salt (the mesylate) that survives being made into capsules or liquids (inferred from chemistry). The quality problem is different. Outside the LUM-201 programme there is no pharmaceutical-grade supply, so every grey-market product is a research chemical with whatever purity and dose accuracy its maker provides. No published independent survey of retail MK-677 content was found for this review."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Not approved anywhere. In the US it is an investigational drug, in phase 3 as LUM-201 for paediatric growth hormone deficiency, with orphan designations in the US and EU [1,2] (verified).","In sport it is named in the WADA Prohibited List under S2 as a growth hormone secretagogue, prohibited at all times [9] (verified). Urinary metabolites are the detection target in anti-doping work."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does lean mass gained this way ever become strength or function? The best trial says no at one year in older adults; nobody has tested younger people properly.","What is the glucose cost over years? Every trial moved glucose the wrong way, and none ran long enough to show whether that becomes diabetes in susceptible people.","How many heart failure cases were there, and in which arm? The answer sits in the full hip fracture paper and the trial's internal data.","Will a paediatric approval change access? If LUM-201 is approved, the molecule gets a label, a reference standard and a pharmacovigilance record for the first time, which would change how the rest of the evidence can be read."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Wikipedia, Ibutamoren (pharmacology, development codes, legal status, primary citations)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibutamoren","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Synapse drug record, ibutamoren mesylate / LUM-201 development status and trials","url":"https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/f0832b8b7f544f8a94fd41577786b6b2","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Endocrine Abstracts 2009 (summarising Nass et al., Ann Intern Med 2008), oral ghrelin mimetic in older adults","url":"https://www.endocrine-abstracts.org/ea/0019/ea0019S2","year":2009},{"n":4,"t":"Nass et al., Ann Intern Med 2008, effects of an oral ghrelin mimetic on body composition in healthy older adults (abstract as quoted)","url":"https://www.nextbigfuture.com/2008/11/drug-increases-muscle-in-elderly.html","year":2008},{"n":5,"t":"Adunsky et al., Arch Gerontol Geriatr 2011, MK-0677 in patients recovering from hip fracture, phase IIb","url":"https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/mk-0677-ibutamoren-mesylate-for-the-treatment-of-patients-recover/","year":2011},{"n":6,"t":"Sevigny et al., Neurology 2008, MK-677: no clinical effect on Alzheimer's progression","url":"https://alzforum.org/papers/growth-hormone-secretagogue-mk-677-no-clinical-effect-ad-progression-randomized-trial","year":2008},{"n":7,"t":"ScienceDaily (AAN release), growth hormone drug does not slow Alzheimer's","url":"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2008/11/081117192902.htm","year":2008},{"n":8,"t":"Copinschi et al., Neuroendocrinology 1997, prolonged oral MK-677 improves sleep quality","url":"https://karger.com/nen/article-abstract/66/4/278/224995/Prolonged-Oral-Treatment-with-MK-677-a-Novel","year":1997},{"n":9,"t":"German Sport University Cologne, WADA S2 class listing (growth hormone secretagogues incl. ibutamoren)","url":"https://www.dshs-koeln.de/en/universitaet/organisation/institute-einrichtungen/department-of-biochemistry/doping-substances/prohibited-classes-of-substances-and-methods/s2-peptide-hormones-growth-factors-etc","year":2026}]},"slu-pp-332":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from: a tool compound from one academic network","where":"lab","body":["It was designed by medicinal chemists working with Thomas Burris's group, across Saint Louis University, Washington University in St. Louis and later the University of Florida, as a probe for the estrogen-related receptors [1,2]. Those receptors had been studied almost entirely by switching them off genetically; a usable agonist let the group switch them on in a living animal (verified that this was the stated aim).","The published record is three papers from that network and its collaborators: an exercise paper in 2023, a metabolic syndrome paper in 2024, and a heart failure paper with a Baylor cardiology group in 2024 [1,2,3] (verified). No independent laboratory has published a replication, and no clinical programme has been announced. The group's own stated next steps were to refine the structure, ideally into something orally usable, and to do more animal safety work before any human trial [4] (reported)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, in the numbers the papers report","where":"cells","body":["Potency depends on the assay. In a chimeric receptor assay the half-maximal concentrations were about 8 nanomolar for ERR alpha, 280 for beta and 770 for gamma; with the full-length receptors they were about 98, 230 and 430 [1] (verified). That is roughly four-fold preference for alpha in the more realistic assay, closer to a pan-agonist than the alpha label suggests.","The subtypes appear to carry different jobs. The endurance effect required muscle ERR alpha: in mice lacking it in muscle, treatment no longer improved running, and several target genes stopped responding [1] (verified, animals). In the heart, the protective effect ran mainly through ERR gamma [3] (verified, animals). One molecule is therefore doing two partly separate things in two organs.","The heart work also found that, alongside switching on fat-burning and mitochondrial genes, the agonists turned down cell-cycle and developmental gene programmes in heart muscle cells, partly through a regulator called E2F1 [3] (verified, animals and cells). In a failing heart that was presented as part of the benefit. What the same suppression does in tissues that divide normally has not been examined."]},{"h":"What the animal studies measured","where":"animals","body":["Endurance. Mice given the compound by intraperitoneal injection ran about 70% longer and about 45% further before exhaustion than vehicle-treated mice. Muscle showed stronger succinate dehydrogenase staining, more oxidative type IIa fibres, more mitochondria on electron microscopy, higher mitochondrial DNA relative to nuclear DNA, and more Complex I, Complex V and cytochrome c protein [1] (verified, animals). Myofibres were smaller in diameter, which is the opposite of a muscle-building drug and consistent with an endurance phenotype.","Obesity. In diet-induced obese mice, twice-daily treatment for a month produced about 12% weight loss, with roughly ten-fold less fat gained than untreated mice, improved insulin sensitivity and higher energy expenditure. Food intake did not change, and the mice did not move more; the investigator's summary was that they used more energy at rest [2,4] (reported for the numbers, which come from the university's release rather than the paper's abstract).","Heart failure. In a mouse pressure-overload model, this compound and a sibling improved ejection fraction, reduced fibrosis and improved survival without changing the degree of cardiac enlargement [3] (verified, animals). This is the strongest outcome in the whole record, and it is in a disease model rather than in healthy animals."]},{"h":"What the body does with it, and why route matters","where":"animals","body":["Exposure was measured in mice after a single 30 mg per kg intraperitoneal dose: about 0.8 micromolar in muscle and about 0.1 micromolar in plasma at two hours [1] (verified, animals). No plasma half-life, no oral bioavailability figure and no brain exposure were reported in the accessible version of that work, although brain was listed among tissues collected.","Every animal result above was produced by injection into the abdominal cavity, twice a day [1,2]. That is a laboratory route, not a human one, and the group's stated goal of making an orally usable version implies this molecule is not it (inferred from their own description [4]). Community oral and subcutaneous use is therefore extrapolating across both species and route from a compound whose oral behaviour has not been published.","No human pharmacokinetic data exist, and no established human dose exists."]},{"h":"Safety: what has been looked at","where":"animals","body":["The only safety assessment in the record is short. At 50 mg per kg twice daily for ten days in mice, the authors reported no overt toxicity, with normal blood counts and electrolytes and no significant change in creatine kinase [1] (verified, animals, ten days).","What that leaves untouched: no repeat-dose toxicology beyond those ten days, no genotoxicity, no reproductive or developmental studies, no carcinogenicity work, and no cardiac safety study in healthy animals. The heart results come from animals with induced heart failure, where raising oxidative metabolism helps; nothing tells you what sustained ERR activation does to a healthy heart (inferred).","One mechanistic question deserves naming rather than burying. The compound drives mitochondrial biogenesis and fat oxidation in several tissues at once, and in heart cells it suppressed cell-cycle programmes [1,3]. Both of those are broad transcriptional changes rather than narrow effects, and no study has looked for off-target consequences in a healthy animal over months."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["A research chemical with no approval anywhere, no investigational new drug programme announced, and no clinical trial registered for this molecule. Material sold for human use is unapproved by definition.","In sport it falls under WADA's S0 class, substances with no current approval for human therapeutic use, prohibited at all times (inferred from the class definition; the compound is not named individually on the list). Metabolic modulators are also covered as a class under S4, and anti-doping laboratories have added ERR agonists to screening interest as exercise mimetics (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does anything here happen in a person? Three animal papers from one network, no human data, no replication.","Does it work by mouth? The group's own plan to engineer an oral version suggests the published molecule is not well suited to it, and no oral exposure data have been published.","What does sustained ERR activation do to a healthy heart, given that the cardiac benefit was shown in failing hearts and came with suppression of cell-cycle genes?","Why do the mice burn more energy without moving more, and is that sustainable? The obesity paper reports the result; the thermogenic mechanism in intact animals is not pinned down.","Nothing beyond ten days has been examined in any species. That is the single largest gap, given that community use runs in multi-week cycles."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Billon et al., ACS Chem Biol 2023, synthetic ERR agonist induces an acute aerobic exercise response and enhances exercise capacity (preprint full text)","url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2022.10.05.510974.full.pdf","year":2023},{"n":2,"t":"Billon et al., J Pharmacol Exp Ther 2024, a synthetic ERR agonist alleviates metabolic syndrome","url":"https://profiles.wustl.edu/en/publications/a-synthetic-err-agonist-alleviates-metabolic-syndrome/","year":2024},{"n":3,"t":"Xu et al., Circulation 2024, novel pan-ERR agonists ameliorate heart failure through cardiac fatty acid metabolism and mitochondrial function","url":"https://collections.uhsp.edu/pharm-admin-sciences_pubs/45","year":2024},{"n":4,"t":"University of Florida news release, exercise-mimicking drug sheds weight and boosts muscle activity in mice","url":"https://news.ufl.edu/2023/09/exercise-mimicking-drug/","year":2023}]},"cerebrolysin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What is actually in the ampoule, and why that is the central question","where":"lab","body":["This is not a peptide, it is a manufacturing process. Purified brain tissue from pigs approved for human food is digested with enzymes under controlled conditions, then ultrafiltered so that what passes through is mostly below 10 kilodaltons [1] (reported). The resulting solution is roughly a quarter low molecular weight peptides and three quarters free amino acids, standardised at about 215 mg per mL of nitrogen-containing substances [1] (reported).","No active ingredient has been identified. There is no sequence, no single molecular weight and no reference standard, so batches are released against process controls, composition tests and biological activity assays rather than against a defined molecule [1] (reported). Batch-to-batch variability is inherent to that approach and has been raised by regulators [1] (reported). An independent assessment described it plainly: because it is prepared from brain homogenate, content and concentration can vary between preparations and between manufacturers [2] (verified).","Two practical consequences. First, an animal-derived injectable carries contamination risk in a way a synthetic peptide does not, and that risk was explicitly noted in the same assessment [2] (verified). Second, products sold as \"cerebrolysin\" powder by research chemical suppliers cannot be the same thing as the approved European solution, because the approved product is defined by its manufacturer's process; nothing connects an unrelated powder to the trial evidence (inferred)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, and the gap in it","where":"mixed","body":["The proposal is neurotrophic activity resembling the body's own nerve growth factors, supporting neuron survival, synapse formation and neurogenesis, and limiting cell death after injury. That proposal rests on cell and animal work, and the card covers it.","What the mechanism story lacks is a bridge to the dose. With no identified active component, there is no way to say which molecule in the mixture produces which effect, no structure-activity relationship, and no way to measure drug exposure in a patient (inferred from the absence of an identified active substance). Pharmacokinetics in the ordinary sense cannot be done on an undefined mixture, which is why no half-life, distribution or clearance data exist for the product as such.","Free amino acids make up most of the mass and are not considered pharmacologically active [1] (reported). If that is right, the active fraction is a minority of what is injected, and its composition is controlled statistically rather than exactly."]},{"h":"The human record, by indication","where":"people","body":["Acute ischaemic stroke is the best-studied and the least encouraging. A 2023 Cochrane review covering seven randomised trials and 1,773 participants found no effect on all-cause death, with a risk ratio of 0.96 (95% CI 0.65 to 1.41), at moderate certainty [3] (verified). On harms it found the opposite of reassuring: non-fatal serious adverse events were more common on treatment, risk ratio 2.39 (95% CI 1.10 to 5.23) across three trials and 1,335 participants, and 2.87 (95% CI 1.24 to 6.69) in the subgroup given 30 mL for ten days [3] (verified).","The same review recorded that the manufacturer supported three of the multicentre studies, by funding them, supplying drug and placebo or randomisation codes, providing grants or supplying statisticians, and noted this may have affected design, conduct and reporting [3] (verified).","Vascular dementia reads better but weaker. A 2019 Cochrane review of six trials and 597 participants found benefit on pooled cognitive scales (standardised mean difference 0.36, 95% CI 0.13 to 0.58) and on global clinical response (risk ratio 2.69, 95% CI 1.82 to 3.98), with no difference in adverse events. Every key outcome was rated very low quality, the reviewers flagged high risk of bias and heterogeneity, warned the benefit may be too small to matter clinically, and recorded that all three trials with known funding were industry-supported [4] (verified).","Traumatic brain injury has the most favourable published result and the smallest numbers. A prospective meta-analysis of two phase 3b/4 placebo-controlled trials analysed 185 patients with admission Glasgow Coma Scale 6 to 12, given 50 mL a day for ten days then two further ten-day cycles at 10 mL. On a multidimensional ensemble of functional and neuropsychological scales it reported a small-to-medium effect favouring treatment at day 30 (standardised mean difference 0.31, p = 0.0156) and day 90 (0.34, p = 0.0146), with comparable safety [5] (verified). Note the endpoint design: a combined ensemble analysed with pre-ordered hypotheses rather than a single conventional outcome, which is a legitimate but unusual approach and one that makes comparison with other trials difficult (inferred).","Alzheimer's disease has meta-analyses reporting benefit on cognition and global change in mild to moderate disease, using 30 mL daily by infusion for four weeks to six months; an independent assessment judged several component trials small, the effects modest, one key Russian trial unreviewable because it was published only in Russian and never replicated, and the overall evidence insufficient to recommend routine use [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"Safety: a rare case where the harm signal is better quantified than the benefit","where":"people","body":["Most sources describe it as well tolerated, with transient headache, dizziness, agitation, feeling hot and weight loss, and adverse event rates similar to placebo over courses up to three years [2] (verified).","Against that, the stroke review's finding of roughly 2.4-fold more non-fatal serious adverse events, at moderate certainty, is the single most statistically solid clinical result in the whole record, and it points the wrong way [3] (verified). It was also dose-related: the signal was strongest in the 30 mL arm. The honest summary is that in acute stroke the evidence probably shows no benefit on survival and a possible increase in serious non-fatal harm.","Separate from the molecule: it is given intravenously or intramuscularly in multi-week courses, and it is biological material of animal origin, so sterility and viral or bacterial contamination are live concerns rather than theoretical ones [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["The product has one manufacturer, and the manufacturer's involvement in the trial base is documented rather than suspected: funding, drug and placebo supply, randomisation codes, grants and statistical support across several multicentre studies, with Cochrane reviewers in two separate reviews noting industry support and its possible effect on reporting [3,4] (verified).","The trial network is also concentrated in a small group of investigators across Europe and Asia, with overlapping authorship between the stroke, dementia and brain injury programmes [5] (verified by authorship). That is normal for a single-sponsor drug and it means replication by unrelated groups is largely missing."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Approved and marketed in many European, Asian, Latin American and post-Soviet countries for stroke, traumatic brain injury and dementia; not approved in the United States for anything [2] (verified). It is a prescription parenteral medicine where approved, administered in courses under supervision.","In sport, the substance is not named on the WADA Prohibited List. The route matters more than the molecule: intravenous infusions or injections totalling more than 100 mL per 12 hours outside hospital treatment, surgery or clinical investigation are a prohibited method, and clinical courses of 20 to 50 mL sit below that threshold while repeated same-day dosing could approach it (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What is the active component? Without it there is no dose-response, no exposure measurement and no way to make a defined synthetic equivalent.","Why do the stroke and brain injury results diverge? Different injury type, different timing, different endpoint design and different trial sizes are all candidates, and nobody has reconciled them.","What drove the excess non-fatal serious adverse events in the stroke trials, and is it dose-related? The subgroup pattern suggests dose; the mechanism is unknown.","Would an independently funded, adequately powered trial reproduce the dementia and brain injury findings? Both Cochrane reviews asked for exactly that, and it has not happened.","What is in a non-manufacturer product sold under this name? Nothing links such material to the approved preparation or to any of the evidence above."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Peptide Protocol Wiki, cerebrolysin molecule and manufacture page (composition, ultrafiltration, batch control)","url":"https://www.peptideprotocolwiki.com/peptides/cerebrolysin/molecule","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Alzheimer's Drug Discovery Foundation, Cognitive Vitality report on cerebrolysin","url":"https://www.alzdiscovery.org/uploads/cognitive_vitality_media/addf-cerebrolysin-full-report.pdf","year":2019},{"n":3,"t":"Ziganshina et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2023, cerebrolysin for acute ischaemic stroke","url":"https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD007026.pub7/media/CDSR/CD007026/CD007026.pdf","year":2023},{"n":4,"t":"Cui et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2019, cerebrolysin for vascular dementia","url":"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD008900_cerebrolysin-vascular-dementia","year":2019},{"n":5,"t":"Vester et al., Neurol Sci 2021, prospective meta-analysis of the CAPTAIN trial series in moderate to severe TBI","url":"https://hub.hku.hk/handle/10722/306124","year":2021}]},"bpc-157":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and what is still missing from that story","where":"lab","body":["The compound has been described in the published literature since 1993, out of a single laboratory in Zagreb [1,2]. The part of the origin story that never arrived is the parent protein. A fragment is, by definition, cut from something larger, but the protein it was supposedly cut from has never been identified in print. That means the usual chain of evidence for a natural peptide, find the protein, find the cleavage, show the body makes the fragment on purpose, was never completed (verified). In practice it is treated as a synthetic 15-residue peptide whose sequence happens to have been written down in 1993.","The naming problem is not cosmetic. BPC-157 is a common name, not an approved generic name, and the FDA flagged inconsistent naming as a safety risk in its own right, because without a reference standard there is nothing to check a batch against [2]. There are also two different substances sharing the name: the free base (CAS 137525-51-0) and the acetate salt (CAS 216441-37-1). When two compounding pharmacies nominated it for the US compounding list, they themselves were inconsistent about which of the two they meant, so the agency ended up evaluating both separately [2] (verified).","Earlier development ran under other names, PL-10, PLD-116, PL 14736 and Bepecin, through a Croatian pharmaceutical programme [1] (verified). Those names matter when reading old trial records, because the human work was done on a rectal formulation under those codes, not on anything resembling what circulates now."]},{"h":"What is actually in a vial, as far as anyone has documented","where":"lab","body":["This is the least discussed and most practically important part of the record. Reviewing the certificates of analysis submitted with the US nominations, the FDA found: no certificate at all for the free base; for the acetate, one certificate reporting 97.8% purity with related substances at 1.1% maximum individual and 2.2% total, and a second reporting only a purity figure of 98.34% [2]. In both cases the impurities were not identified, only counted. Neither certificate included endotoxin testing, bioburden, residual solvent or aggregate testing [2] (verified).","Those gaps are the reason the agency concluded the substance is not well characterised, and they feed directly into its immunogenicity concern. Solid-phase peptide synthesis can leave peptide-related impurities and aggregates behind, and aggregates are a known trigger for immune responses to peptide drugs. No immunogenicity studies of this compound exist, so the risk could not be ruled out either way [2] (verified). For a 15-residue peptide given by injection or nasal spray, the agency considered that risk significant rather than theoretical.","Stability is tighter than most handling assumes. The lyophilised powder is recommended for storage below minus 18 degrees Celsius, and once reconstituted the solution is documented as stable for only two to three weeks at refrigerator temperature [2] (verified). Nothing in the record supports the longer shelf lives often quoted.","Independent batch testing exists outside the official record and is worth reading for what it shows about spread rather than for any single verdict. One public testing service tracks 149 vendors for this compound; its default standard scores purity, dose accuracy and label quality together, and a representative vendor record shows 11 tests with 7 passing and 4 failing, including one result with a fill-volume deviation of +30% [3] (reported). The same service offers endotoxin testing but does not publish those results, so sterility and endotoxin remain the least visible part of product quality."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["No receptor has been identified, which shapes everything below. What exists is a set of signalling observations, mostly from two groups, each tied to a specific preparation.","Step one, vessel growth. In a 2017 study from a Taiwanese group, the compound raised VEGFR2 at both mRNA and protein level in endothelial cells, increased vessel density in the chick chorioallantoic membrane assay and in endothelial tube formation, and sped blood-flow recovery in rat hind-limb ischaemia measured by laser Doppler [4]. The informative detail is what did not change: VEGF-A itself was not increased [4]. So the proposal is not more growth factor but a more responsive receptor. The same work showed the receptor being pulled into the cell, and that blocking endocytosis with dynasore abolished both the downstream signalling and the extra tube formation [4] (verified, in cells and rats).","Step two, how that receptor gets switched on without its usual ligand. The same group's 2020 follow-up worked in isolated rat aortic rings and human umbilical vein endothelial cells. Relaxation was concentration-dependent and reached about 48% in rings with intact endothelium but only about 19% with the endothelium stripped away, so the effect runs through the vessel lining rather than the muscle [5]. Blocking nitric oxide cut relaxation from about 38% to 10 to 12%. In cells, phosphorylation of Src, caveolin-1 and eNOS all rose, a Src inhibitor blunted them, and the binding between eNOS and caveolin-1 fell to about half of control, which is the step that frees the enzyme to make nitric oxide [5]. Nitric oxide signal rose about 1.35-fold (verified, in cells and isolated tissue).","Step three, tendon cells specifically. In fibroblasts taken from rat Achilles tendon, a gene expression screen put the growth hormone receptor among the most strongly upregulated genes after treatment, confirmed at mRNA and protein level, dose- and time-dependently. Adding growth hormone to treated cells then raised proliferation and activated JAK2, the receptor's normal downstream kinase [6] (verified, in rat cells). The proposal is sensitisation: the peptide does not act like growth hormone, it makes the cell listen harder to whatever growth hormone is present.","What that chain does not include is a dose-response relationship. Reviewing the rodent pharmacology across colonic fistula, NSAID-lesion and liver-injury models, the FDA's assessment was that the studies lacked established dose-response relationships and mechanistic understanding [2] (verified). That is the weakest joint in the mechanism story: effects are reported across a very wide dose span without the orderly increase that normally distinguishes a drug effect from an artefact."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"animals","body":["One proper pharmacokinetic programme exists, published in 2022 by a group at Air Force Medical University in Xi'an, funded by Chinese national and provincial science foundations [7]. It is the source for nearly every number quoted elsewhere, so it is worth knowing what it actually did.","Rats received 20 micrograms per kilogram intravenously and 20, 100 or 500 micrograms per kilogram intramuscularly, plus a seven-day repeat arm and a tritium-labelled arm. Dogs received 6 micrograms per kilogram intravenously and 6, 30 or 150 intramuscularly, plus a seven-day repeat arm [7]. Elimination half-life was under 30 minutes in both species, and the FDA's reading of the data put it at 15.2 minutes in rats and 5.3 minutes in dogs [2,7]. Kinetics were linear across the tested range.","Bioavailability after intramuscular injection was about 14 to 19% in rats but 45 to 51% in dogs [7] (verified). That three-fold species gap is rarely mentioned and makes any confident extrapolation to humans unwise.","Distribution in rats peaked around one hour, highest in kidney, then liver, stomach wall, spleen and thymus; intestine, lung and skin tracked plasma; gonads, heart and skeletal muscle sat lower; brain and body fat were lowest, which suggests little crosses into the brain [7]. Over 72 hours, recovery was 15.9% in urine, 9.1% in bile, 2.3% in faeces, with about 54% still in the carcass [7]. The peptide breaks down quickly to smaller fragments and then to free amino acids that enter ordinary metabolism, with proline the main one [7] (verified).","No human pharmacokinetic data exist for oral, subcutaneous, nasal or transdermal use. In the rectal studies where plasma was measured at all, levels were undetectable or mostly below the limit of quantification [2] (verified). So there is no human exposure figure to anchor anything to.","For anyone subject to testing: metabolites are detectable in urine for four to five days by mass spectrometry, at limits of detection well below the threshold used for peptides in sport [8] (verified)."]},{"h":"The human record, in full, because it is short","where":"people","body":["A 2025 systematic review in a peer-reviewed orthopaedic journal screened the literature and found 36 studies: 35 preclinical and one clinical [8]. That ratio is the single most honest summary of the evidence base.","One controlled trial exists. Reported only as a 2005 meeting abstract, it randomised 53 people with mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis one-to-one to a daily 80 mg enema or placebo for two weeks; 46 completed, with three withdrawals for adverse events on drug and two on placebo. Mean disease activity index change was minus 3.2 (95% CI minus 5.58 to minus 0.82) on drug against minus 1.6 (95% CI minus 3.86 to 0.67) on placebo, and the reported between-group difference was 1.6 with a confidence interval from minus 4.84 to 1.62, which crosses zero [2]. Reviewing it, the FDA found the abstract inadequate: no endpoint definition, no inclusion criteria, no statistical methods [2] (verified). This is the trial that gets cited as human evidence of benefit. It did not show one.","Everything else is uncontrolled. A 2021 retrospective chart review from a private wellness clinic in Orlando enrolled 17 people with varied chronic knee pain and reached 16 by telephone; 12 received the peptide alone, of whom 11 reported improvement, and 4 received it with thymosin beta-4, of whom 3 did. Follow-up was a phone survey administered by a college student volunteer, all outcomes were self-reported with no objective testing, only 4 of 16 had a knee MRI, and patients paid out of pocket [9] (verified). The authors declared no conflicts and reported no adverse events.","A 2024 case series gave 12 women with interstitial cystitis a single 10 mg intravesical injection during cystoscopy after they had failed standard therapy; 10 reported complete symptom resolution and 2 reported about 80% improvement, with no adverse events and no dropouts [10] (reported). Uncontrolled, self-reported, single-arm.","The remaining human exposure is small and mostly safety-oriented: a rectal enema up to 2 mg/kg daily for eight days in 24 healthy subjects, and an intravenous sequence of 10 mg then 20 mg in 2 healthy subjects [2]. No serious adverse events were reported in any of these, but the FDA noted the monitoring was unclear and the studies small and short [2]. A Phase 1 safety and pharmacokinetic trial registered in Mexico in 2015 for 42 healthy volunteers has never posted results and was reportedly withdrawn from submission in 2016 [2,8] (verified).","Put plainly: there is no established human dose. Nothing in the record establishes an effective dose, a dose-response relationship, or a safe long-term dose by any route people actually use. Every figure in circulation is either an animal dose converted by body weight, a single-procedure dose from one of the uncontrolled series above, or a community convention."]},{"h":"Two trials that could change this, and how to read them","where":"people","body":["A Phase 2 trial in acute hamstring strain is recruiting: randomised, placebo-controlled, quadruple-blind, one-to-one, 120 participants aged 18 to 45, daily subcutaneous dosing for 14 days against matching placebo, both arms on standardised rehabilitation, with assessments at days 3, 7, 14, 28 and 56 and recurrence follow-up three months after return to play. The co-primary endpoints are time to unrestricted sport and change in MRI-assessed injury volume at day 14 [11] (verified). That is a real design, and MRI volume is a hard endpoint.","One caveat belongs with it. Trial registration is self-submitted and is not peer review. The sponsor's peptide studies share a single site and identical listed start and primary completion dates across a cluster of records, and one record in that cluster carries \"Mock Study\" in its registered title [12] (reported). That is not evidence of wrongdoing by anyone, and it is not a reason to dismiss the trial. It is a reason to treat the registration as an announcement rather than a result, and to wait for reported data.","A Phase 1 trial at a US university medical centre is registered for recovery after arthroscopic rotator cuff repair: randomised, parallel, quadruple-masked, self-administered daily subcutaneous dosing for 90 days after surgery, against both matched saline and a no-treatment arm, with isometric shoulder strength by handheld dynamometer at baseline, 12 weeks and 5 months as the primary outcome. Enrolment is listed as 30 in the structured field and twenty in the plain-language summary; listed start is January 2027 [12,13] (verified). Neither trial has results. Until one does, the human efficacy question stays open."]},{"h":"Safety: what has been looked at, and the shape of what has not","where":"mixed","body":["Acute animal toxicity is reassuring as far as it goes. No lethal or toxic dose has been identified. Single intramuscular dosing gave no-observed-adverse-effect levels of 20 mg/kg in rats and 10 mg/kg in dogs, and gross and histological examination of major organs found no changes over six weeks in rats and dogs [2,8]. The genotoxicity battery was negative: Ames at 5 to 2000 micrograms per plate, chromosomal aberration in CHL cells at 100 to 400 micrograms per millilitre, and a mouse micronucleus assay at 25 to 100 mg/kg intramuscularly [2]. A rat teratogenicity study gave a no-effect level of 4 mg/kg [2] (verified).","The 28-day repeat-dose study is where the picture gets more textured. Rats received 0.2, 1.0 or 4.0 mg/kg daily and dogs 0.1, 0.5 or 2.0 mg/kg daily, all intramuscular [2,14]. Clotting time moved in opposite directions in the two species: shortened in rats, prolonged in dogs [2]. Rats showed raised ALT, glucose and triglycerides, and significant changes in mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration, platelet indices and red cell distribution width; the histopathology for that study was not included in the published manuscript [2]. In dogs, serum creatinine fell significantly in females at the top dose and in males at the middle dose and above [2] (verified). None of this is alarming on its own. It is also not a clean bill of health, and it is the opposite of the \"no side effects in any study\" claim that circulates.","The absences matter more than any finding. There are no carcinogenicity studies. There are no repeat-dose studies longer than 28 days, in any species. There are no acute or repeat-dose studies by the routes people actually use, oral, subcutaneous, nasal or transdermal. There are no peri- or post-natal developmental studies [2] (verified).","Human adverse event reporting is thin and hard to attribute. Three reports reached the FDA's surveillance system, all involving injectable products: injection-site redness and swelling in someone also injecting thymosin, shortness of breath with minimal detail, and hyperpigmentation with darkening of the gums that recurred on re-exposure to a combination product labelled for research purposes only. None could be attributed to this peptide alone [2] (verified).","Two theoretical concerns deserve to be named as theoretical. First, a mechanism that works by promoting new vessel growth is, in principle, a mechanism that could support tumour growth; no study has tested this, and one cancer-cachexia study found no significant effect on tumour size [1] (speculated). Second, an independent review noted that proline, the main metabolite, can generate reactive oxygen species through proline oxidase, and that sustained excess nitric oxide has its own downstream problems [1] (speculated). Both are reasoned arguments from known biology, not observations."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence, and why that is part of the evidence","where":"lab","body":["Most of the animal literature traces to one group in Zagreb across three decades [1,2]. That is not an accusation; founding groups often dominate early literature on a compound they discovered. It does mean the usual safeguard of independent replication is largely absent, and that reading five papers does not necessarily mean reading five independent assessments.","The useful development is that independent groups have now entered. The angiogenesis and vasomotor mechanism work came from a Taiwanese university group with no connection to the original laboratory [4,5]. The pharmacokinetics came from a Chinese military medical university [7]. A 2025 literature and patent review came from an independent Polish academic group, and it drew the cancer and metabolite concerns above, which the original group then contested in a published comment-and-reply exchange in the same journal [1] (verified). Disagreement in print is a sign of a field becoming normal.","Independent does not mean disinterested. The authors of the 2025 orthopaedic systematic review disclosed relationships with several orthopaedic device companies, and the review itself contains internal inconsistencies in its study counts between text and tables [8] (verified). Worth knowing when quoting its numbers.","The patent landscape is scattered across many unrelated private parties, covering uses from acute respiratory distress to scar dressings to a sublingual combination with semaglutide [1]. No single company holds a development programme that would plausibly fund the trials needed for approval, which is part of why the evidence has stayed where it is."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product exists in any country, and the substance appears in no major pharmacopoeia [2] (verified).","The US picture changed twice in 2026 and is easy to misread. In April, the FDA removed twelve peptide bulk substances from the compounding Category 2 list, effective 22 April 2026. That happened because the original nominators withdrew their nominations, not because the agency revised a safety judgement [15]. Removal from Category 2 did not place the substance in Category 1 or on the approved bulks list, so it sits in a gap: still an unapproved new drug, without even the Category 1 enforcement-discretion policy to rely on [15] (verified).","In July, the agency's Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee met on the 23rd and 24th and voted 8 to 6 with one abstention to recommend adding it to the 503A bulks list, for ulcerative colitis, the indication the agency had reviewed [16,17]. The agency's own scientists had recommended against, finding the evaluation criteria weighed against both the free base and the acetate [2]. Their stated difficulty was identity before efficacy: these peptides have no universally accepted chemical definition, which makes evaluation very hard [16]. The committee also recommended KPV and TB-500 by the same margin [17].","What that vote is not: approval, a finding of benefit, or permission to compound. It is advisory. Adding a substance to the bulks list requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, which the agency has not begun and faces no deadline to begin; one attorney quoted at the time estimated eight to twelve months before pharmacies would have unambiguous authority [16,18] (verified).","The live development is an interim route. On 14 October 2026, the agency and its department sent an \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\" to the White House budget office for regulatory review. As of mid-October it had not been issued or published, and its terms, including which peptides it covers and what conditions attach, are not public [18] (verified). The expected shape is enforcement discretion for prescription compounding by state-licensed 503A pharmacies, possibly with route restrictions, patient disclosures and adverse-event reporting attached, and it could be withdrawn without rulemaking [18] (reported). Pressure runs both ways: a drug-safety organisation filed a citizen petition in October 2026 asking the agency to stop compounding of untested peptides [19] (reported).","Elsewhere: prohibited in sport at all times under the non-approved substances class [2,20]. On the US Department of Defense prohibited ingredients list, so service members are told to avoid it by any route [20]. Not a controlled substance under US drug scheduling, which is why it is sold as a research chemical with not-for-human-consumption labelling [8,20]. A New Zealand committee proposed prescription classification in May 2023 [2]. The agency has separately cautioned against compounded products containing it, citing safety risks and possible contamination with other substances [20] (verified)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What protein is it a fragment of? Until that is published, the claim that this is a natural human peptide rests on a 1993 assertion rather than a demonstrated biosynthetic pathway.","Is there a receptor? Every mechanism above is a downstream signalling observation. Without a binding target, there is no way to predict tissue selectivity, no structure-activity work, and no rational basis for dose.","Does any of it survive a controlled human trial? The hamstring trial's MRI endpoint is the first design capable of answering that for soft tissue. One negative result there would matter a great deal.","Why does the species gap in bioavailability exist, and which way do humans fall? Three-fold between rats and dogs, with no human data, is a wide gap to guess across.","What happens past 28 days? The entire long-term safety question is unexamined, in any species, by any route. That is the single largest hole, given that reported use runs in multi-week courses.","Does the pro-angiogenic mechanism carry an oncological risk? Unstudied. It cannot be ruled in or out from current data.","Is the material people obtain the material in the studies? Given unidentified impurities, absent endotoxin and aggregate testing, and a documented spread in independent batch results, this is arguably the most answerable open question, and the one most within reach of better testing rather than better trials."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Józwiak et al., Pharmaceuticals, multifunctionality and patent review","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11859134/","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Finnrick, independent vendor batch test records","url":"https://www.finnrick.com/products/bpc-157/peptide-partners","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Hsieh et al., J Mol Med, pro-angiogenic action via VEGFR2","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27847966/","year":2017},{"n":5,"t":"Hsieh et al., Sci Rep, vasomotor tone and Src-caveolin-1-eNOS","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7555539/","year":2020},{"n":6,"t":"Chang et al., Molecules, growth hormone receptor in tendon fibroblasts","url":"https://doaj.org/article/0dc8d3023dc34ac1b5f4ca3d303a5aba","year":2014},{"n":7,"t":"He et al., Front Pharmacol, pharmacokinetics and excretion in rats and dogs","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9794587","year":2022},{"n":8,"t":"Vasireddi et al., HSS J, systematic review in orthopaedic sports medicine","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12313605/","year":2025},{"n":9,"t":"Lee and Padgett, Altern Ther Health Med, intra-articular series for knee pain","url":"https://asipp.org/wp-content/uploads/Intra-Articular-Injection-of-BPC-157-for-Multiple-Types-of-Knee-Pain-2021-Alternative-Therapies-in-Health-and-Medicine.pdf","year":2021},{"n":10,"t":"Lee et al., Altern Ther Health Med, interstitial cystitis pilot","url":"https://www.urotoday.com/recent-abstracts/pelvic-health-reconstruction/interstitial-cystitis/155444-effect-of-bpc-157-on-symptoms-in-patients-with-interstitial-cystitis-a-pilot-study.html","year":2024},{"n":11,"t":"ClinicalTrials.gov NCT07437547, Phase 2 acute hamstring strain","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07437547","year":2026},{"n":12,"t":"The Peptide Catalog, registration provenance and rotator cuff trial detail","url":"https://thepeptidecatalog.com/articles/bpc-157-rotator-cuff-surgery-trial","year":2026},{"n":13,"t":"ClinicalTrials.gov NCT07803250, Phase 1 after rotator cuff repair","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07803250","year":2026},{"n":14,"t":"Xu et al., Regul Toxicol Pharmacol, preclinical safety evaluation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.yrtph.2020.104665","year":2020},{"n":15,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 Category 2 removal","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":16,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026},{"n":17,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":18,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":19,"t":"Partnership for Safe Medicines, citizen petition on compounded peptides","url":"https://www.safemedicines.org/2026/10/psm-files-citizen-petitio.html","year":2026},{"n":20,"t":"Operation Supplement Safety (US DoD), prohibited peptide and unapproved drug","url":"https://www.opss.org/article/bpc-157-prohibited-peptide-and-unapproved-drug-found-health-and-wellness-products","year":2025}]},"tb-500-frag-17-23":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What was actually nominated, and under which name","where":"lab","body":["In 2026 US regulators evaluated this fragment for the compounding list, and the paperwork is instructive. The nominated product was a 3 mg per millilitre lyophilised powder for subcutaneous and intramuscular injection, for wound healing [1] (verified). Not tendon repair, not recovery, not injury. Wound healing is the single claim that was formally assessed.","The nomination described the active entity as the LKKTETQ site within the parent protein, and regulators evaluated two substances under the name: a free base at 889.01 g/mol under one CAS number, and an acetate salt at 949.1 under another [1]. The nomination's own stated alternate identifier matched neither of them [1] (verified). The named substance was the free base while the submitted certificate of analysis was for the acetate, listing the acetate's CAS number, formula and molecular weight.","Regulators noted that this is a common name rather than an approved generic name, and treated the inconsistent naming as a patient safety issue in itself: with no reference standard, there is nothing against which a batch can be checked [1] (verified). The doping literature's use of the name for this fragment, rather than for the full protein, is the older and more precise usage, but it is not a regulatory identity."]},{"h":"What the certificates do and do not cover","where":"lab","body":["For the free base, no certificate of analysis was submitted with the nomination. A publicly available one tested appearance, identity by mass spectrometry and HPLC, and peptide purity, and did not test assay, impurities, bacterial endotoxins or aggregates [1] (verified).","For the acetate, the certificate was more complete: appearance, solubility, amino acid composition, water content, acetate content, impurity limits of 2.0% total and 1.0% for the largest single impurity, and purity. It did not report identification, assay, aggregates or bacterial endotoxins [1] (verified).","The impurity limits are the quiet problem. A limit caps how much unidentified material is present without saying what it is. Regulators' concern is that unidentified peptide-related impurities and aggregates are a known driver of immune responses to injected peptides, and they judged injectable routes to carry significant immunogenicity risk on that basis, with no immunogenicity studies available for either form [1] (verified). Both were concluded to be not physically and chemically well characterised.","Storage is colder than most handling assumes: below minus 20 degrees Celsius for the free base, below minus 15 for the acetate [1]."]},{"h":"What a seven-residue fragment can and cannot inherit","where":"cells","body":["The inheritance argument is that this stretch is the actin-binding motif, so it should carry the parent protein's repair effects. There is a specific reason to be careful with that reasoning, and it is not the one usually given.","The front end of this fragment carries an acetyl group. The wound-healing data most often invoked for it came from a 2003 study that used the non-acetylated seven-residue sequence, applied topically, in aged mice. Regulators' position is that this cannot be extrapolated to the acetylated injected form, because both the molecule and the route differ [1] (verified). So the foundational positive result is for a chemically different peptide delivered a different way.","That matters more than it sounds. Acetylation changes charge at the front end of a very short peptide, and in a seven-residue molecule the front end is a large fraction of the whole. It is not a cosmetic modification.","Regulators also stated plainly that this fragment and the 43-residue parent protein are not the same substance [1] (verified). The practical consequence runs in both directions: data from the parent cannot be assumed to transfer down, and a vial containing the parent under this name does not contain what the label says."]},{"h":"The one direct test of the marketed molecule","where":"cells","body":["This is the most important and least circulated finding about this compound.","In a 2024 in vitro fibroblast scratch assay, the free base at 50 micrograms per millilitre did not significantly promote wound closure. A metabolite, the N-acetylated five-residue fragment, showed a small but statistically significant effect at the same concentration [1] (verified, cells).","A single negative assay does not settle a question. But this is the only direct test of the marketed fragment against its own nominated indication, it used a high concentration, and it did not support the claim. The accompanying observation that a shorter breakdown product did something is more interesting than the negative itself: if activity sits with a metabolite rather than the administered peptide, then the whole logic of what to give and when changes (inferred).","Set against that, there is no cell or animal study showing the acetylated fragment reproducing the parent protein's wound, cornea or heart results. The assumption of shared activity has not been tested and, where tested at all, was not confirmed."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"animals","body":["Pharmacokinetics amount to one study in a species nobody is dosing. Horses given 10 mg subcutaneously reached plasma peaks of 0.05 and 0.08 nanograms per millilitre [1] (verified). There is no intramuscular pharmacokinetic data for either form, and none in humans by any route [1].","Those plasma figures are strikingly low for a 10 mg dose, which is consistent with a short peptide being cleared and broken down fast. It also means there is no exposure number from any species to anchor a human expectation to, and nothing to connect a given amount injected to a concentration at a tendon.","The detection side is better developed than the therapeutic side, because the compound's first scientific attention came from drug testing rather than from medicine. That asymmetry is worth noticing: the methods for proving someone used it are more mature than the methods for showing it works."]},{"h":"Safety: unopened rather than open","where":"lab","body":["No acute toxicity studies, no repeat-dose studies, no genotoxicity studies, no developmental or reproductive studies and no carcinogenicity studies were submitted or identified for either form [1] (verified).","So the standard questions have not been asked: what dose causes harm, which organ is affected first, what happens over months, whether it damages DNA, whether it affects pregnancy, whether it causes tumours. Regulators noted the absence of carcinogenicity data without drawing a conclusion from it [1].","Human surveillance is similarly empty. The adverse event database returned nothing for this substance. A separate complaint system returned two cases involving a blended product combining it with another repair peptide, with no safety assessment attached, and causality could not be assigned to either component [1] (verified). That is a direct cost of blending, not a finding about this molecule.","The commonly repeated claim of an excellent safety profile with no reported side effects is, against this record, a statement about absent studies and absent surveillance. The reported pattern of headache and flu-like fatigue in community use has no counterpart anywhere in the formal literature and no mechanism has been investigated for it (reported)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Regulators proposed not adding either form to the 503A compounding bulks list, citing poor characterisation, no history of use in compounding, no evidence of effectiveness for wound healing, no human safety data, immunogenicity risk, and the existence of approved wound therapies [1] (verified).","The advisory committee recommended adding it anyway, voting 8 to 6 with one abstention on 23 July 2026, for wound healing [2,3]. Agency staff had recommended against all seven peptides considered that week.","That recommendation is advisory. Addition requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, which has not begun and carries no deadline [2] (verified). An \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\" went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [2]. Removal from the do-not-compound category in April 2026 followed withdrawn nominations rather than a revised safety finding, and left the substance outside any recognised category rather than cleared [4] (verified)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the acetylated fragment do anything at all? The one direct test against its own nominated indication was negative, and the positive wound data belongs to a chemically different peptide applied topically. This is the field's central unexamined assumption, and it is testable cheaply.","If activity sits with a metabolite, what follows? The 2024 assay's only positive result was for a breakdown product, which would change the logic of what is worth administering.","Which molecule is in the vial? Regulators state the fragment and the parent protein are different substances, and no routine identity test in grey-market supply distinguishes them, so pooled user reports describe a mixture of molecules.","What exposure does any dose produce in a human? One horse study is the entire pharmacokinetic record.","Everything about toxicology. There is none, in any species, by any route, which makes long-term safety an unopened question rather than an open one.","Why the divergence between forum reports and app-logged favourability? Both populations are self-selected, and the gap may be as much about which molecule people actually received as about effect."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026}]},"ghk-cu":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The experiment underneath the claim","where":"cells","body":["The origin story is usually told as \"young blood made old tissue act young\", which is true but skips the actual design, and the design is what sets the limits of the claim. The work grew out of studies of fibrinogen, a clotting protein that rises with age. Liver tissue from patients aged 60 to 80 showed elevated fibrinogen production. When liver cells from those older patients were incubated in blood from younger people, they behaved almost like younger tissue [1] (verified).","So the original observation was about a liver cell's protein output in culture, not about skin, not about collagen, and not about an organism getting younger. The peptide was isolated from human plasma albumin and reported in Nature in 1973 (Pickart and Thaler, 243:85-87), and the sequence was identified four years later in Experientia (Schlesinger, Pickart and Thaler, 1977, 33:324-325) [1] (verified). Everything since has been an extension from that starting point to tissues it was not originally studied in.","The widely quoted figures for plasma levels declining with age, roughly 200 nanograms per millilitre at 20 against 80 at 60, circulate without a clear primary citation attached to them in the reference literature [1] (reported). They may well be right. They are also the load-bearing premise of the entire replacement rationale, and that premise is less well sourced than its popularity suggests."]},{"h":"Why bound copper behaves differently from loose copper","where":"lab","body":["This is the most chemically solid part of the record and the part that gets least attention. Free copper ions are reactive in a way that damages tissue: they cycle between oxidation states and generate reactive species. A 1981 study in the Biochemical Journal (Lau and Sarkar) showed that when copper is held by this tripeptide, that redox activity is effectively silenced [1] (verified, in vitro).","That is the real argument for the complex over a copper salt. It is not that the peptide delivers more copper, it is that it delivers copper in a form that does not do damage on the way. The binding geometry has been determined independently by X-ray crystallography, electron paramagnetic resonance and nuclear magnetic resonance (Hureau et al., Chemistry, 2011) [1] (verified), so this is structural chemistry rather than inference.","The practical reading: the copper chemistry is well understood, and it is the best-established thing about this molecule. The biology built on top of it is considerably softer."]},{"h":"What the cell studies measured, and the direction nobody quotes","where":"cells","body":["The fibroblast work is real and reasonably old. Collagen synthesis in cultured skin fibroblasts was reported in FEBS Letters in 1988 (Maquart et al.); sulfated glycosaminoglycan synthesis in Life Sciences in 1992 (Wegrowski et al.); matrix metalloproteinase-2 expression in fibroblast cultures in Life Sciences in 2000 (Siméon et al.), with decorin reported by the same group [1] (reported, cells). These are the primary sources for almost every claim about collagen and matrix.","Here is the part that rarely survives the trip into marketing copy. The molecule stimulates both the matrix-degrading enzymes and their inhibitors. The reference literature draws the obvious conclusion from that and says it should therefore be used with caution [1] (verified). Simultaneously pushing breakdown and the brake on breakdown is coherent as a remodelling signal, where old matrix has to come out before new matrix goes in. It is also not the same thing as \"builds collagen\", and in a tissue that is not actively repairing, net remodelling is not obviously a benefit.","A note on how to read these: cell-culture concentrations in this literature are often in the nanomolar range, applied directly to fibroblasts in a dish. Nothing in that design speaks to what concentration reaches a fibroblast in living skin through any route."]},{"h":"The gene-expression claim, traced to its source","where":"cells","body":["The \"resets thousands of genes toward repair\" framing has one main scientific parent, and it is worth reading accurately because what it found is more interesting and less flattering than the summary.","A 2012 study in Genome Medicine profiled gene expression in lung tissue from eight regions each of six patients with severe emphysema plus two donor lungs, 64 regions in total, and matched the expression patterns to emphysema severity measured histologically. The resulting signature was then queried against a drug-expression database to find compounds whose effects ran opposite to it. This tripeptide came out of that screen [2] (verified).","Then they tested it. In distal lung fibroblasts from former smokers with emphysema, the peptide at 10 nanomolar did three things: it changed where integrin-beta-1 sat in the cell and reorganised actin into contractile filaments; it restored collagen gel contraction, which is impaired in these cells, back toward the level seen in former smokers without the disease; and under second-harmonic imaging the treated cells remodelled collagen into fibrils more effectively [2] (verified, human cells).","The detail that reframes everything: the paper reports that these effects resembled activation of the TGF-beta pathway, and that the peptide acted like TGF-beta-1 in these assays [2] (verified). TGF-beta is the central pro-fibrotic signal in the body. So the mechanism behind the lung finding is not a gentle rejuvenation programme, it is engagement of a fibrosis-adjacent pathway that happens to be useful when the problem is tissue that has lost its ability to contract and remodel. That is a genuinely promising finding for a specific repair deficit. It is a poor argument for continuous use in healthy tissue (inferred)."]},{"h":"The animal wound work","where":"animals","body":["Wound healing has been reported across rats, rabbits, mice and pigs, much of it in veterinary journals: Veterinary Dermatology in 2006 and 2008 (Cangul et al.; Gul et al.), Veterinary Surgery in 2003 (Canapp et al.), and Life Sciences in 2007 in diabetic rats (Arul et al.) [1] (reported, animals).","The most quotable single number comes from a rat ischaemic skin flap model, where wound area fell 64.5% against 28.2% in controls [1] (reported). Ischaemic flaps and diabetic rats share a feature worth noticing: both are models of impaired healing. The pattern across this literature is that effects show up most clearly where repair is already failing, which is consistent with the lung finding above and inconsistent with a general-purpose enhancement story."]},{"h":"The human record, including the trial that failed","where":"people","body":["The published positive human evidence is topical and small: controlled facial studies reporting firming and reduced wrinkle depth, which the reference literature itself characterises as having relatively small sample sizes and short durations [1] (verified). That is a fair description and not a dismissal; small short trials are how cosmetic ingredients are normally assessed.","What is almost entirely missing from the popular account is that this molecule had a proper pivotal programme, and it did not succeed. Under the name Iamin Gel, a Washington-state company ran two Phase III trials in diabetic plantar foot ulcers, enrolling 511 patients across 30 medical centres, testing 0.5% and 2% formulations against placebo [3,4]. The result, announced in October, was that the drug was no better than placebo [4] (verified). The company's share price fell 68% on the day [4].","That matters for how the earlier positive ulcer trial should be read. A smaller randomised trial reporting faster closure, followed by a 511-patient multi-centre pivotal programme finding no difference against placebo, is the classic pattern of an early signal that did not replicate at scale. The pivotal result is the more informative of the two, because it was larger, controlled, multi-centre and designed to be decisive. No approved product followed.","For the injected route, there is still no human study of any kind, and no therapeutic trial registered. That has not changed, and it is the route with the thinnest evidence and the loudest claims."]},{"h":"The route with no regulatory file","where":"lab","body":["There is an asymmetry worth knowing when comparing this compound to its stack-mates. For BPC-157, TB-500 and KPV, the US agency published detailed assessments in 2026 covering identity, certificates of analysis, impurities, endotoxin and aggregate testing, toxicology and immunogenicity. For injectable copper tripeptide, no such document exists yet, because its advisory review was deferred to a later meeting [5,6] (verified).","So the honest statement is not that injected use has been assessed and cleared, nor that it has been assessed and faulted. It has not been assessed. The questions the agency asked of the other three, what exactly is in the vial, what the unidentified impurities are, whether aggregates are present, whether endotoxin is tested, whether an injected peptide-metal complex raises immunogenicity concerns, have not yet been asked in public of this one.","The additional variable here, absent from the other three, is the metal. An injected copper complex raises questions a plain peptide does not: how much copper is delivered, what happens to it after the peptide is degraded, and what repeated dosing does to copper handling. Nothing in the available record addresses this (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["The sequence in 2026 was messier than the summaries suggest, and the detail matters because it changes what is permitted. On 22 April 2026, non-injectable copper tripeptide was removed from Category 1 as part of a bulk withdrawal action affecting twelve peptide substances, which happened because nominators withdrew rather than because of any safety finding. On 5 May 2026 the nominator clarified that it had intended to withdraw only the injectable-route nomination, and the topical form was restored to Category 1, where it sits under temporary compounding enforcement discretion [5] (verified).","The injectable route was left unclassified: not in Category 1, not in Category 2, not on the approved bulks list [5] (verified). Legal commentary on that gap advises against compounding it while it persists.","Injectable copper tripeptide is scheduled for review at a second advisory committee meeting expected before the end of February 2027, alongside four other peptides, with no date set as of mid-October 2026 [5,6] (reported). That meeting is the next real decision point.","Cosmetic topical use is a separate world and continues unaffected, since the ingredient is a long-established cosmetic additive rather than a drug in that context."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Why did the pivotal ulcer programme fail where the smaller trial succeeded? Dose, formulation, wound selection and trial size are all candidates, and nobody has published a post-mortem. Until someone does, the honest reading is that topical healing benefit did not replicate at scale.","Is the TGF-beta-like action a feature or a warning? It explains the lung and wound results and it is also the pathway behind fibrosis. The answer probably depends entirely on whether the tissue is damaged or healthy, which is exactly the distinction continuous cosmetic use erases.","What does the plasma-decline figure actually rest on? The replacement rationale depends on it and its primary source is hard to pin down.","What happens to the copper after injection, and does repeated dosing shift copper handling? Unstudied, and unique to this compound among the common repair peptides.","Does simultaneous stimulation of matrix-degrading enzymes and their inhibitors net out positively in skin that is not injured? The reference literature's own caution suggests this is not settled.","Does injected use do anything a topical does not? With no human study of the injected route at all, this remains a claim rather than a finding, despite being the form most discussed."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Encyclopedia reference article, copper peptide GHK-Cu, with primary citations indexed","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_peptide_GHK-Cu","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Campbell et al., Genome Medicine, emphysema gene signature and its reversal by GHK","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/gm367","year":2012},{"n":3,"t":"BioCentury, peptide-copper gel completed two Phase III trials in diabetic foot ulcers","url":"https://biocentury.com/article/111229/iamin-gel-peptide-copper-compound-completed-two-phase-iii-trials","year":1994},{"n":4,"t":"The Pharma Letter, lead product no better than placebo in pivotal trials","url":"https://www.thepharmaletter.com/procyte-s-lead-product-no-better-than-placebo","year":1994},{"n":5,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"kpv":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What was actually nominated, and for what","where":"lab","body":["When a compounding pharmacy asked US regulators to allow this tripeptide onto the 503A list, the request was for a 0.1% cream or gel, applied topically, for wound healing and inflammatory skin conditions such as psoriasis and eczema [1] (verified). The nomination was later withdrawn, and the agency carried on evaluating both the free base and the acetate salt on its own initiative.","That detail is worth holding onto, because the official evidence review is about a skin cream. It is not about injection, not about oral capsules and not about the gut, even though the agency noted that online marketing promotes injectable, oral, topical and nasal forms for a long list of conditions including colitis and Crohn's disease [1] (verified). The gap between what was formally assessed and what circulates is wider here than for any other peptide in this group.","Both salts share a single CAS number in most public references, which sounds convenient and is actually a problem: it means the paperwork does not reliably distinguish them. The free base is 342.43 g/mol, the acetate 402.5 [1]. In the nomination the agency reviewed, the certificate of analysis was titled for the acetate while listing the free base's molecular formula [1] (verified). Like its stack-mates, KPV is a common name rather than an approved generic name, which the agency flagged as a safety risk in itself."]},{"h":"The citation audit","where":"lab","body":["This is the most clarifying single fact in the record, and it comes from the agency simply reading the sources it was given. The nomination cited nine references in support of effectiveness. Eight were animal studies of alpha-MSH derivatives. One was a study in human cadaver skin. None of the nine tested this tripeptide in a living human [1] (verified).","Reviewing the wider literature itself, the agency found no clinical evidence for either nominated indication, and noted that approved therapies exist for both [1]. It also found no published human studies of compounded products containing it, and no record of any outsourcing facility compounding it between January 2017 and June 2025 [1] (verified). So the claim of established practical use does not hold up either.","A fair summary: the evidence for this molecule is a well-constructed argument from a parent hormone's biology, supported by animal and cell work, with nothing human attached to it at any point."]},{"h":"Mechanism, step by step, and where each step was shown","where":"cells","body":["Step one, getting in. The proposed route into cells is the PepT1 transporter, a di- and tripeptide carrier. This was shown in human intestinal epithelial cell lines, specifically Caco2-BBE and HT29-CL.19A, and in Jurkat T cells [1,2] (verified, cells). The appeal of this mechanism is that the transporter is more active in inflamed intestine, so the molecule would concentrate where it is needed. That logic is sound and it is also why oral use became the common route.","Step two, what happens inside. The proposed actions are inhibition of NF-kappa-B activation and interference with interleukin-1-beta signalling [1] (verified as proposed). NF-kappa-B is the master switch for inflammatory gene expression, so blocking its activation is a coherent explanation for broad calming effects across tissues.","What is missing from this chain is any quantitative anchor. There is no measured binding affinity to a target, no demonstrated dose-response in a living animal tied to tissue concentration, and no pharmacokinetic data at all, in any species, by any route [1] (verified). The mechanism is a plausible route and a plausible destination with no measurements of the journey."]},{"h":"The receptor question, answered more sharply than usual","where":"cells","body":["The familiar framing is that this fragment keeps alpha-MSH's calming action without the tanning, and works without the tanning receptor. The agency's review puts it more strongly than that, and the stronger version is more interesting.","Tested directly, the tripeptide did not displace alpha-MSH from its binding site, and it did not raise cyclic AMP in macrophages expressing the MC1R receptor. The agency's conclusion was that melanocortin receptors are unlikely to be its targets at all [1] (verified, cells).","So this is not a selective melanocortin agonist that skips the pigment effect. It is a fragment of a hormone that appears to work through a different mechanism entirely, probably intracellular, reached by a peptide transporter rather than a surface receptor. That reframes the whole family resemblance: the alpha-MSH lineage explains where the sequence came from, not how it acts (inferred).","It also means the usual reassurance, that this is just a well-understood hormone's active core, does not hold. The hormone's receptor pharmacology does not transfer to it."]},{"h":"The colitis work, in detail","where":"animals","body":["The animal result that built this molecule's reputation is a 2008 study in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases, and it is genuinely striking. It used two independent colitis models: chemical colitis induced by 2.5% dextran sodium sulfate in drinking water for six days, and an immune-driven model created by transferring CD45RBhi T cells [3] (verified).","In the chemical model, treated mice recovered earlier and regained more body weight, with fewer inflammatory infiltrates and lower colonic myeloperoxidase activity, which is a direct measure of neutrophil infiltration rather than a subjective score. In the transfer model, treated mice recovered, regained weight and showed less histological inflammation [3].","The decisive arm used mice with a non-functional MC1R receptor. In those animals, treatment rescued every treated mouse from death during chemical colitis [3] (verified). That is the experiment behind the receptor-independence claim, and it is a strong design: if the effect survives removal of the proposed receptor, the receptor is not required.","Two limits on reading it. The published abstract does not state the dose, schedule or route, so the study cannot be used to derive any dosing logic [3]. And both models are acute chemical or immune injury in mice, which is the standard proxy for inflammatory bowel disease and has a long history of producing results that did not carry into human trials (verified)."]},{"h":"Human data, and what that means for dose","where":"people","body":["There are no human studies of this tripeptide, for any indication, by any route. The agency found no human products, no clinical studies and no human pharmacokinetic data [1] (verified). Its adverse event database and its complaint system together returned no reports through 3 December 2025 [1].","That absence of adverse event reports is sometimes read as evidence of safety. It is better read as evidence of absent surveillance: an unapproved substance bought as a research chemical and self-administered does not generate the reports that an approved drug does, and nobody is collecting them.","There is no established human dose, and no study from which one could be derived. Every number in circulation originates from community convention or from converting animal doses, and the underlying animal studies frequently do not publish the doses they used in retrievable form. This is a case where the usual practice of citing \"reported\" ranges has nothing underneath it at the research end.","One route-specific finding deserves attention, because it cuts against the nominated form. The tripeptide did not permeate human cadaver skin well. Penetration increased in vitro when iontophoresis or microneedle abrasion was applied [1] (verified). So the one form that was formally nominated, a plain topical cream, faces a delivery problem that the nomination's own supporting science identified."]},{"h":"Chemistry and product reality","where":"lab","body":["For the free base, no certificate of analysis accompanied the nomination. Publicly available certificates report only purity, with no impurity limits, no aggregate testing and no microbial testing [1]. For the acetate, the certificate reported total impurities of 0.9% against a 2.0% limit and a largest single impurity of 0.24% against a 1.0% limit, which is good, but gave no impurity identities, no aggregate data and no microbial testing [1] (verified).","The agency concluded that neither form is well characterised, and that there is insufficient data to conclude that immunogenicity and aggregation risks are absent [1] (verified).","Two chemistry details with practical consequences. The salts differ sharply in solubility: the free base dissolves to about 0.70 mg per millilitre in water, the acetate to about 5 mg per millilitre [1]. A product made with one and handled as though it were the other will not behave as expected. And storage differs: the free base is reported stable for up to three years at minus 20 degrees Celsius, while the acetate's stated storage is ordinary refrigeration at 2 to 8 degrees [1].","Under forced degradation the acetate breaks down to a cyclised product, Lys-Pro diketopiperazine [1] (verified). Cyclisation of a proline-containing peptide is a known and predictable degradation route, and it means a degraded vial does not simply contain less of the peptide, it contains something else."]},{"h":"Safety: unexamined rather than reassuring","where":"lab","body":["No acute toxicity studies, no repeat-dose studies, no genotoxicity studies, no developmental or reproductive studies and no carcinogenicity studies were submitted or identified for either form [1] (verified). No pharmacokinetic or toxicokinetic studies exist either.","So the standard questions have not been asked. What dose causes harm, which organ is affected first, what happens over months, whether it affects DNA or pregnancy: all unexamined.","The reported pattern of early reactions in community use, headaches and flu-like feelings sometimes at low exposures, with people who have mast-cell problems reacting at the smallest amounts, has no counterpart anywhere in the formal record, and no mechanism has been investigated for it (reported). Given a proposed mechanism that acts on inflammatory signalling and an entry route through a transporter that immune cells express, an immune-mediated reaction is not implausible, but nothing has tested it (speculated).","The agency's overall position was that there is no human safety data and the risks are unknown [1]. That is a precise statement and worth preferring over any confident claim in either direction."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["The agency's own staff proposed not adding either form to the 503A bulks list, for four stated reasons: neither substance is well characterised, the extent of compounding use is unknown, there is no evidence of effectiveness in humans, and there is no human safety data with risks unknown [1] (verified).","The advisory committee recommended adding it anyway, voting 8 to 6 with one abstention on 23 July 2026, for wound healing and inflammatory conditions [2,4]. Staff had found that the evidence weighed against it, noting that none of the studies they located involved humans [5].","As with the others, that recommendation is advisory and alters nothing by itself. Addition requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, which has not started and has no deadline [4] (verified). An \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\" went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [4]. Removal from the do-not-compound category in April 2026 happened because nominations were withdrawn, not because of a revised safety judgement, and left the substance outside any recognised category [6] (verified).","In sport, it is not named on the prohibited list, which places it under the catch-all class for non-approved substances rather than outside the rules."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["If melanocortin receptors are not the target, what is? Without an identified target there is no basis for predicting selectivity, no structure-activity work and no rational dose.","Does anything reach the intended tissue? With zero pharmacokinetic data in any species and poor permeation through human skin, the delivery question is unanswered for every route in use.","Do the mouse colitis results mean anything for human inflammatory bowel disease? This model has a long record of promising results that did not translate, and no human trial has ever been attempted here, let alone registered.","What explains the early reactions people report at very low exposures? Nothing in the formal record addresses this, and it is the most common practical complaint.","Which salt is in a given product, and was it handled accordingly? Shared CAS numbers, a seven-fold solubility difference and different storage requirements make this genuinely ambiguous.","Everything about long-term safety. There is no toxicology of any kind, which makes this unopened rather than open."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, KPV bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193346/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Dalmasso et al., Gastroenterology, PepT1-mediated KPV uptake reduces intestinal inflammation","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2431115","year":2008},{"n":3,"t":"Kannengiesser et al., Inflamm Bowel Dis, KPV in murine models of inflammatory bowel disease","url":"https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/14/3/324/4653598","year":2008},{"n":4,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026}]},"thymosin-beta-4":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Two different molecules, one market name","where":"lab","body":["This is the thing to settle before anything else, because almost every confused claim about this compound starts here. The full protein is 43 residues. The material nominated and sold as TB-500 is a seven-residue fragment, residues 17 to 23, carrying an acetyl group on its front end. Reviewing them for the US compounding list, the FDA stated plainly that TB-500 and thymosin beta-4 are not the same substance [1] (verified).","That has a practical consequence that cuts against the usual reassurance. People often say grey-market TB-500 is \"actually the full molecule, which is better studied\". Sometimes it is. But the two have different evidence bases, different stability, different molecular weights and different regulatory records, and a label naming one while containing the other is a mislabelling either way. The free base fragment is listed at 889.01 g/mol under one CAS number, the acetate at 949.1 under another, and the nomination's own stated identifier matched neither of them [1] (verified).","The identity confusion was not limited to buyers. In the nomination the agency reviewed, the substance named was the free base while the submitted certificate of analysis was for the acetate salt, listing the acetate's CAS number, formula and molecular weight [1]. Like the other peptides in this group, TB-500 is a common name rather than an approved generic name, which the agency flagged as a patient safety risk because there is no reference standard to test a batch against [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"What was actually nominated, and what the paperwork showed","where":"lab","body":["The nominated product was a 3 mg per millilitre lyophilised powder for subcutaneous and intramuscular injection, for wound healing [1] (verified). That is the specific claim the agency evaluated, not recovery, not tendon repair.","For the free base, no certificate of analysis was submitted at all. A publicly available certificate tested appearance, identity by mass spectrometry and HPLC, and peptide purity, and did not test assay, impurities, bacterial endotoxins or aggregates [1]. For the acetate, the supplied certificate was more thorough, covering appearance, solubility, amino acid composition, water, acetate content, impurity limits of 2.0% total and 1.0% for the largest single impurity, and purity, but it did not report identification, assay, aggregates or bacterial endotoxins [1] (verified).","As with the other peptides in this review round, the impurity limits leave impurities of unknown identity, and the agency's concern is that unidentified impurities and aggregation can drive immune responses to an injected peptide. It judged injectable routes to pose a significant immunogenicity risk on that basis, with no immunogenicity studies available either way [1] (verified). Both forms were concluded to be not physically and chemically well characterised.","Storage is cold: below minus 20 degrees Celsius for the free base, below minus 15 for the acetate [1]."]},{"h":"Mechanism, and which molecule each step was shown with","where":"mixed","body":["The actin story belongs to the full protein. Holding actin monomers in reserve is what sets how fast a cell can rearrange its own scaffolding and crawl, which is the common root of the wound, cornea and heart findings. Everything downstream of that is a consequence of cells being able to move and survive, not of a receptor being switched on.","The heart survival step came from a 2004 paper in Nature working in mouse cardiac tissue and cells, which reported that the full protein activates integrin-linked kinase and promotes cardiac cell migration and survival after injury [2] (verified, animals and cells). This is the most cited mechanistic result in the field and it was done with the 43-residue protein.","Here is where the fragment's own record gets uncomfortable. The wound-healing data most often invoked for TB-500 came from a 2003 study using the non-acetylated seven-residue sequence applied topically to aged mice. The agency's position is that this cannot be extrapolated to the acetylated injected fragment, because it differs in both the molecule and the route [1] (verified).","And when the fragment itself was tested directly, in a 2024 in vitro fibroblast scratch assay, TB-500 free base at 50 micrograms per millilitre did not significantly promote wound closure. A metabolite, the N-acetylated five-residue fragment, showed a small but statistically significant effect at the same concentration [1] (verified, cells). A negative result in a simple assay at a high concentration is not the end of the question, but it is the only direct test of the marketed fragment's core claim, and it did not support it.","Pharmacokinetics for the fragment amount to one animal study in a different species: horses given 10 mg subcutaneously reached plasma peaks of 0.05 and 0.08 nanograms per millilitre [1]. There is no intramuscular pharmacokinetic data, and none in humans, for either the fragment or by any route [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The human record for the full protein","where":"people","body":["Systemic human exposure exists, and it was well tolerated. A Phase 1 programme on an injectable formulation of the full protein ran a double-blind, placebo-controlled single ascending intravenous dose stage in 40 healthy volunteers and a 14-day dosing stage in a further 40, reporting no adverse events judged related to the drug [3] (verified). Doses and pharmacokinetics were not reported in the public announcement, which is a real limitation when that study is cited as the safety basis for injected use.","A separate first-in-human randomised, double-blind single- and multiple-dose Phase 1 study of recombinant human thymosin beta-4 in healthy volunteers has been published [4] (reported). Together these establish that intravenous dosing of the full protein has been tolerated in small, short, monitored settings. They do not establish anything about subcutaneous dosing of a seven-residue fragment over weeks, which is the actual pattern of use.","For the fragment, the agency found no clinical studies, no human exposure data and no human pharmacokinetic data by any route [1]. Its adverse event database returned nothing, and a separate complaint system returned two cases involving a blended fragment-and-BPC-157 product with no safety assessment attached [1] (verified). There is no established human dose for the fragment, and no study from which one could be derived.","The eye programme is where the full protein has been tested properly, and it is the most informative part of the record precisely because it is rigorous. The third pivotal trial in neurotrophic keratitis completed its last patient visit in August 2026 and entered data cleaning and analysis, run across 50 sites, 41 in the United States and 9 across Italy, Spain and Poland, with topline results aimed for November 2026 [5,6] (reported). Given that the earlier pivotal trials in this programme did not deliver a clear win, this readout is the single most consequential pending result for the molecule, in either form."]},{"h":"Safety: an absence rather than a finding","where":"lab","body":["For the fragment, the toxicology section of the record is empty. No acute toxicity studies, no repeat-dose studies, no genotoxicity studies, no developmental or reproductive studies and no carcinogenicity studies were submitted or identified for either the free base or the acetate [1] (verified).","That is a different situation from a compound that has been tested and raised concerns. It means the ordinary questions, what dose causes harm, what organ is affected first, whether it damages DNA, whether it affects pregnancy, whether it causes tumours, have not been asked of this molecule at all. The agency noted the absence of carcinogenicity data without drawing a conclusion from it [1] (verified).","The widely repeated line that this peptide has an excellent safety profile with no reported side effects is, read against the record, a statement about the absence of studies and of surveillance reports rather than about the presence of safety data. Both readings produce the same sentence; only one of them is informative.","One consideration specific to the biology is worth stating carefully. A molecule whose job is to make cells migrate and survive is, in principle, acting on capabilities that matter in tumour spread as well as in repair. No study has examined this for either form (speculated). It is an argument from mechanism, not an observation."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["The agency proposed not adding either the free base or the acetate to the 503A compounding bulks list. Its reasons were, in order: neither substance is well characterised; there is no history of use in compounding; there is no evidence of effectiveness for wound healing; there is no human safety data; injectable routes carry immunogenicity risk; and approved wound therapies already exist [1] (verified).","The advisory committee recommended adding it anyway, voting 8 to 6 with one abstention on 23 July 2026, for wound healing [7,8]. The same committee recommended BPC-157 and KPV by the same margin, against the same staff opposition [8,9].","That recommendation is advisory and changes nothing by itself. Adding the substance requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, which has not begun and carries no deadline [7] (verified). The interim path under consideration is an \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\", sent to the White House budget office for review on 14 October 2026 and unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [7] (verified). It would be enforcement discretion rather than approval, and could be withdrawn without rulemaking.","In sport, injectable use of either form is prohibited under the peptide hormones and growth factors class. That is a straightforward position and has not changed."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the acetylated seven-residue fragment do anything? The one direct test of it against its own nominated indication was negative, and the positive wound data belongs to a different molecule applied a different way. This is the field's central unexamined assumption.","If the fragment works through a metabolite, as the 2024 assay faintly suggested, then dose, timing and route logic all change, and none of the existing conventions were built on that premise.","What is in the vial, the fragment or the full protein? There is no routine identity testing that distinguishes them in grey-market supply, and the two are regulated and evidenced separately.","Does the full protein work in the eye? The November 2026 readout will largely answer this, and it is the only well-powered test of the molecule in humans anywhere.","Does intravenous tolerability in 80 monitored volunteers over 14 days say anything about repeated subcutaneous dosing over months? Nothing in the record connects the two.","Everything about long-term safety, in both forms. There is no toxicology at all for the fragment, which makes this less an open question than an unopened one."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Bock-Marquette et al., Nature, thymosin beta-4 activates integrin-linked kinase","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/nature03000","year":2004},{"n":3,"t":"RegeneRx, final Phase 1 clinical results for injectable thymosin beta-4","url":"https://www.biospace.com/regenerx-biopharmaceuticals-inc-announces-final-clinical-results-from-phase-i-trial-with-rgn-352","year":2009},{"n":4,"t":"First-in-human Phase 1 of recombinant human thymosin beta-4 in healthy volunteers","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8419156/","year":2021},{"n":5,"t":"ClinicalTrials.gov NCT05555589, Phase 3 eye drop trial in neurotrophic keratitis","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05555589","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Financial News, last patient visit completed in the Phase 3 eye programme","url":"https://en.fnnews.com/news/202608261308363456","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026}]},"semaglutide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What the cardiovascular trial actually measured","where":"people","body":["This is the trial that changed the drug's standing, so it is worth knowing its shape rather than its headline. It enrolled 17,604 adults across 41 countries, aged 45 or older, with a body mass index of 27 or above, with established cardiovascular disease and no history of diabetes. They received 2.4 mg once weekly by injection or placebo, added on top of standard care, for up to five years [1] (verified).","The endpoint was a composite: first occurrence of cardiovascular death, non-fatal heart attack or non-fatal stroke. There were 1,270 first events across the trial, and the drug was associated with a statistically significant 20% reduction, with all three components of the composite contributing to the effect [1] (verified).","Two things follow that are often lost. First, a composite endpoint with all components moving in the same direction is a stronger result than a composite carried by one soft component, so this is a genuinely robust finding. Second, the comparison was against placebo on top of standard cardiovascular care, in people who already had heart disease. It is not a result about healthy people, and it was not designed to be.","The population also explains the most common misreading. This was a trial in people with obesity or overweight and established cardiovascular disease, deliberately excluding diabetes, because the question was whether the benefit existed outside diabetes. It did. That does not extend to people without cardiovascular disease, in whom the absolute benefit would be much smaller even if the relative effect were identical (inferred)."]},{"h":"The gap between the trials and how it is actually used","where":"people","body":["The trial programme tested a specific thing: a stepped titration to a defined maintenance dose, taken continuously, in defined populations, with weight or cardiovascular events as the measured outcome. A large share of current use does not resemble that.","Sub-label dosing for appetite control, metabolic tidiness or longevity has no trial behind it. There is no study measuring outcomes at a fraction of the maintenance dose, no study of intermittent or every-few-weeks schedules, and no study in people without obesity or diabetes. The absence is not a hint that it does not work; it is simply unmeasured territory, and the honest label for it is unstudied rather than reported (verified as absent).","The long half-life, around a week, has a consequence that matters more at the edges of use than in the middle. Changes take weeks to express fully and weeks to wash out, so self-adjusting dose on the basis of how a given week felt is working with a signal that lags the input substantially (inferred). The trials sidestepped this by using fixed four-week titration steps.","The ceiling moved in March 2026, when a higher-dose 7.2 mg weekly version was approved for obesity in the US [2] (verified). Worth noting the direction of travel: the approved range has been extending upward on trial evidence, while a good deal of community practice has been moving downward on no evidence at all. Both trends are real and they are not in conversation with each other."]},{"h":"Product reality: where this one differs from every other peptide here","where":"people","body":["Unlike the repair peptides, an approved, characterised version of this molecule exists. That makes the grey-market and compounded supply a different kind of problem: not an unstudied substance, but a studied substance of uncertain identity, concentration and sterility, used without the labelling and titration support the evidence was generated with.","The documented failure mode is dosing error, and it is specific. Regulators described reports of patients receiving 5 to 20 times the intended dose from multi-dose vials; clinicians miscalculating when converting milligrams to units or millilitres, including one prescription written for 20 units instead of 2, which delivered ten times the intended dose to three patients; a patient who received no clear instructions from a telemedicine prescriber, searched online and took five times the intended dose; a clinician who recalculated his own dose and gave himself ten times the intended amount; and patients supplied with syringes far larger than the prescribed volume [3] (verified). Reported consequences included gastrointestinal effects, fainting, dehydration, headache, gallstones and acute pancreatitis, with some hospitalisations [3].","That list is worth reading closely because none of those events are pharmacology. They are unit-conversion and packaging failures, and they are the predictable result of separating a titrated microgram-scale injectable from the pen that doses it.","A 2025 analysis covering 2018 to 2024 linked compounded versions of this drug class to a higher rate of adverse effects than the non-compounded products, and it was cited in the US advisory debate on peptide compounding as a caution about pharmacy standards [4] (reported). Enforcement has followed the same line: warning letters went to online peptide vendors in August 2026, and to a compounder over its products in this class and its telehealth prescribing practices in September 2026 [5] (verified).","The practical reading for anyone comparing sources: with this molecule, the concentration stated on a vial and the volume in a syringe are where the risk lives, far more than the molecule itself."]},{"h":"The generic wave, and why it changes the picture","where":"people","body":["The patent position began unwinding in several large markets during 2026, and this is the development most likely to reshape grey-market demand.","In India, a generic launched around March 2026. In the US, a first tentative generic approval was granted in April 2026. In Canada, generics of the diabetes product were approved on 29 April 2026 and launched in mid-May, Apotex on 14 May and a second company two days later, both as pre-filled pens for type 2 diabetes in adults, making Canada the first G7 market with a generic version on pharmacy shelves. In China, a marketing application for a generic of the obesity product was accepted for review in February 2026. Health Canada was reviewing at least four further submissions [6] (verified).","Why an encyclopedia entry should care: the main driver of grey-market use of this molecule has been price and access, not the absence of an approved option. Characterised, pen-delivered generics at generic prices remove much of that rationale in the markets where they land. The dosing-error failure mode described above is almost entirely a property of vial-and-syringe supply, and it largely disappears with a pen (inferred).","The uneven part is that approvals so far are mostly for the diabetes indication and the diabetes dose range, not the obesity product, so access for weight management remains patchier than the headlines imply [6] (verified)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What happens below the tested dose range? Sub-label dosing is now widespread and has never been measured for any outcome, including the appetite effect people are actually chasing.","Who benefits cardiovascularly outside the trial population? The 20% relative reduction was measured in people with established heart disease. The absolute benefit in lower-risk people is unknown and would necessarily be smaller.","What does very long-term use look like? The pivotal cardiovascular trial ran up to five years, which is long by trial standards and short relative to a treatment many people expect to continue indefinitely.","Does the muscle-loss question have a dose-dependent answer? It is well recognised as a risk and poorly quantified across the dose range, and it interacts with the sub-label dosing question above.","Will generic availability actually displace grey-market vials, or will price simply fall on both sides? The dosing-error evidence suggests the public-health answer hinges on delivery format rather than on the molecule.","For compounded and grey-market supply specifically: what is the real distribution of concentration accuracy? The adverse-event signal points at it, and unlike most questions on this page, it is answerable by testing rather than by trials."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"American College of Cardiology, SELECT trial summary, semaglutide and major adverse cardiovascular events","url":"https://www.acc.org/Latest-in-Cardiology/Articles/2023/08/10/14/29/SELECT-Semaglutide-Reduces-Risk-of-MACE-in-Adults-With-Overweight-or-Obesity","year":2023},{"n":2,"t":"Drug Topics, FDA approves higher-dose semaglutide for weight loss","url":"https://drugtopics.com/view/fda-approves-new-higher-dose-of-semaglutide-wegovy-hd-for-weight-loss","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"The Hospitalist, compounded semaglutide overdoses tied to hospitalizations, reporting the FDA dosing-error alert","url":"https://blogs.the-hospitalist.org/content/compounded-semaglutide-overdoses-tied-hospitalizations","year":2024},{"n":4,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides, including compounded GLP-1 safety analysis cited in debate","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions and enforcement","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Pearce IP, generic semaglutide approvals and launches across markets","url":"https://www.pearceip.law/2026/05/14/apotex-dr-reddys-launch-generic-semaglutide-in-canada/","year":2026}]},"tirzepatide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The head-to-head trial, because this is the only pair in the class directly compared","where":"people","body":["Most drug comparisons are inferred by lining up separate trials, which is unreliable because populations and designs differ. Here there is a real head-to-head, and it is the most useful single piece of evidence about this compound.","SURMOUNT-5 randomised 751 adults one-to-one, in the US and Puerto Rico, to maximum tolerated doses of either drug, for 72 weeks, with both groups receiving diet and activity counselling. Participants had obesity, or overweight with at least one of hypertension, dyslipidaemia, obstructive sleep apnoea or cardiovascular disease, and no diabetes [1] (verified).","Average weight loss at 72 weeks was 20.2% against 13.7%, a 47% greater relative reduction [1]. The threshold figures matter more for a decision: at least 20% of body weight lost by 48.4% against 27.3%, and at least 25% lost by 31.6% against 16.1% [1] (verified). Waist circumference fell 18.4 centimetres against 13.0.","Two design caveats belong with those numbers. The trial was open-label, meaning everyone knew which drug they were taking, which can influence reported outcomes and adherence. And it was not powered to compare safety and tolerability, so the discontinuation figures, 6.1% against 8.0%, are descriptive rather than a finding [1] (verified).","The honest summary: on weight, superiority is established in a direct comparison. On everything else, the trial was not built to adjudicate."]},{"h":"What the sleep apnoea trials measured, and why it was a stronger result than the weight data","where":"people","body":["The sleep apnoea programme deserves attention because it used a hard, machine-measured endpoint rather than a scale reading.","Two parallel 52-week randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials enrolled 469 adults with moderate-to-severe obstructive sleep apnoea and obesity: one in people unable or unwilling to use positive airway pressure therapy, the other in people already using it [2] (verified).","The apnoea-hypopnoea index, the count of breathing interruptions per hour, fell by 27.4 events per hour against 4.8 on placebo in the first trial, and by 30.4 against 6.0 in the second [2]. Weight loss was about 18% and 20% respectively, and systolic blood pressure fell 9.6 and 7.6 points [2] (verified).","That is a large effect on an objective measure, in both the treated and untreated-by-device populations, and it is the basis for the sleep indication. It also illustrates something general about this drug class: the downstream benefits of substantial weight loss are often easier to demonstrate cleanly than weight loss itself, because they are measured by instruments rather than reported."]},{"h":"The cardiovascular trial, and the result people get backwards","where":"people","body":["This is the most misreported trial in the compound's record, so the design matters.","SURPASS-CVOT randomised 13,165 adults with type 2 diabetes and established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, 6,586 to this drug titrated up to 15 mg weekly and 6,579 to dulaglutide 1.5 mg weekly, with median follow-up around four years [3] (verified). Mean age 64, mean HbA1c 8%, mean diabetes duration 15 years.","Critically, the comparator was not placebo. It was another drug in the same family already shown to reduce cardiovascular events. The trial was designed to test non-inferiority, with a margin of 1.05 [3].","The composite of cardiovascular death, heart attack or stroke occurred in 12% against 13%, hazard ratio 0.92. Non-inferiority was met. Superiority was not: the superiority test did not reach significance [3] (verified).","So the correct reading is that this drug is at least as protective as an established comparator, in a population already on good cardiovascular care, and was not shown to be better. Headlines describing it as beating the comparator on cardiovascular protection overstate a non-significant trend. Headlines describing it as failing a cardiovascular trial understate a met non-inferiority endpoint against an active drug, which is a harder bar than placebo (verified). The authors noted the absence of a placebo group, limited global diversity and some between-group imbalances as limitations [3]."]},{"h":"Where the approved product and the catalog vial diverge","where":"people","body":["This molecule has been tested about as thoroughly as a metabolic drug can be: diabetes and obesity programmes, a sleep apnoea programme, a four-year cardiovascular outcome trial in over 13,000 people, and a direct head-to-head [1,2,3]. The evidence question is largely settled. The supply question is entirely separate and untouched by any of it.","The documented failure mode across this drug class in non-pen supply is dosing error rather than pharmacology. Regulators described reports of patients receiving five to twenty times the intended dose from multi-dose vials, clinicians miscalculating conversions between milligrams, units and millilitres, and patients supplied with syringes far larger than the prescribed volume, with consequences including dehydration, gallstones and acute pancreatitis [4] (verified). None of those events are properties of the drug.","Enforcement has tracked the same problem: warning letters went to online peptide vendors in August 2026 and to a compounder over products in this class and its telehealth prescribing practices in September 2026 [5] (verified).","The useful distinction for anyone comparing sources: with this compound the molecule is well characterised and the container is not. That is the inverse of the situation for the repair peptides in this catalog, and it calls for a different kind of caution (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the cardiovascular benefit extend beyond diabetes with established disease? The four-year trial was run in that specific population against an active comparator, and says nothing directly about lower-risk people.","Would a placebo-controlled cardiovascular trial have shown superiority? Non-inferiority against an effective drug leaves the absolute size of the benefit unquantified.","How much of the sleep apnoea effect is weight loss and how much is anything else? The trials were not designed to separate those, and the distinction matters for whether the benefit persists if weight returns.","What does the head-to-head weight advantage mean for an individual? A 6.5 percentage point average difference in an open-label trial does not predict which drug suits a given person, and tolerability was not formally compared.","What happens to body composition at 20% or more weight loss, and does resistance training change the answer? Widely discussed, poorly quantified across doses.","For grey-market and compounded supply specifically: what is the real distribution of concentration accuracy? The adverse-event pattern points squarely at this, and it is answerable by testing rather than by trials."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Eli Lilly, SURMOUNT-5 complete results, head-to-head against semaglutide, published in NEJM","url":"https://www.drugs.com/clinical_trials/zepbound-tirzepatide-showed-superior-weight-loss-over-wegovy-semaglutide-complete-surmount-5-22029.html","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"American Diabetes Association, SURMOUNT-OSA results in obstructive sleep apnoea","url":"https://diabetes.org/sites/default/files/2024-06/ADA_SURMOUNT-OSAPressRelease_Final.pdf","year":2024},{"n":3,"t":"American College of Cardiology journal scan, SURPASS-CVOT (Nicholls et al., NEJM 2025)","url":"https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2026/01/07/14/20/surpass-cvot","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"The Hospitalist, compounded GLP-1 dosing errors and hospitalisations, reporting the FDA alert","url":"https://blogs.the-hospitalist.org/content/compounded-semaglutide-overdoses-tied-hospitalizations","year":2024},{"n":5,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions and enforcement","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"mots-c":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Why the discovery was interesting, independent of whether the compound works","where":"lab","body":["The 2015 paper that introduced this peptide made a claim about biology that was more startling than any claim about therapy: that the mitochondrial genome, long treated as a small set of genes for respiratory-chain components, also encodes short signalling peptides that act on the rest of the cell [1] (verified).","That reframes the mitochondrion from a power plant taking orders to a compartment that sends messages outward. The idea has held up well enough to generate a field, and it is the main reason this molecule attracts serious researchers rather than only enthusiasts.","The mechanism proposed in that paper is worth stating precisely, because the popular version compresses it into something simpler. The peptide was proposed to act on the folate-dependent one-carbon pool and to inhibit the de novo purine synthesis pathway tethered to it. Blocking that pathway causes an intermediate to accumulate, and that intermediate is itself a potent activator of the cell's main energy sensor. So the energy-sensor activation is proposed to be indirect, a downstream consequence of a metabolic bottleneck, not a direct binding event [1] (verified as the paper's proposed model).","The 2015 paper presents this as a proposed model in its own figure legend [1]. It is a hypothesis with supporting data, not a settled pathway, and no receptor has been identified."]},{"h":"Why a short peptide encoded inside a ribosomal RNA gene is contested","where":"lab","body":["There is a genuine scientific argument here that rarely surfaces in consumer material, and it is worth understanding because it bears on how seriously to take the biology.","The reading frame sits inside a gene whose product is a structural RNA, in a genome that uses a different genetic code from the nucleus and has its own translation machinery. For the peptide to be a real signalling molecule, a short open reading frame inside that RNA gene has to be translated, and the product has to leave the organelle and act elsewhere. Each of those steps is plausible and each has been questioned (verified that the mechanism is proposed rather than established).","This is not a reason to dismiss the molecule. Synthetic peptide given to an animal can have effects whether or not the body makes it the same way. It is a reason to treat the framing of this compound as a natural hormone that declines with age as the least secure part of the story, because that framing depends on the endogenous biology being exactly as proposed (inferred).","The observed associations, that circulating levels rise with exercise and fall with age, are correlations in human samples. They do not establish that supplying the peptide reproduces what exercise does, and the gap between an exercise-associated molecule and an exercise substitute is the whole question (inferred)."]},{"h":"What was actually measured in animals","where":"animals","body":["The animal work targets skeletal muscle and metabolic handling, and the effects reported are metabolic rather than structural: better glucose handling, resistance to diet-induced obesity, greater running endurance (verified as the literature's consistent findings, in mice).","What is missing from the animal literature is the thing that would make the exercise analogy real. Exercise produces cardiovascular remodelling, mitochondrial biogenesis, bone loading, and effects on mood and sleep. The animal data here speak to glucose and fat handling and to endurance capacity, which is a subset (inferred).","There is also a dose-response and exposure gap shared with most peptides in this catalog. No pharmacokinetic data in any species was identified in the regulatory review, so there is no way to connect an injected amount to a tissue concentration, and the animal doses cannot be translated (verified as absent in the regulatory record [2])."]},{"h":"The human record, and the analogue that was tested instead","where":"people","body":["No completed trial of this peptide itself exists. The closest human evidence belongs to a different molecule: a modified analogue developed by a company, which completed a small early-phase study in fatty liver disease and obesity around 2021 (verified as the card records; details of that programme were not retrievable in this pass).","An analogue is a different compound. A developer modifies a natural sequence precisely because the natural one has properties, usually stability or exposure, that make it unsuitable as a drug. So the existence of an analogue programme is simultaneously evidence that the target interested a drug developer and evidence that the unmodified peptide was not considered the right molecule to develop (inferred).","There is no human efficacy data for the peptide, no human safety dataset, and no established human dose. Every circulating figure derives from community convention or from converting mouse doses (verified as absent).","The reported user experience, energy, drive and improved glucose readings, is the pattern one would expect from a compound acting on the cell's energy sensor, and it is also the pattern one would expect from almost any intervention undertaken deliberately by someone paying attention to their metabolic numbers. Self-reported energy is the least specific endpoint in this entire field (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["In the July 2026 advisory round, agency staff proposed that neither the free base nor the acetate be added to the 503A compounding bulks list, applying the four standard criteria of characterisation, safety, effectiveness and history of compounding use [2] (verified). The original nomination had been withdrawn, and the agency proceeded anyway.","The committee recommended it regardless, by 7 to 5 with two abstentions on 23 July 2026, for obesity and osteoporosis, the indications the agency had reviewed [3] (verified). That margin is narrower and the abstentions more numerous than for the repair peptides considered the same week.","As with the others, the recommendation is advisory and changes nothing by itself. Addition requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, not begun and with no deadline [4] (verified). An interim enforcement-discretion policy covering certain peptides went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances not public [4].","In sport, the position follows from the mechanism rather than from a named listing: metabolic modulators acting on the cell's main energy sensor fall within a prohibited class, so a compound whose entire proposed mechanism is activation of that sensor sits inside it (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is the peptide genuinely translated and secreted in humans, or is the therapeutic effect of synthetic peptide unrelated to an endogenous signalling system? The replacement framing depends entirely on the former.","Is energy-sensor activation really downstream of a purine-synthesis bottleneck? That is the proposed model in the original paper and remains a model.","Does anything reach muscle after injection? With no pharmacokinetic data in any species, this is unanswered for every route in use.","Why did development proceed with a modified analogue rather than the natural sequence? The answer would say a lot about the unmodified peptide's drug-likeness.","Do exercise-associated levels mean anything causal? Falling with age and rising with exercise is consistent with the peptide being a marker of mitochondrial activity rather than a driver of it.","What is the toxicology? None was identified in the regulatory review, so long-term safety is unopened rather than open.","Does it do anything measurable in a human? A small, short, placebo-controlled trial with glucose handling as the endpoint would settle more than another decade of mouse work."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Lee et al., Cell Metabolism, mitochondrial-derived peptide regulating metabolic homeostasis (proposed model, Fig. 7)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4350682/figure/F7","year":2015},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document and voting questions, July 2026 session including MOTS-c","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193342/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"ss-31":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What the approval was actually based on, which is not what it sounds like","where":"people","body":["This is the most important thing to understand about this compound, and the summary version gets it backwards.","The pivotal trial, TAZPOWER, enrolled 12 males with genetically confirmed Barth syndrome in a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover design: six to drug and six to placebo for 12 weeks, a four-week washout, then crossover [1] (verified). The primary endpoints were six-minute walk distance and a total fatigue score.","In that randomised phase, there was no statistically significant difference in walk distance, no difference in fatigue score, and no improvement in knee extensor strength [1] (verified). The controlled portion of the pivotal trial missed everything it set out to measure.","The approval rests on what came afterward: a 168-week open-label extension, in which all participants received drug. Knee extensor strength first appeared to improve at week 36 but was not sustained. By week 168, all subjects showed significant improvement, with a median change of 63 newtons [1] (verified). Accelerated approval followed in September 2025 for improving muscle strength in patients weighing at least 30 kilograms, at 40 mg subcutaneously once daily, with a required post-approval randomised placebo-controlled confirmatory trial [1].","So the evidence is: twelve people, a failed randomised phase, and a benefit visible only in an uncontrolled extension after more than three years of continuous treatment. The agency accepted that for an ultra-rare fatal disease with no other treatment, which is a defensible regulatory judgement. It is not a demonstration that the molecule reliably improves muscle strength (verified)."]},{"h":"Why an open-label extension after three years is weak evidence, and why it was accepted anyway","where":"people","body":["In an open-label extension everyone knows they are on drug, there is no comparison group, and the people still enrolled at week 168 are the ones who chose to stay. Each of those independently biases toward an apparent benefit. Natural history also runs in the background: a measure taken repeatedly over three years in a dozen young people will move for reasons unrelated to treatment (inferred).","The strength signal's own shape is a caution. It appeared at week 36, then was not sustained, then was present at week 168 [1]. A genuine pharmacological effect usually does not vanish and return; a noisy measure in a tiny sample does exactly that.","What makes the approval reasonable despite all that is the disease. Barth syndrome is a rare genetic disorder of cardiolipin, the specific phospholipid this molecule binds, and there was no approved treatment. Accelerated approval exists for precisely this situation: accept weaker evidence of a surrogate benefit, require a proper trial afterwards. The confirmatory trial is mandated and has not reported [1] (verified).","The practical consequence for anyone reading the approval as validation of the molecule's broader promise: it is the narrowest possible approval, in the one disease where the target is the known defect, on the weakest evidence the pathway permits."]},{"h":"The broader programme, and what it failed to show","where":"people","body":["This molecule has been tested in considerably more than its approved indication, and the pattern of those results is the most informative thing about it.","A Phase 3 trial in primary mitochondrial myopathy missed its main endpoint. Heart failure work produced mixed results. Dry age-related macular degeneration has been trialled (verified, as the card records). Extensive animal work shows protection of heart, kidney and muscle mitochondria.","That shape, strong and consistent preclinical protection followed by a failed Phase 3 in the most obvious human indication, is one of the most common patterns in drug development and usually means one of three things: the animal models do not capture the human disease, the exposure achieved in people is insufficient at the target, or the endpoint cannot detect the effect size present (inferred). None of those has been established here.","The mechanism remains the compound's strongest asset. Binding cardiolipin in the inner mitochondrial membrane is a specific, structurally grounded proposal, and it explains why Barth syndrome, a disease of defective cardiolipin, is the clearest target. It also implies the opposite of a general-purpose benefit: the clearer the mechanism, the narrower the population in whom it should matter (inferred)."]},{"h":"What the label says about handling the drug, and why that is relevant outside the label","where":"people","body":["Because an approved version exists, there is a characterised safety and handling profile here that no other unapproved peptide in this catalog has. It is worth reading for what it reveals.","Dose is 40 mg subcutaneously once daily, with a reduction to 20 mg daily in severe renal impairment, meaning estimated filtration below 30 and not on dialysis [1] (verified). That renal adjustment tells you the compound is cleared by the kidneys and that exposure rises when they are impaired.","The most common adverse events are injection-site reactions, specifically redness, induration, itching and pain. In the open-label extension, 2 of 10 participants discontinued because of injection-site reactions [1] (verified). For a daily subcutaneous injection, that is a meaningful discontinuation driver rather than a nuisance.","Warnings include hypersensitivity reactions, including serious allergic reactions. The formulation is not approved for neonates because it contains benzyl alcohol [1] (verified).","The label also makes an unusual statement about time course: measurable benefit was observed only after more than three years of treatment, and long-term use is needed [1]. That is a direct consequence of the evidence base described above, and it is irreconcilable with the way the compound is discussed as a short-course energy intervention (inferred).","Grey-market material is not the approved product, and the approved product's characterisation does not transfer to it (verified)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the confirmatory trial replicate the strength benefit? That trial is required and has not reported, and it is the single result that would convert an accelerated approval into an established one.","Why did the randomised phase fail while the extension succeeded? Sample size, duration, measure choice and open-label bias are all candidates, and distinguishing them matters for every other indication.","Why did the large mitochondrial myopathy Phase 3 miss? Without an answer, there is no rational basis for expecting benefit in any other mitochondrial condition.","Does the mechanism predict benefit outside cardiolipin-defect disease? Binding cardiolipin is specific, which argues for a narrow population rather than a broad one.","What exposure does the approved daily dose achieve at the inner mitochondrial membrane, and is that the step the failed trials fell at? Not publicly established.","Does anything in the approval support the energy and recovery uses it is bought for? Nothing in the record addresses healthy people, short courses, or any outcome resembling those claims.","What happens over years in people without the disease? The only long-duration human data comes from twelve patients with a specific genetic defect."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Clinician.com, elamipretide injection approval, TAZPOWER trial detail and prescribing information","url":"https://www.clinician.com/articles/elamipretide-injection-forzinity","year":2025}]},"tesamorelin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What the pivotal trial measured, with the numbers that matter","where":"people","body":["This is the best-evidenced compound in the growth-hormone-releasing family, so the pivotal trial is worth reading in detail rather than by its conclusion.","It enrolled 412 people with HIV and abdominal fat accumulation across 43 sites, 86% men, randomised two-to-one to 2 mg daily subcutaneously or placebo for 26 weeks [1] (verified).","Visceral adipose tissue, measured by imaging rather than reported, fell 15.2% on drug while rising 5.0% on placebo [1]. Insulin-like growth factor 1 rose 81%, about 109 nanograms per millilitre, against a 5% fall on placebo. Triglycerides fell 50 milligrams per decilitre against a 9 milligram rise [1] (verified).","The measure to notice is the first one. Visceral fat assessed by imaging is an objective endpoint, and a 20 percentage point separation from placebo on it is a solid result. This is the reason the compound carries more credibility than the rest of its family: the effect was demonstrated on an instrument, not a questionnaire.","Glucose is the honest complication. There were no significant differences between groups in fasting glucose, two-hour glucose or insulin in this trial [1] (verified). That is a better result than the mechanism predicts, and it sits alongside the label's requirement to monitor glucose. The resolution is that growth hormone raises glucose reliably enough to warrant monitoring, while a 26-week trial in this population did not detect a group difference (inferred)."]},{"h":"The immunogenicity finding nobody quotes","where":"people","body":["Antibodies to the drug appeared in 48.6% of treated participants against 2.7% on placebo, and six patients developed urticaria [1] (verified).","Nearly half of treated people mounting an antibody response is a striking number, and it is the single most useful data point in this catalog for thinking about injected peptides generally. Regulators repeatedly flag immunogenicity as an unquantified risk for unapproved injected peptides, noting that aggregates and unidentified impurities can drive immune responses and that no studies exist to settle it. Here is a well-characterised, properly manufactured, approved 44-residue peptide, studied in a controlled trial, and the answer was: about half (verified).","That reframes the concern. The question for unapproved peptides is not whether an injected peptide can provoke an antibody response. It evidently can, even when the product is clean. The open question is what those antibodies do, and for this compound the trial found no loss of effect attributable to them, which is reassuring for it specifically and says nothing about any other molecule (inferred).","Discontinuation for adverse events was 12.1% on drug against 2.9% on placebo, and treatment-related adverse events ran 53.8% against 36.5% [1] (verified). Overall and serious adverse event rates did not differ significantly. So the tolerability cost is real and mostly non-serious."]},{"h":"Where it has been tested beyond its approval, and what those trials found","where":"people","body":["Two further randomised trials are more interesting than the approval, because they tested whether the visceral-fat mechanism translates into organ-level benefit.","A liver trial randomised 61 people with HIV and a hepatic fat fraction above 5% to 2 mg daily or placebo for 12 months, followed by a six-month open-label phase [2] (verified). Liver fat fell by 4.1 percentage points more than placebo, a 37% relative reduction. Steatosis resolved, meaning fat fraction fell below 5%, in 35% of treated participants against 4% on placebo. Fibrosis progressed in 2 treated participants against 9 on placebo [2] (verified).","That fibrosis result is the most clinically meaningful number associated with this compound. Fibrosis progression is what turns fatty liver into liver disease, and a reduction in it is a harder outcome than a fat percentage. The trial was small and the comparison was a secondary one, so it is a strong signal rather than a settled finding (inferred).","The liver trial also found no effect on fasting glucose, no overall effect on ALT or lipids, with ALT falling only in those whose levels were elevated at baseline, and a significant reduction in C-reactive protein [2] (verified).","A separate randomised trial in older adults reported better memory performance alongside a rise in brain GABA levels (reported, as the card records). That result is small, single and has not been replicated, and cognition trials in ageing have a poor record of replication generally (inferred)."]},{"h":"Why the mechanism predicts the shape of the benefit, including its limits","where":"mixed","body":["The compound does not supply growth hormone. It stimulates the pituitary to release it in pulses, which leaves the body's feedback control intact. That design decision explains most of what follows.","Pulsatile release under intact feedback is why this compound does not produce the acromegalic effects of supraphysiological growth hormone, and why its effects are modest rather than dramatic. The ceiling is set by how much the pituitary will release, not by how much is injected (inferred).","It also explains the tissue selectivity. Visceral fat is unusually growth-hormone-sensitive and metabolically active, which is why it responds while other fat depots respond less, and why liver fat falls by the same route [1,2] (inferred from the mechanism and consistent with both trials).","The same logic sets the limits. An intact pituitary is required, which is why the label contraindicates pituitary tumour, pituitary surgery and brain radiation. Raised growth hormone signalling means raised insulin-like growth factor 1, which is why active malignancy is contraindicated. And growth hormone opposes insulin, which is why glucose monitoring is required (verified via the label positions the card records).","Visceral fat returns after stopping, which the mechanism also predicts: nothing about pulsed stimulation changes the set point that produced the fat in the first place (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the fibrosis result replicate? Two progressions against nine in a 61-person trial is the most clinically important number here and the one most in need of a larger confirmation.","Does any of this transfer outside HIV-associated fat accumulation? Both randomised trials were run in that population, which has a specific pattern of fat distribution and metabolic disturbance.","What happens to glucose over years rather than months? Two trials found no group difference while the label requires monitoring, which means the question is unresolved rather than answered.","What do the antibodies do over long-term use? Nearly half of treated participants developed them in 26 weeks, with no detected loss of effect in that window.","Is the cognition signal real? One small randomised trial, unreplicated, in a field where unreplicated cognition results are the norm.","What is the right comparison for off-label use? The approved evidence is for a daily injection in a specific disease, and nothing in the record addresses intermittent use in healthy people for body composition.","Does the visceral-fat benefit matter if it reverses on stopping? That is a question about indefinite treatment, and no trial has run long enough to answer it."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Falutz et al., NEJM, metabolic effects of a growth hormone-releasing factor in HIV (Phase 3)","url":"https://natap.org/2007/HIV/121307_03.htm","year":2007},{"n":2,"t":"Stanley et al., Lancet HIV, effects on non-alcoholic fatty liver disease in HIV","url":"https://i-base.info/htb/36999","year":2019}]},"kisspeptin-10":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The human study of this exact fragment, which is better than the field usually gets","where":"people","body":["Unlike most compounds in this catalog, the ten-residue fragment itself has been given to humans in a properly conducted study, with hormone measurements rather than self-report. A 2011 study in a major endocrine journal tested three protocols in healthy men: an intravenous bolus dose-response in 6 participants, a high-dose infusion in 4, and a low-dose infusion in 4 [1] (verified).","Bolus doses spanned 0.01 to 3.0 micrograms per kilogram. Luteinising hormone rose dose-dependently and peaked at 1 microgram per kilogram, from 4.1 to 12.4 international units per litre at 30 minutes [1]. Testosterone did not change significantly after any bolus dose [1] (verified).","The infusions did raise testosterone. The high-dose infusion, 4 micrograms per kilogram per hour for 22.5 hours, raised luteinising hormone from about 5.5 to about 21 units per litre and testosterone from 16.6 to 24.0 nanomoles per litre [1]. The lower infusion, 1.5 micrograms per kilogram per hour for 9 hours, raised luteinising hormone from 5.2 to 14.1 units per litre and increased pulse frequency from 0.7 to 1.0 pulses per hour, with pulse mass rising from 3.9 to 12.8 [1] (verified).","The pattern is informative. A single injection moves the pituitary hormone and does not move testosterone; sustained exposure moves both. Testosterone is downstream of accumulated pulses, so a brief spike in the signal above it does not translate (inferred, consistent with the measured data).","No adverse effects were reported, and blood pressure, heart rate, oxygen saturation and blood, kidney and liver tests remained stable [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The dose-response is not monotonic, and that is the most useful finding","where":"people","body":["At 3 micrograms per kilogram, the highest bolus tested, the luteinising hormone response was not significantly different from vehicle, and was lower than the responses at 0.3 and 1 micrograms per kilogram [1] (verified).","So more produced less. The peak response sat in the middle of the tested range and fell away above it. For a compound bought and used on the assumption that a larger amount gives a larger hormonal push, this is the single most practically relevant result in the human literature, and it points the opposite way.","Receptor-level explanations are available, chiefly that this receptor family internalises and desensitises on strong stimulation, but the study did not establish the cause (inferred).","It also complicates the common framing that this compound works upstream so the response stays physiological and self-limiting. The response is indeed self-limiting. One consequence of that is that overshooting the dose does not produce a bigger effect, it produces a smaller one, which is a different and less comfortable kind of self-limitation (inferred)."]},{"h":"Desensitisation: what the human data showed, and where the worry comes from","where":"mixed","body":["The concern that continuous exposure shuts the axis down is reasonable from receptor biology and is widely repeated. The human data on it is more specific and more reassuring than the general claim.","Across the 22.5-hour high-dose infusion, no tachyphylaxis was observed. Luteinising hormone in the final 90 minutes, 23.9 units per litre, was not lower than in the first 90 minutes, 16.3 units per litre [1] (verified). The authors noted that primate studies had shown tachyphylaxis and suggested the difference was dose: the primate work used higher amounts [1].","So the accurate statement is that sustained exposure for roughly a day at these amounts did not desensitise the axis in healthy men, while higher exposures in primates did. Both the worry and the reassurance are dose-dependent, and nobody has tested exposures running weeks (verified as absent).","That bounds the question usefully. The risk is not that any continuous exposure collapses the axis; it is that enough exposure does, and the threshold in humans is unmapped."]},{"h":"Why the longer form is used in research and the shorter one is sold","where":"animals","body":["Most of the headline human work on this hormone family, the ovulation trigger in fertility treatment and the brain-imaging studies of sexual and emotional processing, used the longer 54-residue form rather than this fragment. There is a specific pharmacological reason, and a 2017 mouse study examined it directly [2].","Circulating half-life is about 32 minutes for the longer form against about 4 minutes for this fragment [2] (verified). But clearance was not the whole explanation: repeating fragment injections every 10 minutes for an hour did not reproduce the sustained hormone rise the longer form produced [2].","The proposed difference is access. After systemic injection in mice, only the longer form activated the immediate-early gene marker in the hormone-releasing neurons sitting behind the blood-brain barrier, and the authors suggest the longer form crosses while the fragment is less able to [2] (verified, in mice).","If that holds in humans, it has a sharp consequence. The target neurons sit behind a barrier, and the fragment may be reaching them poorly or reaching only the subset in regions where the barrier is incomplete. That would explain why the fragment needs sustained infusion to move testosterone while the longer form works more readily, and it means the fertility and desire findings from the longer form should not be transferred to this one (inferred).","It also means the very short half-life is not merely an inconvenience to be solved with more frequent dosing. The mouse data specifically tested that workaround and it did not work [2]."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does this fragment reach the target neurons in humans? The mouse comparison suggests the longer form crosses into the relevant region and this one may not, which would undercut the whole rationale for using the shorter peptide.","What dose is optimal, given that the response peaked mid-range and fell away above it? Nobody has mapped the top of the curve in humans, and the one study that looked found more giving less.","Does intermittent subcutaneous use do anything to testosterone? The human study that moved testosterone used continuous intravenous infusion for hours. A single injection moved the pituitary hormone and not testosterone.","Where is the desensitisation threshold in humans? None appeared over 22.5 hours at the amounts tested; primates desensitised at higher amounts; nothing has been tested over weeks.","Do the desire and fertility findings transfer from the longer form? They were obtained with a different molecule that appears to have different access to the brain.","What happens with repeated courses? There is no human data beyond about a day of continuous exposure, for any endpoint.","Is a short-lived peptide requiring sustained exposure a sensible format at all? The pharmacology suggests the longer form or a stabilised analogue is the better tool, which is what research programmes have mostly used."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"George et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab, kisspeptin-10 stimulates LH and increases pulse frequency in men","url":"https://repository.up.ac.za/bitstreams/98f0beb6-df38-4c10-af7d-b7c84f2af3af/download","year":2011},{"n":2,"t":"de Tassigny et al., PLOS One, mechanistic insights into the more potent effect of KP-54 compared to KP-10 in vivo","url":"https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/entities/publication/30f84436-d438-4959-8dd2-7d67ba0d10cc","year":2017}]},"selank":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and the gap in the story","where":"lab","body":["Selank was designed at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences together with the Zakusov Research Institute of Pharmacology in Moscow [1,2] (verified). The starting point was tuftsin, a four-residue fragment of human immunoglobulin G with immune-stimulating activity. The Pro-Gly-Pro tail was added to slow breakdown and stretch the duration of action [1] (verified).","The part of the story that is never explained in print is the jump from an immune fragment to an anti-anxiety drug. The early clinical claim, that it calms like a tranquiliser without the amnesia, withdrawal or dependence of benzodiazepines, is cited in later papers to Russian reports from 1990 and 1998 rather than shown in an accessible trial [1] (reported). No receptor for the molecule has been identified, so every mechanism below is an observed downstream change, not a binding target."]},{"h":"What the licensed product is, and what circulates instead","where":"lab","body":["The registered Russian medicine is a 0.15% nasal drop: 1.5 mg of peptide per millilitre, preserved with methylparaben, in purified water. The registration holder and manufacturer is a single Moscow company, and the label restricts it to adults, excludes pregnancy and breastfeeding because no studies were done, and runs it as a 14-day course [3] (verified).","What circulates elsewhere is mostly lyophilised powder, often sold as an acetate salt or as modified versions such as an amidated or acetylated form. None of those modified forms is the registered drug, and none has its own published clinical record (inferred). In the US nomination system, a nomination for \"Selank acetate (TP-7)\" was removed from the do-not-compound category after the nominator said it had meant a different substance [8] (verified). That is a small detail with a large meaning: even the people asking regulators to allow it were unsure what they were naming.","Unlike Semax, Selank was not on the July 2026 advisory committee agenda, so no regulator has published a review of its purity, impurities or endotoxin testing [9] (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Enzyme inhibition, in human serum in a test tube. Selank inhibited the enzymes that break down enkephalins with an IC50 of about 20 micromolar, against 10 micromolar for Semax; its pentapeptide fragment also worked, shorter fragments did not [4] (verified). The catch is concentration. Twenty micromolar is a high, lab-scale level, and nothing in the record shows a nasal dose producing anything close to it in blood or brain (inferred).","The GABA question, in rats. One intranasal dose of 300 micrograms per kilogram changed expression of 45 neurotransmission genes in rat frontal cortex at one hour, and the pattern correlated strongly with the pattern GABA itself produced (r = 0.86). By three hours the correlation had turned slightly negative (r = minus 0.39), and the biggest single change was an orexin gene rising about 128-fold [1] (verified). The authors read this as possible allosteric action on GABA signalling and asked for more work.","The GABA question, in human cells. In human neuroblastoma cells that carry GABA-A receptors, Selank alone changed none of 69 measured genes, but it almost completely suppressed the changes GABA caused when the two were given together [2] (verified). The authors concluded the data only partially support a GABA-A mechanism. So the most-repeated claim about Selank, that it is a GABA-A modulator, rests on gene-expression patterns, not on receptor binding or electrophysiology.","Immune and growth-factor effects, in people and rats. Suppression of IL-6 gene expression in blood cells of depressed patients, a shifted Th1/Th2 cytokine balance, and a rapid rise in hippocampal BDNF in rats are all reported in Russian-language work [11] (reported). These were not opened in full for this read."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"people","body":["The Russian label gives the only human-facing pharmacokinetic figures: absolute bioavailability of 92.8% after nasal use, detection in plasma about 30 seconds after dosing, plasma levels falling away over 5 to 5.5 minutes, and neither intact drug nor metabolites found in 24-hour urine because tissue peptidases break it down completely [3] (verified). The label says it reaches brain tissue but gives no figure for how much.","The label does not state a half-life. The commonly quoted figure of about two minutes is not on it (verified as absent). Whatever the exact number, exposure is measured in minutes, while the reported effects are said to last days, including about a week after the last dose in one trial [6]. That gap is either a sign of downstream signalling outlasting the drug, or a sign the effect is softer than reported. Nothing in the record separates the two (inferred)."]},{"h":"The human record, read closely","where":"people","body":["The core trial randomised 62 people with generalised anxiety disorder or neurasthenia to Selank (30) or the benzodiazepine medazepam (32), scored on Hamilton, Zung and clinical global scales. Anxiety relief was similar in both arms, and Selank added anti-fatigue and mildly stimulating effects [5] (verified). The trial also measured how fast blood broke down leu-enkephalin; that half-life was short at baseline and lengthened on Selank, which fits the enzyme mechanism above [5]. There was no placebo arm.","A 2014 comparison against phenazepam in 60 people with phobic-anxiety and somatoform disorders reported pronounced anti-anxiety and mild nootropic effects, lasting about a week after the last dose, with better quality of life [6] (verified). A 2012 congress abstract followed 20 people with anxiety on 2.7 mg a day: 40% improved within three days (Hamilton score 20.3 to 7.0), the rest gradually by day 14 (16.1 to 6.2), and the fast responders showed EEG changes after a single dose [7] (verified). It had no control group.","Put together: every accessible human study is small, Russian, either uncontrolled or compared against another active drug, and none has a placebo arm. Comparing against a benzodiazepine and finding \"similar\" results without a placebo cannot tell a working drug from a strong placebo response, which is large in anxiety trials (inferred). There is no registered trial outside Russia and no established dose outside the Russian label."]},{"h":"Safety: what is known, and what is just absent","where":"people","body":["The label lists allergic reactions, including delayed ones, and an unpleasant taste when drops run into the throat, both of unknown frequency, and records no overdose cases [3] (verified). That is a short list, and short lists in old national registrations often reflect thin monitoring rather than proven safety (inferred).","No English-language repeat-dose toxicology, reproductive toxicology or carcinogenicity study was located. Use in pregnancy, breastfeeding and under 18 is excluded on the label precisely because it was never studied [3] (verified). Higher-than-label doses, injection, and modified forms have no safety data at all."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"lab","body":["The developer institute appears on both sides of the record. The 2014 clinical comparison lists among its authors the developer group's senior chemist and the scientists who ran the 2001 enzyme study [4,6] (verified). The gene-expression work comes from the same institute [1,2], and the 2012 trial from the co-developing pharmacology institute [7]. This is normal for a national drug programme. It does mean there is no independent replication of either the clinical or mechanistic findings in the accessible literature (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Russia: a registered prescription medicine as 0.15% nasal drops, for anxiety states and adjustment disorders in adults [3] (verified). US: not approved; outside any compounding category since its nomination was withdrawn; not reviewed by the July 2026 advisory committee [8,9] (verified).","Sport: it is not named on the 2026 Prohibited List. The catch-all non-approved class covers substances with no current approval by any government health authority for human use [10]. Selank does hold a national approval, so that class arguably does not fit it, but nothing in the list settles the point (inferred). Athletes cannot assume it is permitted."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does it beat placebo? No accessible trial asks. One adequately sized placebo-controlled study would matter more than every result above.","How does a peptide that is gone from plasma in minutes produce effects reported to last a week? Unexplained.","Is it a GABA-A modulator at all? Binding or electrophysiology data would answer this; gene-expression patterns cannot.","Do modified forms behave like the registered drug? Unstudied."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Volkova et al., Front Pharmacol, Selank and GABAergic gene expression in rat frontal cortex","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2016.00031/full","year":2016},{"n":2,"t":"Filatova et al., Front Pharmacol, GABA, Selank and olanzapine in IMR-32 cells","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5328971","year":2017},{"n":3,"t":"Selank 0.15% nasal drops, Russian product label (RLS database)","url":"https://www.rlsnet.ru/drugs/selank-2638","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Kost et al., Russ J Bioorg Chem, Semax and Selank inhibit enkephalin-degrading enzymes of human serum","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1011373002885","year":2001},{"n":5,"t":"Zozulia et al., Zh Nevrol Psikhiatr, Selank vs medazepam in anxiety disorder and neurasthenia","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18454096/","year":2008},{"n":6,"t":"Medvedev et al., Zh Nevrol Psikhiatr, Selank vs phenazepam in anxiety disorders","url":"https://repository.rudn.ru/en/records/article/record/5048","year":2014},{"n":7,"t":"Syunyakov et al., Eur Psychiatry congress abstract, rapid and slow response to Selank in GAD","url":"https://cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/7A497218D37084BD079EFE143126F56E/S0924933800083309a.pdf/p-1114-rapid-and-slow-response-during-treatment-of-generalized-anxiety-disorder-with-peptide-anxiolytic-selank.pdf","year":2012},{"n":8,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024},{"n":9,"t":"FDA, Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee, 23 and 24 July 2026 meeting page","url":"https://www.fda.gov/advisory-committees/advisory-committee-calendar/july-23-24-2026-meeting-pharmacy-compounding-advisory-committee-07232026","year":2026},{"n":10,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":11,"t":"Wikipedia, Selank, summary of Russian pharmacology literature with references","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selank","year":2026}]},"semax":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from","where":"lab","body":["Semax was built in Moscow from the ACTH(4-7) fragment, Met-Glu-His-Phe, which matches part of alpha-MSH, with a Pro-Gly-Pro tail for stability [5] (verified). It was first described in the literature in 1991 and placed on Russia's list of vital and essential drugs on 7 December 2011 [3] (verified). It has a real national drug history, which sets it apart from most compounds in this library.","What that history does not include is a defined mechanism. Even the licensing country's literature describes the mechanism as unknown [3] (reported). The Russian label itself sells the compound in two very different strengths for two different jobs: 0.1% for cognitive and neurological indications, and 1% for acute ischaemic stroke only [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"What is actually in a vial","where":"lab","body":["The registered product is nasal drops with methylparaben as preservative, in 3 mL bottles [2] (verified). What US nominators proposed instead was injection or nasal spray at 7.5 mg or 1 mg per millilitre [1] (verified).","The FDA's July 2026 review found the naming unreliable: certificates titled \"semax acetate\" carried the identifiers of the free base. The acetate certificates listed identity, purity and water, and allowed single unidentified impurities of up to 1% or 2%, but none reported aggregates, bioburden or endotoxin [1] (verified). Both forms were judged not well characterised.","Solubility is a concrete problem. The free base dissolves in water to about 2 mg/mL and in phosphate buffer to about 10 mg/mL [1] (verified). A 7.5 mg/mL product would therefore need buffer or the salt form, since plain water would not hold it (inferred). Lyophilised powder is quoted as stable four years at minus 20 degrees, but reconstituted solution only 2 to 7 days in the fridge [1] (verified), far shorter than most handling assumes.","No nasal spray device information, meaning container, closure or pump, was supplied, which the agency flagged as a strong concern for a nasal product [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Melanocortin receptors, in cells and animals. Semax competitively blocked alpha-MSH at MC4 and MC5 receptors and did not block MC3 [3] (reported). So the receptor action, where it exists, looks like antagonism or partial agonism rather than switching receptors on.","Enzymes, in human serum. Semax inhibited enkephalin-degrading enzymes with an IC50 of about 10 micromolar [4] (verified), a concentration well above anything nasal dosing is shown to reach (inferred).","Stroke, in rats. A single nasal dose of 250 micrograms per kilogram after photothrombotic stroke gave infarcts about 20% smaller at day 8, and intraperitoneal dosing reduced neuronal damage after carotid occlusion [1] (verified). Transcriptome work after ischaemia shows inflammatory genes turned down and neurotransmission genes turned up, and the Pro-Gly-Pro tail alone does not reproduce it, so the ACTH part matters [5] (verified).","Blood clotting, in rats. This is the least-known part of the profile. Nasal 1 mg/kg a day for five days raised fibrinolytic activity, another group found a hypocoagulation profile, and platelet aggregation fell about 35%. The breakdown product Pro-Gly-Pro was itself anticoagulant [1] (verified). No dose-response was examined.","Dopamine, in mice. Semax potentiated amphetamine-induced dopamine release in the striatum, which the FDA called concerning for abuse potential; no abuse or reinforcement study exists [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"mixed","body":["The label states nasal uptake of up to 60 to 70%, arrival in the brain within about 4 minutes, rapid breakdown in blood and urinary excretion, with active fragments, and an effect lasting 20 to 24 hours after one dose [2] (verified). No half-life is given.","The rat numbers put the brain claim in proportion. After intravenous dosing, peak brain level was about 0.005% of the dose; after nasal dosing, about 0.072% at 2 minutes, with brain-to-blood ratios of 0.24 and 0.67 [1] (verified). Nasal delivery does favour the brain, but the amount arriving is a tiny fraction of what is given. The main metabolite is Pro-Gly-Pro [1].","No human pharmacokinetic study exists for any route, and no subcutaneous data exist in any species [1] (verified). So injection, the route many people now use, has no exposure data behind it."]},{"h":"The human record","where":"people","body":["The stroke use is the oldest claim. A 2018 Russian study of patients at different stages of ischaemic stroke is cited for faster and greater recovery of neurological function [5,6] (reported). The full paper was not opened for this read.","The FDA found only two usable references for the three uses it reviewed. One 2002 meeting abstract in chronic ischaemic brain disease gave clotting labs without full results and no functional outcome [1]. The other, from 1996, was open-label and uncontrolled. In 12 people with migraine, a single nasal dose cut a pain-sensitivity score from 78% to 32%, and 4 of 12 reported the headache stopping within two hours. In 16 people with typical trigeminal neuralgia, nothing changed. In 9 with dental nerve pain, 6 resolved, but attack frequency and duration did not fall, and the authors concluded Semax has no analgesic activity by itself [1] (verified).","Across the safety literature, roughly 33 to 47 healthy adults, 69 adults with medical conditions and 451 children with depression or tics have been exposed, all nasally. Three of the sources were meeting abstracts, one reported no adverse events, and the rest did not discuss them [1] (verified). There is no established human dose outside the Russian label."]},{"h":"Safety: what has been looked at, and what has not","where":"mixed","body":["Absent: acute toxicity, repeat-dose toxicity, reproductive toxicity and two-year carcinogenicity studies [1] (verified). The one cancer-related study gave 3 mg/kg intraperitoneally to female mice that already had mammary tumours; tumours ended smaller (7.4 against 15.0 cubic centimetres) and lifespan longer, but with one dose, one sex and under three months it cannot assess cancer risk [1] (verified).","The label contraindicates Semax in pregnancy, breastfeeding, acute psychosis, anxiety disorders and a history of seizures [2] (verified). That anxiety line matters, because Semax is often sold paired with an anti-anxiety peptide for people who feel overstimulated.","The FDA's adverse event system held one report: a consumer who bought 0.1% nasal drops online reported eye pain and burning, hospitalisation, and pain still unresolved a year later [1] (verified). The antithrombotic animal data raise a bleeding question for anyone on blood thinners or facing surgery; it has not been studied in people (inferred)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"lab","body":["Almost all of it comes from the Moscow institutes that developed the drug and their clinical partners [1,5] (verified). That is typical for a national medicine, and the work is not trivial: there are transcriptome and proteome studies in rat stroke models. What is missing is any independent replication outside Russia, and any trial built to current standards with a placebo arm and pre-registered endpoints (verified as absent in the FDA review)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Russia: a registered medicine on the essential drugs list [2,3] (verified). US: listed in the do-not-compound category as of 2024 [11], then reviewed by the FDA, whose staff concluded the criteria weighed against both forms [1]. On 24 July 2026 the advisory committee nonetheless voted 8 to 5 with one abstention to recommend it, for cerebral ischaemia, migraine and trigeminal neuralgia [7,8] (verified). The vote is advisory, and a bulks-list listing still needs rulemaking.","An interim access policy for compounding certain peptides went to White House regulatory review on 14 October 2026; it had not been issued and the peptides it covers are not public [9] (verified).","Sport: not named on the 2026 Prohibited List. The catch-all class covers substances with no current government approval anywhere [10]; Semax is approved in Russia, so that class arguably does not fit, but the list does not settle it (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does it help stroke recovery in a placebo-controlled trial? This is the most answerable question and the one with the oldest claim.","What do the antithrombotic effects mean in people? Unstudied, and relevant to anyone bleeding-prone.","Does the dopamine potentiation carry abuse risk? No study exists.","What happens with injection, and with long-term use? No exposure or toxicity data exist for either."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, Semax bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193348/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Semax 0.1% and 1% nasal drops, Russian product label (RLS database)","url":"https://www.rlsnet.ru/drugs/semaks-5536","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Wikipedia, Semax, summary of pharmacology and Russian status with references","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semax","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Kost et al., Russ J Bioorg Chem, Semax and Selank inhibit enkephalin-degrading enzymes of human serum","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1011373002885","year":2001},{"n":5,"t":"Sudarkina et al., Int J Mol Sci, brain protein profile after Semax in rat ischaemia-reperfusion","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/22/12/6179","year":2021},{"n":6,"t":"Gusev et al., Zh Nevrol Psikhiatr, efficacy of Semax at different stages of ischaemic stroke (PubMed record)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29798983/","year":2018},{"n":7,"t":"American Med Spa Association, FDA committee recommends six of seven peptides","url":"https://www.americanmedspa.org/news/fda-advisory-committee-recommends-adding-six-of-seven-peptides-to-compounding-list/","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on April 2026 category changes and July votes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":10,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":11,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024}]},"pinealon":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and what kind of evidence that implies","where":"lab","body":["Pinealon is the tripeptide Glu-Asp-Arg, also written EDR, one of a family of short \"bioregulator\" peptides from the St Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology and the Pavlov Institute group around Vladimir Khavinson [2,4] (verified). Unlike a fragment cut from a known hormone, it was designed to stand for a tissue: the logic is that tiny peptides carry tissue-specific signals into cells. That logic is the group's own hypothesis, not an established principle of biology (inferred).","The literature is almost entirely Russian-language or from the originating group, and the English record mostly consists of reviews that summarise that work rather than new primary data [2,4] (verified)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Free radicals, in cells. In rat cerebellar neurons, EDR reduced reactive oxygen species triggered by ouabain, homocysteine and hydrogen peroxide, delayed homocysteine-driven ERK1/2 activation from about 2.5 to about 20 minutes, and lowered lipid hydroperoxides; similar effects were reported in activated neutrophils [1,2] (reported).","Gene control, mostly on a computer. The headline claim that EDR binds DNA and adjusts genes rests on molecular modelling: two predicted binding motifs, a search that found matching motifs in the promoters of two metabolic genes (PPARA and PPARG) and in a serotonin-synthesis gene, and an assumption that the peptide reaches the nucleus [2] (verified). A separate older study reported short fluorescent peptides entering HeLa cell nuclei and binding DNA in a test tube [6] (reported), but the 2021 EDR review itself presents no uptake or nuclear-localisation data for EDR [2] (verified). Prediction, not demonstration.","Brain function, in rats. Reported findings include normalised CNS function in offspring of mothers with high homocysteine, lower brain caspase-3 and better maze learning after hypoxia in old and young rats, and raised antioxidant enzymes in hypoxia-sensitive animals [2,4] (reported). Most are cited secondhand in reviews.","Human neurons in a dish. The most modern study converted skin cells from three women aged 61 to 68 into neurons and gave EDR at 10 micrograms per millilitre for 10 days. Oxidative DNA damage fell 23% (p = 0.057, so borderline), primary processes rose 28% and total dendrite length 46%, while mitochondrial activity, lysosomes and two ageing markers did not change [3] (verified). Three donors, one concentration, a lab dish."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"lab","body":["There is no published pharmacokinetic data for Pinealon in any species (verified as absent in the sources reviewed). Free tripeptides are generally broken down quickly by blood and gut peptidases, so how much of an injected or swallowed dose reaches a neuron intact is unknown (inferred). The product is sold both as oral capsules and as injectable powder, with no study comparing what either route delivers."]},{"h":"The human record","where":"people","body":["The 2021 review by the originating group describes oral EDR added to standard therapy in 72 patients with the after-effects of traumatic brain injury, reporting better memory, fewer and milder headaches and better work performance, and a second group with fewer errors on an attention test and a higher EEG alpha index [2] (reported). Neither design, control group nor blinding is described. Memory improvement in elderly patients is claimed in the abstract without detail.","No randomised, placebo-controlled trial of Pinealon has been located, no trial is registered, and no study outside the originating network exists. There is no established human dose (verified as absent in the sources reviewed)."]},{"h":"Safety, and the shape of what is missing","where":"mixed","body":["No toxicology of any kind, acute, repeat-dose, reproductive or carcinogenicity, has been located, and no adverse-event series exists (verified as absent in the sources reviewed). The claimed mechanism, a peptide that enters the nucleus and changes gene expression, is precisely the kind of action that would normally demand long-term safety work; that it has none is the main gap (inferred).","A practical point: the human reports used oral capsules, while much current use is injection of lyophilised powder. Injected material raises sterility, endotoxin and impurity questions that have never been addressed for this peptide (inferred)."]},{"h":"Status and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Not an approved drug anywhere. Not on any US compounding list reviewed for this read, and not among the peptides at the July 2026 advisory committee. In sport it is not named, and as a substance with no government approval for therapeutic use it falls in the catch-all non-approved class [5] (inferred).","Open questions: does EDR reach the nucleus of a living neuron? Does any effect survive a placebo-controlled trial? Is any oral dose absorbed intact? And does an independent lab reproduce even the antioxidant cell findings? Each is answerable, and none has been answered."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Khavinson et al., Rejuvenation Res, Pinealon increases cell viability by suppressing free radicals","url":"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/abs/10.1089/rej.2011.1172","year":2011},{"n":2,"t":"Khavinson et al., Molecules, EDR peptide: possible mechanism of gene expression regulation","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/26/1/159","year":2021},{"n":3,"t":"Kraskovskaya et al., Int J Mol Sci, short peptides protect fibroblast-derived induced neurons","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/25/21/11363","year":2024},{"n":4,"t":"Wikipedia, Pinealon, summary of published claims with references","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinealon","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":6,"t":"PubMed record, short fluorescence-labelled peptides penetrate HeLa cell nuclei and bind DNA","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22117547/","year":2011}]},"epitalon":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and the substitution hidden in the story","where":"lab","body":["Epithalamin, a crude polypeptide extract of cattle pineal gland, appears in accessible research from 1973. Epitalon, the four-residue Ala-Glu-Asp-Gly, was later synthesised from the extract's amino acid makeup, and its presence in human pineal extract was first reported only in 2017 [2] (verified).","That order matters more than any single study. The most quoted human results, the multi-year mortality cohorts, were done with the extract, not with the synthetic tetrapeptide sold today [4,5] (verified). Treating them as evidence for Epitalon assumes the four residues are the whole active ingredient of a crude extract. That has never been shown (inferred). When the FDA reviewed Epitalon in 2026, it found no human cohort or mortality study of the synthetic peptide at all [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"What is actually in a vial","where":"lab","body":["The FDA found that one nominator's certificate listed a molecular formula matching neither the free base nor the acetate, and another submitted an acetate certificate under free-base identifiers [1] (verified). The acetate certificate that did exist reported 99.5% purity with total impurities up to 2% and single impurities up to 1%, none identified, and no bioburden, endotoxin or aggregate testing [1] (verified). No certificate at all was submitted for the free base.","Water solubility of the free base is limited, and the agency doubted that a 3 mg/mL product could be made in water [1]. Reconstituted solution is reported stable 2 to 7 days at 4 degrees [1] (verified).","Two identity traps are specific to this peptide. Eight stereoisomers are possible and only the all-L form has been studied, and market products have been checked by mass spectrometry to separate genuine material from the swapped-sequence isomer EADG [2] (verified). Products are sold as acetate or trifluoroacetate salts, which differ in weight and in what else ends up in the vial [2]."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Telomerase, in the originating group's cells. Human fetal lung fibroblasts given 50 ng/mL for four days showed higher telomerase expression and activity and longer telomeres, and treated cultures kept dividing past passage 44 while controls stopped at 34 [1,2] (reported).","Telomerase, in an independent lab. A 2025 London university study is the first outside replication and is more complicated than either side tends to quote. In normal fibroblasts and mammary epithelial cells, three weeks at 1 microgram per millilitre raised telomerase activity about 4-fold and 26-fold and lengthened telomeres; four days did nothing. In two breast cancer lines, telomeres also lengthened, but telomerase activity did not rise; instead the alternative, telomerase-independent lengthening pathway (ALT) rose about 10-fold and 3-fold [3] (verified). The authors suggested this supports safe use in healthy people, a claim their experiments did not test [3].","Melatonin, in monkeys. Older rhesus monkeys given 2 micrograms per kilogram intramuscularly for 10 days had evening melatonin more than 4-fold higher and evening cortisol about 30% lower; younger monkeys showed no melatonin change [1] (verified). Yet in isolated rat pineal tissue, Epitalon at up to 100 micromolar did not increase melatonin release [1] (verified), so whatever raises melatonin probably does not act on the gland directly.","Chromatin, in cells. Decondensation of heterochromatin in lymphocytes from donors aged 76 to 80 is reported from the same group [5] (reported)."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"animals","body":["There is no pharmacokinetic data, including half-life, for either form in any species, people included [1] (verified). The only distribution work gave a fluorescent label to pregnant rabbits; signal appeared in liver, lungs and gut at one hour and weakly in brain, and the agency noted it does not prove the signal was the peptide [1]. The commonly quoted two-hour half-life has no source in the regulatory record (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"The human record","where":"people","body":["For the synthetic peptide, the one randomised placebo-controlled study enrolled 75 women aged 40 to 50, gave about 0.5 mg a day under the tongue for 20 days, and found urinary melatonin metabolite about 1.6 to 1.7-fold higher plus changes in clock gene expression [1,2] (verified). Blinding was not specified, no sleep outcome was measured, and the reported mood improvement (83% against 25%) came without a described method [1]. A retinitis pigmentosa series of 162 patients used 5 micrograms per eye by injection beside the eyeball for 10 days [2] (reported).","For the extract, the cohorts are larger and older. A study of 266 people over 60 reported 1.6 to 1.8-fold lower mortality over six years, and up to 4.1-fold with the thymic extract added and yearly courses [5] (reported). A comparison of 39 elderly coronary patients on six courses of epithalamin over three years against 40 on standard therapy reported preserved endurance, normalised melatonin rhythm and lower mortality [4] (verified). The paper's own title says pituitary gland and 15-year follow-up while the abstract describes a pineal peptide over three years [4], which is the kind of inconsistency that should lower confidence.","No trial in insomnia, the use reviewed in 2026, has ever been run, and no trial has used injection under the skin [1] (verified). There is no established human dose for the synthetic peptide."]},{"h":"Safety: what has been looked at, and what has not","where":"mixed","body":["Absent: acute toxicity, repeat-dose toxicity, reproductive toxicity and two-year carcinogenicity [1] (verified). The mouse tumour and lifespan studies used females only, one fixed dose, and at most about 22 weeks of exposure, which the agency said cannot assess cancer risk [1]. No adverse event reports reached the FDA or its food complaint system [1].","The cancer question is the honest centre of this compound. A drug designed to lengthen telomeres pushes in the direction that unusually long telomeres point to in population studies, where they are linked to several cancers, which the agency named as an unresolved concern [1] (verified). The independent 2025 data add a specific wrinkle: in cancer cells it switched on a different lengthening pathway [3]. Neither finding shows harm; together they explain why long-term safety data are not optional here (inferred)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"lab","body":["Nearly all pharmacology and every human study come from one St Petersburg group and its partners, much of it in Russian and not verified by the FDA [1,2] (verified). The 2025 review that catalogued this work came from an independent Warsaw pharmacy faculty with no external funding, and it lists inconsistent reporting, very low or non-monotonic doses and unreplicated 1990s data among the limitations [2] (verified). The 2025 telomere replication is genuinely independent [3]. That makes two outside groups engaging seriously, which is more than most compounds of this kind have."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Not an approved drug anywhere, with no EMA-authorised product and no approval in the major European markets or Canada and Australia [1] (verified). In 2024 it sat in the US do-not-compound category [10]. On 24 July 2026 the advisory committee recommended adding it to the bulks list for insomnia, against staff advice [1,6]. The count is reported as 7 to 4 with one abstention by one trade summary and 7 to 5 by a law firm [6,7] (reported); either way it is advisory and needs rulemaking. An interim access policy for certain peptides went to White House review on 14 October 2026, with covered peptides not public [8] (verified).","Sport: not named, but with no government approval it falls in the catch-all non-approved class, prohibited at all times [9] (inferred application, verified wording)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is the synthetic tetrapeptide the active ingredient of the extract that produced the cohort results? Never tested.","What happens to telomeres and cancer risk over years of courses? Unstudied in any species past about five months.","Does it improve sleep, measured as sleep? No trial has looked.","What reaches the blood after injection or under the tongue, and for how long? No data exist."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, Epitalon bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193345/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Araj et al., Int J Mol Sci, overview of Epitalon","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/26/6/2691","year":2025},{"n":3,"t":"Al-dulaimi et al., Biogerontology, Epitalon lengthens telomeres via telomerase or ALT","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10522-025-10315-x","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Korkushko et al., Bull Exp Biol Med, epithalamin in elderly coronary patients, follow-up","url":"https://link.springer.com/doi/10.1007/s10517-011-1332-x","year":2011},{"n":5,"t":"Wikipedia, Epitalon, summary of cohort and cell studies with references","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epitalon","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"American Med Spa Association, FDA committee recommends six of seven peptides","url":"https://www.americanmedspa.org/news/fda-advisory-committee-recommends-adding-six-of-seven-peptides-to-compounding-list/","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on April 2026 category changes and July votes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":10,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024}]},"dsip":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and the origin that never resolved","where":"animals","body":["A Swiss group isolated the nonapeptide Trp-Ala-Gly-Gly-Asp-Ala-Ser-Gly-Glu from the cerebral venous blood of rabbits held in electrically induced sleep, with the defining papers dated 1977 [2] (verified). Its regulatory name is emideltide [1].","Half a century later, no gene, precursor protein or receptor has been found, and its site of synthesis is unknown [2] (reported). A database search lines the sequence up with a hypothetical protein from a soil bacterium, which raises the possibility, unproven, that what was isolated was not a mammalian hormone at all [2] (speculated). DSIP-like material has been measured in the hypothalamus, pituitary, gut, breast milk and blood, but immunoreactivity is not the same as proof of an endogenous peptide (inferred)."]},{"h":"What is actually in a vial","where":"lab","body":["One certificate submitted to the FDA listed the formula C35H57N13O14S2, which the agency said corresponds to no form of emideltide; another tested for acetic acid but carried free-base identifiers [1] (verified). Neither form's certificate set impurity limits, and none reported endotoxin, bioburden or aggregates [1].","Solubility is a real constraint. The free base dissolves in water at about 0.5 mg/mL, so the proposed 1 mg/mL injection may not be achievable with it; the acetate dissolves at about 10 mg/mL in buffer [1] (verified). Supplier guidance recommends a carrier protein for long-term storage and warns against freeze-thaw [1]."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"mixed","body":["In vitro half-life is about 15 minutes, set by an aminopeptidase-like enzyme [2] (reported). It may circulate partly bound to carrier proteins, but that has not been confirmed. Human plasma levels of DSIP-like material are reported to fall at sleep onset, the opposite of what a sleep-inducing signal might predict, and levels in depression are reported both higher and lower by different studies [2] (reported).","No pharmacokinetic study by subcutaneous injection exists in people, and the FDA found no human trial by that route at all [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The human record, which got weaker as it got better designed","where":"people","body":["Every human trial gave DSIP intravenously, at 25 to 35 nanomoles per kilogram [1] (verified). The pattern across them is worth seeing whole.","The early, smaller or externally controlled studies looked positive. In 6 middle-aged insomniacs in a crossover, sleep improved from the second to the sixth hour after a slight arousal in the first. In 14 people given seven nightly injections, sleep latency fell from 58.4 to 27.6 minutes and total sleep rose from 313 to 385 minutes, but against an externally matched group, not a true placebo [1] (verified).","The properly placebo-controlled studies did not hold up. In 16 insomniacs over three nights, there was no significant objective difference against placebo, and the authors called the benefit little. In 6 severe insomniacs in a randomised crossover, there was no significant difference from placebo or baseline [1] (verified).","Narcolepsy rests on one 35-year-old man: sleep attacks fell from six to three, better with morning dosing [1]. In withdrawal, an uncontrolled study of 60 opioid users reported a good decrease in signs in 40 of those assessed, with anxiety and insomnia returning 24 to 72 hours later; a later open study of 7 saw withdrawal scores fall from 34.4 to 17.8 after the first injection, but only 2 finished and 2 needed another sleep drug [1] (verified). The authors of that later study called effectiveness an open question.","There is no established human dose by any route people use now."]},{"h":"Safety and quality concerns","where":"mixed","body":["What the trials recorded: transient arousal in the first hour, delayed effects on sleep 13 to 22 hours later, and rebound anxiety and insomnia after withdrawal treatment stopped [1] (verified). Long-term safety has never been studied [2].","The agency's main concern was not toxicity but identity and immunogenicity: an injectable peptide with unidentified impurities and no aggregate data, given repeatedly [1] (verified). A Russian preparation, Deltaran, is reported to have extended maximum lifespan in ageing mice by about 24% [2] (reported), but no human safety record follows from that."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Not a registered drug in any country searched, and in no major pharmacopoeia [1] (verified). Listed in the US do-not-compound category in 2024 [6]. On 24 July 2026 the advisory committee voted 6 in favour, 7 against, with one abstention, for opioid withdrawal, chronic insomnia and narcolepsy, making it the only one of seven peptides that day not recommended [3,4] (verified). FDA staff had also recommended against [1].","Sport: not named, but with no government approval it falls within the catch-all non-approved class, prohibited at all times [5] (inferred application, verified wording)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is it a mammalian peptide? Without a gene or precursor, the question from 1977 is still open.","Does it do anything under the skin? The route in use today has never been tested in a human trial.","Why did effects shrink as controls improved? The honest reading is that the early signal was mostly expectation and night-to-night variation, but a larger modern trial would settle it (inferred)."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, emideltide (DSIP) bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193344/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Wikipedia, Delta-sleep-inducing peptide, summary of literature with references","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta-sleep-inducing_peptide","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"American Med Spa Association, FDA committee recommends six of seven peptides","url":"https://www.americanmedspa.org/news/fda-advisory-committee-recommends-adding-six-of-seven-peptides-to-compounding-list/","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":6,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024}]},"thymosin-alpha-1":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and why it is different from most peptides here","where":"lab","body":["Thymosin alpha-1 is a 28-residue peptide first isolated from a calf thymus extract; the drug version, thymalfasin, is synthetic [6] (verified). It is a natural human peptide: healthy adults carry about 0.1 to 1 ng/mL in serum, highest in the thymus [1] (verified).","What sets it apart is that a licensed, manufacturer-controlled product exists. Zadaxin is supplied as a 1.6 mg vial for subcutaneous injection, made in Italy, with a licence holder in Singapore and indications in chronic hepatitis B and, with interferon, hepatitis C [7] (verified). Its approvals span Asia-Pacific, Latin America, Eastern Europe and the Middle East, plus Italy as a flu vaccine adjuvant for immunocompromised people, India for hepatitis B, and an EMA orphan designation in liver cancer [1] (reported; the FDA could not verify the manufacturer's full country list)."]},{"h":"What is actually in a vial, licensed versus not","where":"lab","body":["The licensed product is reconstituted with its own 1 mL of sterile water just before use, and the label is clear it is subcutaneous only, not intravenous [7] (verified).","Material outside that channel is a different question. Reviewing the US nomination, the FDA found free base and acetate confused in the paperwork, certificates without impurity, aggregate, bioburden or endotoxin data, and doubted that the proposed 3 mg/mL could be reached given limited water solubility; the highest concentration it found used clinically was 2 mg/mL [1,2] (verified). The research-grade powder does not inherit the licensed product's quality record (inferred)."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Dendritic cells, in cells and mice. In dendritic cells exposed to the fungus Aspergillus, the peptide drove maturation and IL-12 production through p38 MAPK and NF-kappaB, dependent on MyD88 and Toll-like receptor signalling; in mice it strengthened Th1 antifungal immunity and protected highly susceptible transplant recipients from aspergillosis [5] (verified). This is the study most often cited for the TLR mechanism, and it is a fungal infection model.","T cells and cytokines, in people. Clinical and lab work summarised for hepatitis B describes effects on T-cell development, higher interferon-gamma and IL-2, and more NK cell killing [6] (reported).","Vaccines, in mice. Anti-tetanus antibodies were about 25 to 35% higher in young mice given the peptide, but there was no boost with a pneumococcal vaccine [1] (verified). The adjuvant effect is real in some settings and absent in others."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"people","body":["After 1.6 to 2.2 mg under the skin in healthy volunteers, peak levels were 32.6 to 78.9 ng/mL at 0.67 to 2 hours, half-life was 1.8 to 2.6 hours, and five daily doses did not accumulate [1] (verified). Levels return near baseline within about a day.","Set against the endogenous range, one standard dose raises blood levels roughly 30 to several hundred times above normal for a few hours (inferred from the two ranges). The twice-weekly schedule therefore produces pulses, not a steady state, and the immune effects are presumed to outlast the drug."]},{"h":"The human record, where size changes the answer","where":"people","body":["Hepatitis B is the indication that built its reputation. Seven randomised trials of 1.6 mg twice weekly for six months reported a significantly higher sustained response than no treatment, with a peculiar timing: benefit was not obvious on treatment and complete virological response rose gradually after it stopped [6] (reported). These trials predate today's antivirals.","Sepsis is where the record turned. A 2025 meta-analysis of 11 randomised trials, all from China, found lower 28-day mortality overall (odds ratio 0.73), but the benefit came from the six lower-quality trials (odds ratio 0.41, 359 patients); the five higher-quality trials showed no significant effect (0.82, 95% CI 0.65 to 1.03), and the test for publication bias was positive [4] (verified).","The largest trial settles most of that. TESTS randomised 1,106 adults with sepsis at 22 Chinese centres to 1.6 mg every 12 hours or placebo for seven days. Day-28 mortality was 23.4% against 24.1%, hazard ratio 0.99 (95% CI 0.77 to 1.27); 90-day mortality was also no different [3] (verified). Exploratory subgroups pointed both ways, worse under 60 (hazard ratio 1.67) and better with diabetes (0.58), neither surviving correction for multiple testing [3].","In the US it was trialled in cancer from the early 1980s; a phase 1 escalation in 14 patients reached 9.6 mg/m2 without a dose-limiting toxicity [1] (verified). More than 4,400 trial subjects are cited across infections, cancers and vaccines [1], but no US approval followed."]},{"h":"Safety: a real record, with real limits","where":"mixed","body":["Of all the peptides in this library, this one has the most human safety data. In TESTS, adverse events (66.4% against 67.6%) and serious adverse events (26.8% against 29.3%) matched placebo [3] (verified). The manufacturer reports over 17 million doses given with infrequent, mild adverse experiences, an estimate from manufacturer-affiliated authors [1] (reported). Injection-site redness and discomfort are the common complaint [1].","The licensed label contraindicates it in people being deliberately immunosuppressed, such as transplant recipients, unless benefit clearly outweighs risk, because it is designed to stimulate immunity [7] (verified). Anyone with an autoimmune condition faces the same logic, though that has not been formally studied (inferred).","The gaps: no carcinogenicity study, and the acute and 13-week toxicology the manufacturer reports (no signals up to 20 mg/kg once, or 6 mg/kg a day) was never provided to the FDA as data [1] (verified). The FDA's adverse event system held one report, in a hepatitis C patient also on peginterferon [1]."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["The licence holder co-funded TESTS, and several authors reported grants or consulting fees from it [3] (verified). That trial is also the one that found no effect, which counts in favour of its credibility. The sepsis meta-analysis found that the positive signal sat in small single-centre trials [4], a familiar pattern for industry-adjacent fields."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Not FDA-approved; no NDA has been approved, though four US orphan designations exist [1,2] (verified). It left the do-not-compound category when its nominator withdrew [8], and at the December 2024 advisory committee the members voted 17 to 4 against adding either form to the bulks list, the acetate vote citing a lack of convincing effectiveness data [2] (verified). It was not part of the 2026 peptide votes.","Sport: not named on the 2026 Prohibited List [9]. Because it is approved in several countries, the catch-all non-approved class does not obviously apply (inferred). Thymosin beta-4 and its fragments are a different molecule and are explicitly banned [9] (verified)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does it help hepatitis B on top of modern antivirals? The original trials compared it to nothing.","Is there any group in sepsis that benefits? TESTS hints at subgroups but cannot prove one.","Is research-grade powder equivalent to the licensed product? Unknown without impurity and endotoxin testing.","What does repeated immune stimulation do over years in healthy people? Unstudied."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, thymosin alpha-1 bulk drug substances","url":"https://fda.gov/media/183820/download","year":2024},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC 4 December 2024 final summary minutes","url":"https://fda.gov/media/185642/download","year":2025},{"n":3,"t":"Wu et al., BMJ, TESTS phase 3 trial of thymosin alpha 1 in sepsis","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2024-082583","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Gu et al., Front Cell Infect Microbiol, meta-analysis of thymosin alpha 1 in sepsis","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fcimb.2025.1673959/full","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"Romani et al., Blood, thymosin alpha 1 activates dendritic cells through TLR signalling","url":"https://research.unipg.it/handle/11391/120773","year":2004},{"n":6,"t":"Chien and Liaw, Expert Rev Anti Infect Ther, thymalfasin for chronic hepatitis B","url":"https://pure.lib.cgu.edu.tw/en/publications/thymalfasin-for-the-treatment-of-chronic-hepatitis-b/","year":2004},{"n":7,"t":"Singapore National Drug Formulary, Zadaxin injection product information","url":"https://www.ndf.gov.sg/about-drugs/product-information/SIN07483P/","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024},{"n":9,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025}]},"pt-141":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The part of the origin story that explains the side effects","where":"lab","body":["Bremelanotide is not a designed-from-scratch molecule. It is the active breakdown product of melanotan II, differing from it by one chemical group: where melanotan II ends in an amide, this ends in an acid [1] (verified). Palatin stopped work on melanotan II in 2000 and carried the metabolite forward instead [1].","That lineage matters because the parent was a tanning peptide, and the family it belongs to acts on all the melanocortin receptors except the one that drives cortisol. Bremelanotide is described as a non-selective agonist across MC1, MC3, MC4 and MC5, acting mainly at MC3 and MC4 [1] (verified). The arousal effect and the pigment effect are not two separate things that happen to coexist; they are two receptors in one family being hit by one molecule. Nothing about the design removed the pigment arm, it was reduced relative to the parent.","The development path is also the clearest safety signal in the file, and it is usually left out. A nasal spray was taken into mid-stage trials in both women and men, including four trials in erectile dysfunction. The FDA halted that programme in 2007 over blood pressure increases, the commercial partner ended its agreement, and the nasal formulation was abandoned in 2008 [1] (verified). The injection that was eventually approved in June 2019 exists because the nasal route failed on cardiovascular grounds, not because injection was preferred."]},{"h":"What the approved trials actually measured, and what they found","where":"people","body":["Two identical 24-week randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trials supported approval, with 635 women on drug and 632 on placebo, followed by a 52-week open-label extension [2] (verified).","There were two co-primary endpoints, both questionnaires. On the desire domain of the Female Sexual Function Index, the change from baseline was +0.5 against +0.2 on placebo in the first trial and +0.6 against +0.2 in the second. On the single question measuring distress about low desire, the change was minus 0.7 against minus 0.4 in both [2] (verified). The p-values are small, the differences are not: roughly a third of a point on one scale and three tenths on another, against placebo.","The endpoint that is easiest for a non-specialist to interpret was negative. The number of satisfying sexual events did not differ from placebo, with p-values of 0.76 and 0.70 [2] (verified). So the approved claim rests on self-reported desire and distress, not on a change in what actually happened.","The dropout figures belong next to the efficacy figures. Premature discontinuation during the blinded period was 40% against 13% on placebo in the first trial and 39% against 25% in the second, and 18% of women on drug stopped specifically for adverse reactions against 2% on placebo [2] (verified). A treatment that four in ten participants stop inside six months is being described accurately only when that is said out loud."]},{"h":"The tolerability profile in plain numbers","where":"people","body":["Nausea affected 40% of women on drug against 1.3% on placebo, usually starting within an hour and lasting about two hours. It was heaviest on the first dose, 21%, and fell to around 3% with later doses. Thirteen percent needed anti-sickness medication and 8% stopped treatment because of it [2] (verified). Flushing reached 20.3% against 0.3%, headache 11.3% against 1.9%, vomiting 4.8%, and injection-site reactions 13.2% against 8.4% [2].","Pigment change is the effect most often treated as cosmetic trivia, and the label's own numbers argue otherwise. Focal darkening of the face, gums and breasts occurred in 1% with as-needed use, but in a study using daily dosing 38% developed it, with a further 14% developing new changes on continued use. Risk was higher with darker skin and with frequent dosing, and resolution was not confirmed in everyone [2] (verified). Frequent or daily use is exactly the pattern reported outside the label, and it is the pattern associated with the pigment change that may not reverse (inferred).","One case of acute hepatitis occurred in the open-label extension and the drug's role could not be definitively excluded, though liver test outliers were not imbalanced in the blinded trials [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"The cardiovascular arm, measured rather than described","where":"people","body":["Every dose raises blood pressure and lowers heart rate, typically resolving within 12 hours [2] (verified). In a 127-patient ambulatory monitoring study the mean daytime rise was 1.9 mmHg systolic and 1.7 mmHg diastolic, with a peak mean systolic rise of 2.8 mmHg and a mean heart rate fall of 0.5 beats per minute [2] (verified).","Those averages look small, and the label's response to them is not small: it is contraindicated in uncontrolled high blood pressure and in known cardiovascular disease, not recommended in people at high cardiovascular risk, and requires cardiovascular risk assessment before starting and periodically afterwards [2] (verified). The averages are averages; the regulator priced in the individuals at the tail, and the 2007 halt of the nasal programme shows what the tail can look like with higher systemic exposure [1].","This is the one section where off-label male use diverges sharply from the evidence base. The trials that generated every number above enrolled premenopausal women. No approved indication, and no completed late-stage programme, exists for men; the male trials in erectile dysfunction were part of the nasal programme the FDA stopped [1] (verified). The cardiovascular screening the label requires has no counterpart when the compound is obtained outside that system (inferred)."]},{"h":"What the body does with it, including the interactions nobody expects","where":"people","body":["Subcutaneous bioavailability is essentially complete, around 100%. Peak concentration averages 72.8 nanograms per millilitre at a median of about one hour, plasma protein binding is 21%, volume of distribution about 25 litres, clearance about 6.5 litres per hour, and terminal half-life about 2.7 hours. It is broken down by hydrolysis of the ring's amide bonds, with about 65% of a radiolabelled dose recovered in urine and 23% in faeces [2] (verified).","Impairment matters more than the short half-life suggests. Exposure rose 1.2-fold in mild, 1.5-fold in moderate and 2-fold in severe kidney impairment, and 1.2-fold in mild and 1.7-fold in moderate liver impairment, with severe liver impairment never studied [2] (verified).","The interaction section is the part most likely to catch someone out, because it has nothing to do with sexual medicine. The drug slows stomach emptying, so it can reduce absorption of swallowed medicines. The label says to avoid oral naltrexone products used for alcohol or opioid dependence, because naltrexone exposure may be significantly reduced, and to avoid oral drugs that depend on reaching a threshold concentration, such as antibiotics; it also flags drugs needed to act fast, such as indomethacin [2] (verified).","Use is not recommended in pregnancy, with contraception advised. Human data are limited to a handful of outcomes in the trials. In animals, fetal harm appeared in dogs at exposures at least 16 times the maximum human dose and developmental delays in mice at roughly 125 times [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"Who owns it now, and where the law stands","where":"mixed","body":["The commercial history is a string of partners handing it on: King Pharmaceuticals in 2004 and out after the 2007 halt, Gedeon Richter for Europe in 2014 and terminated in 2016, AMAG for North America in 2017, approval in 2019 [1] (verified). Palatin then sold the product outright to Cosette Pharmaceuticals in 2023 in a deal worth up to $171 million [3] (verified). Four partners and an outright sale inside twenty years is a commercial signal, not a scientific one, but it tells you how the owners read the market.","It is approved in the United States for premenopausal women with acquired, generalised hypoactive sexual desire disorder, with the label explicitly excluding postmenopausal women, men, and use to enhance performance [2] (verified). It is not approved in Europe or the UK [1] (verified as of the source; EMA pages could not be opened directly for this review).","For sport: bremelanotide is not named on the 2026 Prohibited List, and because it holds government approval for human therapeutic use it does not fall into the catch-all class for non-approved substances either, so on the published text it is not prohibited [4] (inferred from the class definitions). That is a different position from most compounds in this library and should not be generalised to melanotan II."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is a third of a point on a desire questionnaire, with no change in satisfying events, a difference people can feel? The approval says the endpoint was met; it does not settle what the effect is worth, and the 40% dropout rate suggests many participants answered that question for themselves.","Does the pigment change reverse? The label says resolution was not confirmed in all patients, and the highest rates came from daily dosing [2]. Nobody has followed frequent users long enough to answer it.","What does repeated use do in men, at the doses actually used? The entire safety dataset is female, the male programme was stopped on blood pressure grounds, and no one has repeated it [1].","Why does the brain effect need a molecule that also hits pigment and vascular receptors? A genuinely MC4-selective agonist would answer whether the side effects are separable from the effect.","How much of the reported benefit is anticipation? With an as-needed drug taken before a planned event, a short-onset side effect profile that is obvious to the user makes effective blinding difficult, which the 40% versus 13% dropout gap hints at (inferred)."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Bremelanotide, development history and pharmacology (medical wiki mirror)","url":"https://kiwix.lema.org/mdwiki_en_all_2024-03/A/Bremelanotide","year":2024},{"n":2,"t":"FDA label, Vyleesi (bremelanotide) injection, full prescribing information","url":"https://drugcentral.org/label/9146ae05-918b-483e-b86d-933485ce36eb/view","year":2019},{"n":3,"t":"Palatin Technologies, completion of the sale of Vyleesi to Cosette Pharmaceuticals","url":"https://palatin.com/press_releases/palatin-completes-sale-of-vyleesi-to-cosette-pharmaceuticals-for-up-to-171-million/","year":2023},{"n":4,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"oxytocin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Two drugs with one name","where":"people","body":["Oxytocin as an approved medicine is an injection given in hospital to start or strengthen labour and control bleeding after birth. Oxytocin as people use it socially is a nasal spray taken for closeness, calm or sex. They share a molecule and almost nothing else: different route, different dose logic, different evidence and a different regulatory status (inferred from the sources below).","The licensed injection is standardised in units, not milligrams: 10 USP units per millilitre, with 0.5% chlorobutanol as preservative, buffered to pH 3.5, for intravenous infusion or intramuscular injection [1] (verified). A unit is a measure of biological activity, so units and micrograms do not convert cleanly from one product to another (inferred)."]},{"h":"What happened to the nasal spray","where":"people","body":["A licensed nasal oxytocin did exist in the US. Syntocinon Nasal Spray was approved in 1960 to help milk let-down after birth, and was discontinued in 1997 for commercial reasons [6] (verified). A 2013 licence deal aimed to bring it back for lactation and to trial it in schizophrenia and autism [6] (verified); no relaunch is evident in the current record (inferred). So nasal oxytocin bought in the US today is, in practice, a compounded or imported product, not a licensed one (inferred)."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"mixed","body":["In blood, oxytocin lasts minutes: a plasma half-life of about 1 to 6 minutes, shorter in late pregnancy, cleared by kidney and liver [1] (verified). It also has a built-in antidiuretic effect; with long infusions this has caused water intoxication with seizures, coma and maternal death [1] (verified). That risk belongs to high-dose infusion, not a nasal puff, but it shows the hormone is not only about bonding.","The central question for nasal use is whether it reaches the brain. In monkeys, labelled oxytocin given nasally, not intravenously, appeared two hours later in brain regions along the olfactory and trigeminal nerve routes, including the striatum and orbitofrontal cortex [4] (verified). In people, the evidence is thinner: in measured studies, the share of a nasal dose recovered in cerebrospinal fluid is on the order of 0.001% [5] (reported). Some does get in. How much, and whether it is enough to matter, is still argued (inferred)."]},{"h":"The human record on trust and bonding","where":"people","body":["The idea of oxytocin as a trust hormone rests largely on a 2005 study in which 58 investors given nasal oxytocin trusted more in an economic game, at a one-tailed p of 0.029, with no effect on the other side of the exchange or on risk-taking [2] (verified).","A 2015 critical review found six conceptual replications, none of which reliably reproduced it; one found the reverse in women. Pooling seven studies and 481 people gave an effect size of 0.077, not significant, and that was with inclusion rules chosen to favour the hypothesis [2] (verified). Detecting even the upper plausible effect would need more than 400 participants, about the total of every trust study combined at the time [2].","The newer reading is that oxytocin raises the salience of social cues, good and bad, rather than making people more trusting or warmer across the board [8] (reported). That fits why effects vary so much by person and context (inferred)."]},{"h":"The largest clinical test","where":"people","body":["In autism, a 24-week trial randomised 290 children and adolescents aged 3 to 17 to nasal oxytocin, targeting 48 IU a day, or placebo. Social withdrawal scores and cognitive measures did not differ between groups, and adverse events were similar [3] (verified). It is the largest trial of nasal oxytocin in autism, and it was clearly negative.","Sexual and intimacy uses have no comparably sized trial in the record reviewed for this entry (verified as absent in the sources reviewed). There is no established dose for social or sexual use."]},{"h":"Safety, and what has not been studied","where":"people","body":["The injection label's adverse effects are obstetric and serious: uterine hyperstimulation and rupture, arrhythmia, hypertensive episodes, anaphylaxis and fetal distress [1] (verified). The label also states it is not indicated for elective induction of labour, because benefit against risk was never established [1] (verified).","For nasal social use, the large autism trial found adverse events no different from placebo over 24 weeks [3]. Long-term daily nasal use in adults, and use in pregnancy outside obstetric care, are unstudied (verified as absent in the sources reviewed). Because it acts on the uterus, pregnancy is the obvious line not to cross (inferred)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["The early social-neuroscience literature was built on small samples and flexible analyses, and later work points to publication bias in the field [2,8] (reported). The autism trial was the first test at a size able to give a clear answer [3]. The pattern, strong small early results followed by weak large ones, is the same one seen with many compounds here (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["US: approved as an injection for obstetric use; nasal and social use is off-label, typically through compounding [1,6] (verified for the approval; inferred for current supply). Sport: oxytocin is not named on the 2026 Prohibited List and, as an approved medicine, does not fall in the catch-all non-approved class [7] (verified for the list contents; inferred for its application)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["How much of a nasal dose reaches the human brain, and does it act there or through the body? Still unresolved.","Is there a group that reliably benefits socially? The autism trial says not in that population; others are untested at scale.","What does daily use for months do? No long-term adult data exist."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Pitocin (oxytocin injection) professional label (Drugs.com)","url":"https://www.drugs.com/pro/pitocin.html","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Nave, Camerer and McCullough, Perspect Psychol Sci, does oxytocin increase trust in humans","url":"https://pages.ucsd.edu/~memccullough/Papers/NaveCamererMcCullough15.pdf","year":2015},{"n":3,"t":"Sikich et al., N Engl J Med, intranasal oxytocin in autism (MGH summary)","url":"https://mghpsychnews.org/intranasal-oxytocin-and-social-functioning-in-autism-spectrum-disorders/","year":2021},{"n":4,"t":"Lee et al., Nat Commun, labelled intranasal oxytocin reaches the brain in rhesus macaques","url":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/nat/natcom/v11y2020i1d10.1038_s41467-020-15942-1.html","year":2020},{"n":5,"t":"Yeomans et al., Transl Psychiatry, nasal oxytocin and meaningful brain concentrations","url":"https://paperity.org/p/273917268/nasal-oxytocin-for-the-treatment-of-psychiatric-disorders-and-pain-achieving-meaningful","year":2021},{"n":6,"t":"Retrophin press release, US licence for Syntocinon Nasal Spray","url":"https://ir.travere.com/press-releases/news-details/2013/Retrophin-Signs-U-S--License-Agreement-for-Syntocinon-Nasal-Spray-Oxytocin-12-12-2013/default.aspx","year":2013},{"n":7,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":8,"t":"The Transmitter (Spectrum), intranasal oxytocin ineffective for autism in large trial","url":"https://thetransmitter.org/spectrum/intranasal-oxytocin-ineffective-for-autism-in-large-trial","year":2021}]},"glutathione":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The core problem: the body makes it inside cells, and it is hard to deliver there","where":"people","body":["Glutathione is made inside nearly every cell from three amino acids, and it does its work there. Blood levels are low by comparison: about 6 micromoles per litre in plasma in one human study [1] (verified), against concentrations inside cells that are orders of magnitude higher. So the question for any supplement is not whether glutathione matters, which is settled, but whether glutathione given from outside reaches the inside of the cells that need it.","The gut and liver are built to break it down. They carry an enzyme, gamma-glutamyl transferase, that splits it into its component amino acids [1] (verified). The cells then rebuild glutathione from those parts, and the slowest step in that rebuild is usually the supply of cysteine. That is why the precursor N-acetylcysteine became the approved antidote for paracetamol overdose rather than glutathione itself (inferred from the mechanism; NAC's approval is on the card)."]},{"h":"Oral: a negative result, then a positive one, and how they fit","where":"people","body":["In 1992 a Swiss group gave seven healthy volunteers a single dose of about 3 g by mouth and followed blood for four and a half hours. Plasma glutathione, cysteine and glutamate did not rise significantly, and the authors concluded that oral glutathione's systemic availability was negligible [1] (verified).","In 2015 a US group ran a six-month randomised placebo-controlled trial in 54 healthy non-smokers on 250 or 1,000 mg a day. At six months the high dose had raised glutathione in red cells, plasma and lymphocytes by about 30 to 35%, and in cheek cells by about 260%; the low dose raised whole-blood levels 17%. Levels returned to baseline within a month of stopping. Natural killer cell activity more than doubled on the high dose at three months, a secondary outcome measured in a subset [2] (verified).","The two are less contradictory than they look. One measured a single dose over hours; the other measured daily dosing over months and found the rise built slowly. The likely reading is that oral glutathione does very little acutely but can shift body stores with sustained use, possibly by supplying its building blocks (inferred). The 2015 trial used a branded ingredient whose maker publicised the result [2], which is worth knowing though it does not undermine a randomised design."]},{"h":"Skin lightening: what the trials actually tested","where":"people","body":["The pigment mechanism is plausible and comes from cell work: glutathione shifts melanin production from dark eumelanin toward lighter pheomelanin. The human evidence is thinner than the market for it. A 2018 review identified two oral or sublingual studies and one topical trial, which together showed a good safety profile and appreciable but reversible effects on skin tone [3] (verified).","For intravenous use, the route most associated with lightening, the same review found a single study, and described its design as dubious and its analysis as apparently flawed, which casts doubt on both the efficacy and the adverse effects it reported [3] (verified). The authors called parenteral use for lightening unjustified. Injectable glutathione's approved uses, where it has them, are in severe liver disorders and in preventing chemotherapy nerve damage, not in skin [3].","Reversibility is the practically important word. Every positive result faded once dosing stopped [2,3], so lightening, where it happens, depends on continued use (verified)."]},{"h":"The brain: a reasonable idea that has not cleared a placebo","where":"people","body":["Parkinson's disease involves low glutathione in the brain region that degenerates, which made it an obvious candidate. An open-label 1996 study of nine people reported improvement; a 2009 randomised placebo-controlled pilot in about 20 people found the infusions safe and well tolerated, with a possible symptomatic benefit that did not separate clearly from placebo [4,5] (verified).","The intranasal route was then tried, because the nose offers a partial path to the brain. A phase 2b trial randomised 45 people to placebo or two doses three times daily for 12 weeks. The high dose improved scores from baseline, but neither dose beat placebo, because the placebo group improved too, and everyone worsened in the washout. One high-dose participant developed a fast heart rate and cardiomyopathy and withdrew, with causation unclear [6] (verified). The authors concluded the data did not show superiority over placebo."]},{"h":"Safety: the risk is mostly in the vial, not the molecule","where":"people","body":["Glutathione itself has a long record of tolerability by mouth and on the skin [2,3]. The serious harms in the record come from contaminated injectable product, and they have recurred.","In 2019 the FDA reported seven clinic patients who, within minutes of a 1,400 mg infusion of compounded glutathione, developed nausea, vomiting, chills, body aches and in one case low blood pressure and breathing difficulty requiring hospital care; a separate patient was admitted after a 2,400 mg dose. The agency's testing found bacterial endotoxin in every sample, some at five times the appropriate limit, and traced the source to powder labelled for dietary supplement use that had been repackaged for sterile compounding [7] (verified).","It happened again in 2026. In August the FDA reported at least 30 patients with fever, chills, pain, dizziness and sepsis-like symptoms after intravenous glutathione, some hospitalised, again traced to supplement-grade powder from a single lot, again consistent with endotoxin, with recalls by two pharmacies [8] (verified). In September a further compounder recalled glutathione and two other IV products shipped to patients' homes, after nine adverse event reports [9] (reported).","The pattern is the point. The molecule is cheap and widely sold as a supplement ingredient, the same powder can end up in an injectable, and nothing in a finished vial tells the person receiving it which grade was used. For injectable use, ingredient grade and endotoxin testing matter more than dose (inferred from the two episodes).","What has not been studied: long-term high-dose intravenous use, which is how lightening clinics use it, has no controlled safety data at all [3]."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["In the US, glutathione is sold legally as an oral dietary supplement. Injectable glutathione is not an FDA-approved drug; it is made by compounding pharmacies, and the agency has twice warned them, in 2019 and August 2026, not to use supplement-grade ingredient for injectables [7,8] (verified). Some countries approve injectable glutathione for liver disease and chemotherapy neurotoxicity; none approves it for skin lightening [3]. A Philippine health authority advisory against off-label IV use for lightening dates from 2011 [3].","In sport the substance is not prohibited, but the method can be. WADA prohibits intravenous infusions or injections of more than 100 mL in total per 12 hours outside hospital treatment, surgery or clinical investigation [10] (verified). A typical IV drip exceeds that; a small push may not."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does raising blood glutathione by 30% change any outcome that matters to health? The 2015 trial measured stores and immune markers, not disease.","Is IV lightening real, and at what cost? One flawed study is the entire controlled record.","Would a precursor do the same job more cheaply and safely? Plausible by mechanism, rarely tested head to head.","Why did placebo do so well in the Parkinson's trial? The authors flagged it as worth studying in its own right."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Witschi et al., Eur J Clin Pharmacol 1992, the systemic availability of oral glutathione","url":"https://link.springer.com/doi/10.1007/BF02284971","year":1992},{"n":2,"t":"Richie et al., Eur J Nutr 2015, randomized controlled trial of oral glutathione on body stores","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00394-014-0706-z","year":2015},{"n":3,"t":"Sonthalia et al., Dermatol Pract Concept 2018, glutathione for skin lightening: regnant myth or evidence-based verity","url":"https://www.dpcj.org/index.php/dpc/article/view/dermatol-pract-concept-articleid-dp0801a04","year":2018},{"n":4,"t":"Hauser et al., Mov Disord 2009, randomized double-blind pilot of IV glutathione in Parkinson's disease","url":"https://healthscholars.usf.edu/usf-parkinsons-disease-and-movement-disorders-center/publications/randomized-double-blind-pilot-evaluation-intravenous-glutathione-parkinsons-disease","year":2009},{"n":5,"t":"Michael J. Fox Foundation, Ask the MD: glutathione and Parkinson's","url":"https://www.michaeljfox.org/index%2Ephp/news/ask-md-glutathione-and-parkinsons","year":2015},{"n":6,"t":"Mischley et al., J Parkinsons Dis 2017, phase IIb intranasal glutathione","url":"https://doi.org/10.3233/JPD-161040","year":2017},{"n":7,"t":"FDA, highlights concerns using dietary ingredient glutathione to compound sterile injectables","url":"https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/fda-highlights-concerns-using-dietary-ingredient-glutathione-compound-sterile-injectables","year":2019},{"n":8,"t":"FDA, reminds compounders not to use dietary supplement grade glutathione in injectables","url":"https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/fda-reminds-compounders-not-use-dietary-supplement-grade-glutathione-injectables","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"Newsweek, at-home IV recall warns of endotoxin risk","url":"https://www.newsweek.com/at-home-iv-recall-warns-of-potentially-fatal-endotoxin-risk-12436818","year":2026},{"n":10,"t":"German Sport University Cologne, WADA M2 chemical and physical manipulation (IV infusion limit)","url":"https://www.dshs-koeln.de/en/universitaet/organisation/institute-einrichtungen/department-of-biochemistry/doping-substances/prohibited-classes-of-substances-and-methods/m2-chemical-and-physical-manipulation","year":2026}]},"humanin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and the part of its address nobody has settled","where":"lab","body":["It was found in 2001 by a cell-survival screen: material from the unaffected occipital cortex of a person who had died with sporadic Alzheimer's disease was tested for genes that kept neurons alive against Alzheimer's-type insults, and one short reading frame kept surviving [1] (verified, as described in a later review). That reading frame sits inside the gene for the large ribosomal RNA of mitochondria, a stretch of DNA not expected to code for a protein at all.","That location creates a real puzzle. Mitochondria and the rest of the cell read slightly different genetic codes, so the same sequence gives a 21-residue peptide if translated inside mitochondria and a 24-residue one if translated in the cytoplasm. Both forms are active and the last three residues appear not to matter [1] (verified, in cells). The nuclear genome also carries several near-copies of the same sequence, remnants of mitochondrial DNA that migrated into the chromosomes long ago. Which source actually produces the peptide measured in blood, and from which tissue, has not been resolved [1] (verified as open).","So the familiar line, a peptide encoded by mitochondrial DNA, is the leading hypothesis rather than a settled fact. It also means two different peptide lengths share one name, and listings rarely say which is present (inferred)."]},{"h":"One small peptide, five reported partners","where":"cells","body":["The card names Bax and two receptor systems. The fuller picture is that humanin has been reported to bind at least five different things, which is unusual for a peptide this size and worth sitting with.","Inside the cell it binds inactive Bax and prevents the shape change that sends Bax to the mitochondria; it also binds Bid and its cleaved form tBid, which normally help Bax assemble into pores; and it binds IGFBP-3, a growth-factor binding protein that can itself trigger cell death [1] (verified, in cells). Outside the cell it acts on the formyl peptide receptor FPRL1, and on a three-part cytokine-type receptor assembled from CNTFR, WSX-1 and gp130 that switches on JAK2 and STAT3 [1] (verified, in cells).","One detail undercuts the tidy version of this story: neuroprotection persisted when FPRL1 was knocked down, so the receptor account is incomplete [1] (verified). Many binding partners can mean a genuinely central signal, or a sticky peptide that registers activity in whatever assay it is dropped into. The literature has not separated those readings (inferred), which is one reason an effect shown in one tissue does not reliably carry to another."]},{"h":"Most of the animal evidence was not collected with humanin","where":"animals","body":["This is the single most important thing the card cannot fit, and it changes how every quoted animal result should be read. A single-residue variant, serine 14 swapped for glycine, known as S14G or HNG, is reported to be more than a thousand times more potent at protecting cells than the natural sequence [1] (verified as claimed, though the review attributes that figure to a citation that does not support it, which is worth noting). Because it is so much stronger, HNG became the workhorse of the field.","The consequence is that the headline mouse experiments used the analogue. In the 2020 lifespan study, middle-aged female mice received HNG at 4 milligrams per kilogram by abdominal injection twice weekly from 18 months of age for 14 months, in a survival cohort of 100 animals [2] (verified, in animals). The metabolic work in rats that produced the insulin-sensitivity finding infused peptide directly into the brain ventricles during a clamp procedure, with the effect traced to hypothalamic STAT3 [1] (verified, in animals). Neither design resembles a peripheral injection of the natural peptide, and neither tested the natural peptide.","Half-life is given as roughly 30 minutes [2] (reported). No proper pharmacokinetic programme exists for either form, in any species: no bioavailability, no tissue distribution, no clearance route (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"The longevity claim, read against its own best study","where":"animals","body":["The 2020 paper from the USC gerontology group is the most complete test of the longevity idea, and it is more mixed than its title suggests [2].","In worms, extra humanin lengthened mean life from 17.7 to 19.0 days, a small but statistically significant gain, and the gain disappeared in animals lacking daf-16, the worm version of FOXO [2] (verified, in animals). So in the one species where lifespan was extended, the effect required a separate longevity pathway to work through.","In mice, HNG from mid-life did not significantly change lifespan [2] (verified). What it did change was body composition at 28 months, in a small subgroup of five per group: less visceral fat, more lean mass, unchanged subcutaneous fat, with significantly lower circulating IGF-I and food intake no different from controls [2]. The authors suggest the dose may have been too low or started too late. Either way, the mammalian lifespan result is negative, and it is rarely quoted that way.","The cost side appeared in both species. Worms overexpressing humanin were smaller, leaner and less fertile; transgenic mice were about 12% shorter, 10% lighter, and had litters 46.5% smaller at 28 days [2] (verified). The authors read this as the familiar trade-off between reproduction and longevity. It is also a reminder that this is a growth-suppressing signal, not a tonic."]},{"h":"The human record, which is association only","where":"people","body":["No person has ever been given humanin or any analogue of it in a published study. There is no clinical trial, no case series, no human pharmacokinetic measurement, and no safety dataset of any kind (verified as absent; the review itself states that pharmacokinetic and safety data for long-term use are still needed [1]).","The human data that exist are measurements of peptide already in people. Blood levels fall with age in humans and mice, and growth hormone or IGF-1 treatment lowers them [1] (verified). Children of centenarians in an Ashkenazi Jewish cohort had higher circulating levels than age-matched controls, with 18 offspring against 19 controls [2] (verified). In newborns, cord-blood levels tracked mitochondrial DNA copy number across 73 samples [2]. Spinal fluid levels were lower in Alzheimer's disease than in controls, in a comparison of three or four people per group depending on whether the text or the figure legend is read [2] (verified, including the inconsistency).","Those are the numbers behind the longevity reputation: a handful of small cohorts, measuring a natural peptide, in people who were not taking anything. A correlation between high natural levels and long-lived families does not establish that injecting the peptide reproduces whatever the correlation reflects (inferred). It is just as consistent with high levels being a marker of healthier mitochondria rather than a cause of them."]},{"h":"Safety: unexamined, with one specific question that will not go away","where":"mixed","body":["There are no toxicology studies: no acute, no repeat-dose, no genotoxicity, no reproductive, no carcinogenicity work, for either the natural peptide or the analogue (verified as absent). The 14-month mouse dosing study reported no safety outcomes beyond survival and body composition [2].","The question the biology raises is cancer, and it cuts both ways, which is why it stays open. A peptide whose core action is blocking programmed cell death is, in principle, doing for a tumour cell what it does for a neuron. Some authors have proposed it as an oncopeptide, pointing to upregulation of the mitochondrial 16S rRNA region in non-Hodgkin lymphoma, detection in serum from some people with cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, and higher isoform messenger RNA in chemotherapy-resistant stomach tumour tissue [1] (reported). Against that, the analogue delayed tumour growth in medulloblastoma and neuroblastoma models [1] (reported). The reviewers' own conclusion was that the evidence is not conclusive [1] (verified).","The reproductive findings deserve to be read as safety data rather than curiosities. Smaller litters in transgenic mice and reduced reproductive output in worms are the clearest biological effects in the whole file [2]. Nothing is known about what a growth- and IGF-suppressing signal does in a person, at any exposure."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["No approved product anywhere, no registered clinical programme found, and it was not among the peptides nominated for or reviewed under the US compounding lists in 2026 (verified as of October 2026). It is not named on the 2026 WADA Prohibited List, which leaves it inside the catch-all class for pharmacological substances with no government approval for human use, prohibited at all times [3] (inferred from the class wording). Note that the related mitochondrial peptide MOTS-c is named explicitly on that list as an AMPK activator [3], so the family has regulators' attention.","Which gene actually makes the circulating peptide, and in which tissue? Until that is answered, the claim that this is a mitochondrial hormone rests on sequence location rather than demonstrated biology.","Is the 21-residue or the 24-residue form the relevant molecule, and which is in a given vial? No identity testing in general circulation distinguishes them.","Does the natural sequence do anything the analogue does? Nearly all the potent results used the analogue, and it is more than a thousandfold stronger in cells.","If high natural levels mark long-lived families, is the peptide a cause or a readout? Nothing distinguishes these yet, and the mouse lifespan result argues against the simple causal reading.","Does blocking apoptosis for weeks at a time carry an oncological cost? Unstudied, arguable in both directions, and the one question this mechanism cannot avoid."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Gong, Tas and Muzumdar, Frontiers in Endocrinology, humanin and age-related diseases","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fendo.2014.00210/pdf","year":2014},{"n":2,"t":"Yen et al., Aging (Albany NY), humanin as a regulator of lifespan and healthspan","url":"https://www.aging-us.com/article/103534/text","year":2020},{"n":3,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"bam-15":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The problem it was designed to solve","where":"cells","body":["Uncoupling has an unhappy history. In the 1930s doctors prescribed 2,4-dinitrophenol for weight loss; it worked, and it was abandoned after cataracts appeared in a share of patients and deaths followed. US regulators declared it unfit for human consumption in 1938. The mechanism of the harm is the same as the mechanism of the benefit: energy leaves as heat instead of becoming ATP, so metabolic rate rises, and at higher doses body temperature runs away and death typically follows cardiac arrest [1] (verified).","The design brief for this compound was therefore narrow: uncouple mitochondria without the off-target effects that make DNP and the laboratory uncoupler FCCP dangerous. A 2013 screen identified it as a protonophore that carries protons into the mitochondrial matrix bypassing ATP synthase, and the key reported difference was that it does not depolarise the plasma membrane. Against FCCP, described as similarly potent, it produced a higher maximum respiration rate in cultured cells and was less toxic to them [2] (verified, cells). The same paper reported that it was active in living mice and protected them dose-dependently from acute kidney injury caused by interrupted blood flow [2] (verified, animals).","That is the whole safety argument in outline, and it is a comparative one: safer than a compound that killed people, in cells and in mice. It is not a demonstration of safety in humans, and no human study exists."]},{"h":"The main obesity study, in its own numbers","where":"animals","body":["The 2020 paper is the source of nearly everything quoted about this compound, and the details matter because they are often dropped. Male mice, in two designs: prevention, with the compound mixed into a western diet at 0.05, 0.10 or 0.15% by weight for eight days; and reversal, four weeks of western diet to make the mice obese, then five weeks of treatment at 0.10% [3] (verified).","Prevention: the lowest dose prevented more than half of fat gain and the two higher doses prevented it entirely, with food intake and lean mass unchanged, glucose tolerance preserved and fasting glucose and insulin lower [3] (verified, animals).","Reversal: treated mice ended about 15% lighter than diet controls, the difference almost entirely fat, with lean mass and food intake unchanged. Faecal fat excretion did not change, which rules out the simplest alternative explanation that the compound was blocking fat absorption. Liver triglycerides returned to the level of mice on ordinary chow. Glucose intolerance and high insulin reversed within three weeks, and clamp studies showed more glucose taken up by muscle and fat and better suppression of fatty acid release [3] (verified, animals).","On energy use: a single oral dose raised fat oxidation by 51%, and oxygen consumption rose about 15% during the animals' active phase, with the respiratory exchange ratio falling and locomotor activity unchanged [3] (verified). So the weight loss came from burning more, not moving more or eating less, which is what the mechanism predicts.","The temperature question, which is the one that matters given DNP: body temperature did not change up to 200 mg per kg by mouth [3] (verified, mice, acute). That is the single most important number in the compound's file."]},{"h":"What the body does with it, and why mouse doses do not transfer","where":"animals","body":["In mice, oral bioavailability was about 67%, a 10 mg per kg oral dose reached a peak blood concentration of about 8.2 micromolar, the half-life was about 1.7 hours, and distribution was mainly to the liver [3] (verified).","Two of those numbers constrain everything. A 1.7-hour half-life means exposure is brief and spiky from a single dose, which is why the mouse studies delivered it continuously in food rather than as discrete doses [3] (inferred from the study design). And liver-weighted distribution means the liver sees far more than other tissues, which fits the liver fat and insulin results and makes the liver the organ to watch (inferred).","The authors themselves named the short half-life and low solubility as limitations for development [3] (verified as their stated view). Nothing in the record describes human pharmacokinetics, human bioavailability, or what a human tablet or capsule would need to look like. There is no established human dose, and mouse diet-admixture doses cannot be converted into one."]},{"h":"The other animal findings","where":"animals","body":["Kidney: protection against acute ischaemia-reperfusion injury in mice, dose-dependent, in the original 2013 paper [2] (verified).","Ageing muscle: a 2022 study in aged obese mice, reported in a cachexia and sarcopenia journal, found over 20% fat loss alongside roughly 8% more muscle mass and about 40% more strength, with the animals more physically active; the press account gave no doses or duration, and reduced inflammation and mitochondrial turnover were described without figures [4] (reported). Gaining muscle while losing fat is unusual enough to deserve independent confirmation before it is treated as established.","A 2025 preprint reports improved skeletal muscle function and mitochondrial respiration in a sarcopenia model [5] (reported; a preprint has not been peer reviewed, and it is cited here as such)."]},{"h":"Safety: what was measured, and the shape of what was not","where":"animals","body":["The 2020 study ran a reasonable panel for a mouse study: no change in body temperature, ALT, AST, glutamate dehydrogenase, creatine kinase or creatinine; triglycerides fell; blood urea nitrogen rose about 22% but stayed within the normal range; no ketosis. Liver glutathione rose and oxidised lipids and inflammatory eicosanoids fell [3] (verified, mice, up to about five to six weeks).","What is absent is everything a drug needs before people take it: no study beyond a few weeks in any species, no genotoxicity, no reproductive or developmental work, no carcinogenicity study, no second species, no eye examinations in a drug class whose predecessor caused cataracts, and no human trial of any kind (verified as absent from the published record).","The cataract point is worth stating plainly rather than leaving implied. DNP's human harms included cataracts at doses below the lethal range [1]. No published work on this compound reports lens examination. That is not evidence of a problem; it is an untested question in exactly the place the class's history points (inferred).","One structural caution: the therapeutic margin for an uncoupler is the gap between burning more fuel and overheating. In mice that gap looked wide, with no temperature change at twenty times the energy-expenditure dose [3]. Nobody knows what it is in a human, and a short half-life cuts both ways, limiting accumulation but making exposure sensitive to dose and formulation (inferred)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"lab","body":["The compound comes from an academic network spanning Virginia Tech, the University of Virginia, UNSW Sydney, the University of Sydney and the Garvan Institute, with a company among the author affiliations on the 2020 paper [3] (verified). The ageing muscle work came from a separate US group [4], which is the closest thing to independent corroboration in the file.","There is no clinical programme. Commentary following the mouse work described progress toward a possible clinical path as slow, with no human data [6] (reported). Six years after the main paper, that is still the position."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Preclinical. No approval anywhere, no registered human trial found, no investigational new drug programme announced. Material sold under this name is a research chemical, and it is a small molecule rather than a peptide despite where it is usually shelved.","In sport it is not named on the WADA Prohibited List; as a substance with no approval for human therapeutic use it falls under S0, prohibited at all times, and metabolic modulators are separately addressed as a class (inferred from the list's wording)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What is the therapeutic margin in a human? This is the whole question for an uncoupler, and the only data are mouse body temperatures.","Does the eye tolerate it? The class's defining human harm has not been looked for in this compound.","Can muscle really be gained while fat is lost? One study reports it; it needs replication before it is repeated as fact.","What happens past six weeks, and in a second species? Nothing longer or wider has been published.","How would a human dose even be set, given 1.7-hour kinetics, poor solubility and liver-weighted distribution? The authors flagged these as development problems, and they have not been solved in public."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"ATSDR, Toxicological Profile for Dinitrophenols, chapter on public health and 1930s weight-loss use","url":"https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/toxprofiles/tp64-c1.pdf","year":1995},{"n":2,"t":"Kenwood et al., Mol Metab 2013, identification of a novel mitochondrial uncoupler without plasma membrane depolarisation (abstract via Bioblast)","url":"https://bioblast.at/index.php/Kenwood_2013_Mol_Metab","year":2013},{"n":3,"t":"Alexopoulos et al., Nat Commun 2020, mitochondrial uncoupler BAM15 reverses diet-induced obesity and insulin resistance in mice","url":"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10203598","year":2020},{"n":4,"t":"AZoLifeSciences, report of Dantas et al., J Cachexia Sarcopenia Muscle 2022, uncoupler prevents sarcopenic obesity in elderly mice","url":"https://www.azolifesciences.com/news/20220407/A-mitochondrial-uncoupler-prevents-sarcopenic-obesity-in-elderly-mice.aspx","year":2022},{"n":5,"t":"Preprint (bioRxiv 2025), mitochondrial uncoupler BAM15 improves skeletal muscle function and mitochondrial respiration in sarcopenia (not peer reviewed)","url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.10.30.685477.full.pdf","year":2025},{"n":6,"t":"Fight Aging, more on the safe mitochondrial uncoupling compound BAM15","url":"https://www.fightaging.org/?p=49911","year":2022}]},"eloralintide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Why a selective amylin drug, and what that choice is betting on","where":"mixed","body":["Amylin is a hormone released from the same beta cells as insulin. Its receptors are not a single protein: they are the calcitonin receptor paired with one of three accessory proteins called RAMPs, and the calcitonin receptor alone is a separate target again. The first long-acting amylin drug in the current wave, cagrilintide, hits amylin and calcitonin receptors more or less equally. Eloralintide was built to do the opposite, favouring the amylin receptors over the plain calcitonin receptor [1] (verified that selectivity was the design aim).","The discovery work tested the molecule in cells carrying rat or human amylin-1, amylin-3 or calcitonin receptors, then in rats and monkeys, before moving to people [1] (verified). The bet behind selectivity is that fullness and slower stomach emptying come mostly from the amylin receptors in the brainstem, while some of the nausea and taste aversion come from broader calcitonin receptor activation (inferred; the rat taste-aversion comparison on the card is the main published support, and nobody has shown the same separation in humans).","Whether the bet pays off clinically is still open. Nausea was the single most common adverse event in the phase 2 trial [3], so selectivity did not remove the class's main side effect; what the trial suggests is that it can be managed by dosing speed (see below)."]},{"h":"The human record","where":"people","body":["Phase 1, about 100 people over 12 weeks of weekly multiple ascending doses: weight fell between 2.6% and 11.3% depending on dose, with diarrhoea in about 10% and fewer reporting nausea or vomiting [2,4] (reported). For a 12-week study that is a steep curve, comparable to early data for the incretin drugs.","Phase 2, 263 adults with obesity or overweight and no diabetes, 48 weeks, seven arms: placebo, fixed 1, 3, 6 or 9 mg, and two escalation schemes (6 then 9 mg; 3 then 6 then 9 mg). Baseline weight averaged 109 kg and 78% of participants were women [3,5] (verified).","Weight change on the efficacy estimand was 9.5% on 1 mg, 12.4% on 3 mg, 17.6% on 6 mg, 20.1% on 9 mg, 19.9% on 6 then 9, and 16.4% on 3 then 6 then 9, against 0.4% on placebo [3,5] (verified). Counting everyone regardless of whether they stayed on treatment, the 9 mg figure falls to 17.5% and placebo rises to 2.3% [3]. At 9 mg, 57% lost at least 20% of body weight and 19% lost at least 30% [3].","Body composition was measured: 60 to 70% of the weight lost was fat [3] (reported). HbA1c fell in every active arm against placebo even though nobody had diabetes, and fasting glucose, HDL and triglycerides improved significantly in the 9 mg and escalation arms [3] (reported). The primary endpoint was not adjusted for multiple comparisons across seven arms [5], which is normal for a dose-finding study but means individual arm differences should be read as estimates."]},{"h":"Safety: the dose-speed finding, and the signals that need checking","where":"people","body":["Overall adverse events were almost identical between drug and placebo, 81% against 79%, and serious adverse events were 5.3% against 5.4% with no deaths [3] (verified). That near-match partly reflects how often placebo groups report symptoms in a year-long trial, so the per-symptom picture is more informative.","Nausea (33%) and fatigue (27%) were the most common events across the pooled drug arms [3]. Gastrointestinal events clustered in the 6 mg, 9 mg and 6 then 9 arms; the slow 3 then 6 then 9 escalation had rates close to placebo and still produced 16% weight loss [3,5] (verified). That is the most practically useful finding in the trial: speed of escalation, not final dose, drove most of the stomach trouble.","Fatigue is the less familiar signal. It is not a prominent effect of the incretin drugs, it was common here, and all four severe gastrointestinal events were reported alongside fatigue [3] (reported). Why an amylin agonist would cause tiredness has not been explained in anything published.","The card's mentions of hair loss at 9 mg and a slower heart rate could not be confirmed in the trial reports accessible for this review; neither appears in the sponsor's release or the conference coverage [3,5]. They may sit in the full paper's supplementary tables, which could not be opened. They are flagged here as unconfirmed rather than repeated."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"people","body":["The C20 fatty diacid lets it ride on albumin for once-weekly dosing. No human half-life has been published in a source accessible for this review, and the sponsor has not stated one; the phase 1 paper is the place it would appear [2]. Nothing is known publicly about how it is cleared or whether kidney or liver impairment changes exposure."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["Every study, from receptor assays to phase 2, was designed and funded by the developer [1,2,3]. The phase 2 paper went through peer review at The Lancet [5]. No independent trial exists, which is the normal state for a drug at this stage and also means the safety picture rests on about 360 treated people in total."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Investigational everywhere. The phase 3 programme, named ENLIGHTEN, opened in 2026: trials in obesity without diabetes, obesity with type 2 diabetes, and obstructive sleep apnoea, the last planning 800 participants for up to 64 weeks of treatment against placebo [6] (verified). A separate phase 2 is testing it alone and with tirzepatide in people with type 2 diabetes [5].","In sport it falls under WADA's S0 class for substances with no regulatory approval, prohibited at all times (inferred from the class definition; it is not named on the list)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does amylin selectivity matter in humans, or does the escalation schedule explain the tolerability on its own?","Why fatigue? It was common, it tracked with the severe events, and no mechanism has been proposed.","What do the full safety tables show on heart rate and hair? Phase 3, with thousands of participants, will settle whether those are real signals.","How does it combine with an incretin drug? The rationale is two separate fullness pathways; the combination trial is where that gets tested."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Lilly investigators, 2025, eloralintide (LY3841136), a novel amylin receptor agonist: from discovery to clinical proof of concept","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41109426/","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"Lilly investigators, 2026, eloralintide, a selective long-acting amylin receptor agonist: phase 1 proof of concept","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41559929/","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Healio, report of Billings et al., Lancet 2025, eloralintide phase 2 (ObesityWeek presentation)","url":"https://www.healio.com/news/endocrinology/20251210/novel-amylin-receptor-agonist-induces-clinically-meaningful-weight-loss","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Fierce Biotech, eloralintide phase 1 data ahead of ADA 2025","url":"https://www.fiercebiotech.com/biotech/eli-lillys-phase-1-amylin-data-impress-analysts-pairing-11-weight-loss-tolerable-profile","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"Eli Lilly, eloralintide phase 2 results release, Nov 2025 (paper: Billings et al., Lancet 2025, doi 10.1016/S0140-6736(25)02155-5)","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/node/53246","year":2025},{"n":6,"t":"EU Clinical Trials Information System, ENLIGHTEN-3: eloralintide in obstructive sleep apnoea and obesity","url":"https://www.ctis.eu/trial/2025-523769-11-00","year":2026}]},"hcg":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The study behind the fertility-preservation use, in detail","where":"people","body":["The practice of adding this hormone to testosterone therapy rests mainly on one small, well-designed study, and its design is unusually direct: it measured hormone concentrations inside the testis rather than in blood.","Twenty-nine healthy men aged 18 to 45 all received testosterone enanthate 200 mg weekly, plus one of saline placebo or one of three doses given subcutaneously every other day, for three weeks [1] (verified). Testicular fluid was obtained by fine-needle aspiration of both testes at baseline and on day 21.","The placebo arm shows why this matters. Testosterone therapy alone suppressed intratesticular testosterone to about 6% of baseline, a 94% fall [1] (verified). That is the mechanism of testosterone-induced infertility stated numerically, and it is far more dramatic than most discussion conveys.","Against that, the three doses preserved intratesticular testosterone at roughly 75%, 93% and 126% of baseline, with a linear relationship between dose and concentration, and none of the three differing significantly from baseline [1] (verified). Pituitary hormones were suppressed to about 5% and 3% of baseline across the study, as expected on exogenous testosterone.","The honest limitation is the authors' own: the intratesticular testosterone threshold needed to maintain sperm production is unknown [1] (verified). The study establishes that this hormone preserves the local concentration in a dose-dependent way. It does not establish how much is enough, because nobody knows what the target is."]},{"h":"What the dose-response actually implies, including a caution","where":"people","body":["The linear dose-response is the useful finding, and it cuts in two directions.","At the lowest dose, serum testosterone was similar to placebo, meaning the local effect was achieved without adding appreciably to circulating levels. At the two higher doses, serum testosterone rose above the normal reference range [1] (verified). The top dose also pushed intratesticular testosterone 26% above its own baseline.","So more is not simply better. Higher amounts overshoot the local target and add systemic testosterone on top of a replacement dose, which is the plausible route to the oestrogen-related complaints people report, since part of any extra testosterone is converted to oestradiol (inferred, consistent with the measured serum rise).","This is the reverse of the intuition that usually governs how this hormone is used alongside testosterone, where the aim is often framed as keeping the testes as stimulated as possible. The data suggest a target range rather than a maximum, and that the lowest effective amount is the one that leaves circulating levels unchanged (inferred).","A three-week study in 29 healthy men cannot settle long-term practice, and the supporting evidence beyond it is small and mostly retrospective (verified, as the card records)."]},{"h":"Why this one left the compounding world, and what that did to supply","where":"people","body":["The regulatory history here is different from every other compound in this catalog, and it explains the supply problems better than any market story.","On 23 March 2020, a set of products approved as drugs transitioned to licensure as biologics under a statutory provision that deemed their existing approvals to be biologics licences. This hormone was one of four bulk substances that outsourcing facilities had been using and that were affected, alongside hyaluronidase, follicle stimulating hormone and menotropins [2] (verified).","The consequence is categorical rather than discretionary: once a product is licensed as a biologic, it is no longer eligible for the compounding exemptions that pharmacies rely on [2] (verified). This was not a safety finding about the hormone. It was a reclassification that removed an entire supply channel overnight.","What remains is narrower. A guidance on mixing, diluting or repackaging licensed biological products outside an approved label still applies, under conditions that guidance sets out [2]. That is a different and more limited activity than compounding from bulk substance.","The practical result, reported into 2026, is that approved products exist but supply has been patchy, with the hormone appearing on pharmacy shortage listings (reported, as the card records). The useful lesson for reading this catalog: a compound can become hard to obtain for reasons that have nothing to do with its evidence or its safety, and conversely, availability says nothing about either.","Two forms exist, one purified from urine and one made recombinantly, which is relevant because they have different manufacturing supply chains and are not affected identically by any single disruption (inferred)."]},{"h":"The weight-loss claim, and why it is the clearest refuted claim in this catalog","where":"people","body":["Most compounds here suffer from absent evidence. This one has the rarer distinction of a claim that was actually tested and rejected, repeatedly, over decades.","The weight-loss protocol dates to a 1954 proposal pairing the hormone with a roughly 500 calorie daily diet (verified, as the card's timeline records). The approved labelling states that it has not been shown to be effective adjunctive therapy for obesity, and the explanation for any weight lost is the calorie restriction, not the injection (verified).","Regulators and consumer-protection authorities acted against sellers of over-the-counter homeopathic versions of these drops in 2011 (verified, as the card records).","The distinction worth preserving: a 500 calorie daily diet produces weight loss in anyone, which is exactly why the protocol appeared to work and why it persisted. Attributing that loss to the injection is the error, and it is an error that was identified, tested and formally rejected rather than merely doubted (verified).","This matters as a reference case. When people ask why a given peptide's enthusiastic reports should not settle the question, this is the cleanest available answer: a protocol with decades of satisfied users, a plausible hormonal story, and a controlled answer that the hormone contributed nothing."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["What intratesticular testosterone concentration is actually needed to maintain sperm production? The dose-finding study's authors named this as the central unknown, and it is still unresolved. Without it, dose selection for fertility preservation is empirical.","Does preserved local testosterone translate into preserved fertility over years, not months? The supporting evidence is a small retrospective series, and the mechanism is a surrogate for the outcome people care about.","Is the lowest dose the right one? It achieved the local effect without raising serum testosterone, which on the available data looks preferable to the higher amounts, but no trial has compared outcomes across doses.","What is the long-term oestradiol consequence of adding this to a full replacement dose? Mechanistically predictable, poorly quantified.","Does the urine-derived form differ from the recombinant one in practice? They have different manufacturing routes and the comparison is rarely addressed.","Will supply stabilise? The constraint is regulatory classification rather than demand or evidence, so it will resolve on a regulatory timeline or not at all.","For men using it to restart the axis after suppression: what is the actual recovery curve, and does early use change it? Widely practised, thinly studied."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Coviello et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab, low-dose hCG maintains intratesticular testosterone in gonadotropin-suppressed men","url":"https://static.hormonebalance.org/images/documents/Coviello%2005%20hCG%20maintains%20Test%20JCEM.pdf","year":2005},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, notice to compounders on changes affecting compounding as of March 23, 2020","url":"https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/notice-compounders-changes-affect-compounding-march-23-2020","year":2020}]},"mazdutide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and why it reached China first","where":"people","body":["The molecule was designed at Lilly under the code LY3305677 and built on oxyntomodulin, a gut hormone that already touches both the GLP-1 and glucagon receptors [8] (verified). Lilly then licensed the China rights to Innovent, which ran it as IBI362. That split is the key to reading the record: nearly all of the published human data, every phase 3 trial so far and the only approval come from the Chinese programme, while Lilly has run a smaller parallel set of US studies at higher doses [5,6] (verified).","The order of events is unusual for a drug of this class. Most incretin drugs are approved first in the US or Europe and then elsewhere. This one was approved in China in June 2025, before any US phase 3 had read out, which makes it the first marketed GLP-1/glucagon dual agonist anywhere [8] (verified). Anyone outside China using it is using a drug that has an approval, just not in their jurisdiction."]},{"h":"Mechanism, and the trade the glucagon half makes","where":"mixed","body":["The GLP-1 half does what the class is known for: less hunger, slower stomach emptying, more insulin when glucose is high. The glucagon half is the interesting one, because on its face it should push the wrong way. Glucagon raises blood glucose by releasing it from the liver. The design bet is that, paired with GLP-1, the glucose-raising effect is cancelled out while the other glucagon effects survive: more fat burned in the liver and higher energy expenditure (inferred from the receptor pharmacology; the human trials measured the outcomes, not the energy expenditure directly).","The outcome that best supports the bet is liver fat. In GLORY-1, among people who started with a fatty liver, liver fat fell by about 63% on 4 mg and 73% on 6 mg at 48 weeks, against an 8% rise on placebo; in those starting above 10% liver fat it fell by about 66% and 80% [1] (verified). Weight loss alone does reduce liver fat, so this cannot be credited wholly to the glucagon arm, but the size of the drop relative to the weight lost is what the glucagon hypothesis predicts (inferred).","The glucose side also held. In type 2 diabetes the drug lowered HbA1c more than both dulaglutide and semaglutide in head-to-head trials [3,4], which is the clearest human evidence that the glucagon component does not undo glycaemic control at these doses (verified)."]},{"h":"The human record, trial by trial","where":"people","body":["Phase 2 in Chinese adults with obesity: 248 people, 3, 4.5 or 6 mg against placebo for 24 weeks. Weight fell 6.7%, 10.4% and 11.3% against 1.0% on placebo, with no treatment discontinuations for adverse events reported [7] (reported, from the sponsor's summary of the paper).","GLORY-1, the phase 3 that supported approval: 610 Chinese adults, 4 or 6 mg weekly against placebo for 48 weeks. Weight fell 12.1% and 14.8% against 0.5% on placebo (efficacy estimand); counting everyone randomised regardless of adherence, 11.0% and 14.0% [1] (verified). Half of the 6 mg group lost at least 15%. Baseline weight averaged 87 kg, lighter than most Western obesity trials, which matters when comparing percentages across programmes (inferred).","GLORY-2, the higher dose: 9 mg weekly, reached by 3 mg steps every four weeks, for 60 weeks. Weight fell 16.7% against 1.5% on placebo, and 10.7% in the subgroup with diabetes [2] (verified). That is the dose now under review in China.","Diabetes: DREAMS-1 enrolled 320 people on diet and exercise alone; HbA1c fell 1.58 and 2.02 points on 4 and 6 mg against 0.25 on placebo at 24 weeks [3]. DREAMS-2 enrolled 731 on metformin-based therapy against dulaglutide 1.5 mg; HbA1c fell 1.61 and 1.66 against 1.36, and weight 6.6% and 8.5% against 2.8% [3] (reported, sponsor release of the Nature papers). DREAMS-3 put 6 mg against semaglutide 1 mg in 349 people with diabetes and obesity: HbA1c down 2.03 against 1.84 points, weight down 10.3% against 6.0% at 32 weeks [4] (reported).","The US data are smaller and push the dose much higher. A Lilly phase 1 took 32 people to a 16 mg target over 20 weeks and reported 20 to 21% weight loss with no serious adverse events [6] (verified). The US phase 2, published in August 2026, randomised 179 people to 3 to 6, 10 or 16 mg; at 32 weeks weight fell 7.3%, 15.6% and 18.1% against 0.9% on placebo [5] (reported). So the Chinese label doses sit at the low end of what the molecule has been pushed to."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"people","body":["The C20 fatty diacid binds albumin, which is what stretches dosing to once a week [8]. The phase 1 papers report human pharmacokinetics, but the full texts could not be opened for this review, so the half-life on the card stays tagged as reported rather than verified here.","The practically important kinetic fact is about titration, not half-life. Every programme escalates in steps of roughly four weeks, and the adverse event curves peak during those steps and fall after [2,3] (verified). The GLORY-2 heart rate data show the same shape: a mean rise peaking at about 7 beats per minute during titration and settling to about 2.6 by week 60 [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"Safety: what has been measured, and what the higher doses cost","where":"people","body":["At 4 to 6 mg the profile looks like the GLP-1 class: nausea, diarrhoea and vomiting, mostly mild to moderate and concentrated in titration, no severe hypoglycaemia in the diabetes trials, and grade 1 to 2 hypoglycaemia similar to dulaglutide [1,3] (verified).","The higher doses change the picture. At 9 mg in GLORY-2, vomiting was reported in 53%, nausea in 47% and diarrhoea in 39% [2] (verified). In the US phase 2, discontinuation for adverse events reached 20% at 16 mg, mostly gastrointestinal [5] (reported). The pattern is consistent across both programmes: weight loss keeps rising with dose, and so does the share of people who cannot tolerate it.","What has not been looked at yet: no cardiovascular outcome trial has reported, no trial longer than about 60 weeks has published, and none has been run in a non-Asian population at the approved doses. The glucagon component's long-term effects on lean mass, amino acid handling and heart rhythm in humans remain open (inferred from what is absent in the published record)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["Every trial summarised above was sponsored by Innovent or Lilly, and several numbers here come from company releases rather than the papers themselves [3,4,7]. That is normal for a drug in development, and the main results did pass peer review at NEJM, JAMA, Nature and Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology [1,2,3,5]. No independent trial exists yet."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Approved in China for chronic weight management (June 2025) and for type 2 diabetes (September 2025), at 4 and 6 mg; the 9 mg dose was filed in November 2025 and no approval had been found as of late October 2026 [2,8]. Not approved by the FDA or EMA, and no US submission has been announced.","In sport, GLP-1 and glucagon receptor agonists are not on the WADA Prohibited List, and because the drug has a government approval in China the non-approved substances clause does not apply (inferred from the list's wording)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the glucagon arm add anything beyond more weight loss? The liver fat numbers suggest it does, but no trial has compared it against a pure GLP-1 drug at matched weight loss.","Where is the tolerable ceiling? Chinese labels stop at 6 mg, China is reviewing 9 mg, and US studies went to 16 mg with one in five stopping.","Do the results hold outside China? The US phase 2 suggests yes at higher doses, but no phase 3 has run in a Western population.","What happens to heart rate, lean mass and cardiovascular events over years rather than months? None of these has an outcome trial yet."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"GLORY-1 investigators, N Engl J Med 2025, once-weekly mazdutide in Chinese adults with obesity or overweight (sponsor summary)","url":"https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/phase-3-clinical-study-of-mazdutide-in-chinese-adults-with-overweight-or-obesity-glory-1-published-in-the-new-england-journal-of-medicine-nejm","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"Gao et al., JAMA 2026, GLORY-2: mazdutide 9 mg in adults with obesity (report)","url":"https://www.drugtopics.com/view/mazdutide-shows-weight-reductions-for-patients-with-obesity","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Innovent, DREAMS-1 and DREAMS-2 published in Nature (sponsor release)","url":"https://www.utusan.com.my/pr-newswire-en/2025/12/nature-two-phase-3-clinical-results-of-mazdutide-glp-1-gcg-dual-receptor-agonist-in-chinese-adults-with-type-2-diabetes-have-been-back-to-back-published-in-nature/","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"HCPLive, DREAMS-3: mazdutide versus semaglutide in type 2 diabetes with obesity","url":"https://www.hcplive.com/view/mazdutide-demonstrates-superiority-to-semaglutide-in-type-2-diabetes-and-obesity","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"Lilly US phase 2 (NCT06124807), Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2026, 3 to 16 mg in obesity (report)","url":"https://pharmacally.com/?p=21931","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Bhattachar et al., Diabetes Obes Metab 2025, high-dose phase 1 trial to 16 mg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/dom.70040","year":2025},{"n":7,"t":"Innovent, phase 2 full results in Chinese adults with obesity, Nature Communications (sponsor release)","url":"https://www.biospace.com/innovent-announces-publication-of-mazdutide-ibi362-phase-2-full-results-in-chinese-patients-with-overweight-or-obesity-in-nature-communications","year":2023},{"n":8,"t":"Wikipedia, Mazdutide (structure, CAS, approval history with primary citations)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazdutide","year":2026}]},"orforglipron":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Not a peptide, and why that changes everything downstream","where":"lab","body":["Orforglipron is a small molecule, not a peptide, which is why it survives the gut. A 2020 cryo-EM structure of the human GLP-1 receptor bound to it (then coded LY3502970) shows it sitting in a pocket in the upper part of the receptor, touching the extracellular domain and several helices, rather than where the natural hormone binds [1] (verified).","Two consequences follow from that binding mode. It is a partial agonist biased toward G-protein signalling over beta-arrestin recruitment, which may matter for tolerability and receptor desensitisation, though that link is not proven in people [1] (verified for the bias; inferred for its meaning). And it depends on a tryptophan found only in primate receptors, so it barely acts on rodent receptors [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"What that species quirk did to the safety file","where":"animals","body":["Injectable GLP-1 drugs carry a boxed warning because they caused thyroid C-cell tumours in rodents. Orforglipron produced no tumours in a two-year rat study or a 26-week transgenic mouse study, but the label explains why that is not reassurance: the drug is not pharmacologically active in rats or mice [2] (verified). The tumour test could not have shown a receptor-driven effect. The label keeps the class boxed warning and the contraindication in medullary thyroid cancer and MEN 2, with human relevance undetermined [2] (verified).","This is an unusual position for an approved drug: the standard rodent safety model was blind to its main mechanism, so long-term human surveillance carries more of the weight than usual (inferred). Developmental studies used monkeys and rabbits; rabbit malformations appeared only at about 14 times clinical exposure [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"What the body does with it","where":"people","body":["Bioavailability is about 77% at the 0.8 mg dose, peak levels come 4 to 8 hours after a dose, half-life is about 29 to 49 hours, and about 87% leaves in faeces, under 1% in urine; metabolism runs mainly through the liver enzyme CYP3A4 [2] (verified). Food has no clinically relevant effect at approved strengths [2].","That CYP3A4 dependence is the practical part. Clarithromycin raised exposure about 3.5-fold, so with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors the maximum dose is 9 mg; carbamazepine cut exposure 82% and efavirenz 61%, so strong inducers are to be avoided [2] (verified). It also raised simvastatin acid exposure 2 to 2.5-fold, capping simvastatin at 20 mg, and the label asks for a non-oral or backup contraceptive for 30 days after starting and after every dose step [2] (verified). These interactions are a new problem for the class: injectable peptides largely avoid them (inferred)."]},{"h":"The human record","where":"people","body":["Obesity. ATTAIN-1 randomised 3,127 adults without diabetes for 72 weeks. Mean weight change was minus 7.5%, 8.4% and 11.2% at the three trial doses against minus 2.1% on placebo; at the top dose 55% lost at least 10% and 19% at least 20% [3] (verified). Discontinuation for adverse events was 5 to 10% against 3% [3]. On treatment, the figure rises to about 12.4% [7].","Early type 2 diabetes. ACHIEVE-1 gave it alone to 559 people for 40 weeks; HbA1c fell about 1.2 to 1.5 points from a baseline of 8.0%, against 0.4 on placebo [4] (reported, from a trade summary of the paper).","Head to head. ACHIEVE-3 compared it with oral semaglutide in 1,698 people on metformin for 52 weeks, open-label. At the top doses HbA1c fell 2.2 against 1.4 points and weight 9.2% against 5.3%, but discontinuation for adverse events was roughly double, 9.7% against 4.9% [5] (verified). Better effect, worse tolerability, both real.","Heart. ACHIEVE-4 randomised 2,749 people with type 2 diabetes and raised cardiovascular risk against insulin glargine, open-label. Major cardiovascular events had a hazard ratio of 0.84 (95% CI 0.59 to 1.20), meeting the non-inferiority margin of 1.8 but not showing superiority [6] (verified). All-cause death was lower (hazard ratio 0.43), but that analysis was not protected against chance across multiple tests [6], so it is a signal, not a finding. A dedicated outcomes trial in obesity is ongoing [6]."]},{"h":"Side effects in numbers","where":"people","body":["From the label, at the 5.5, 9 and 17.2 mg tablets against placebo: nausea 26, 34 and 35% against 10%; vomiting 13, 21 and 24% against 4%; constipation 20, 27 and 24% against 9%; diarrhoea 21, 23 and 25% against 11%. Overall, 8% stopped for adverse reactions against 3%, mostly gastrointestinal [2] (verified).","Class warnings apply: pancreatitis, gallbladder disease, kidney injury from dehydration, hypoglycaemia with insulin or sulfonylureas, worsening diabetic retinopathy, and aspiration risk under anaesthesia [2] (verified)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["Discovered by a Japanese company and licensed in 2018 to the current US developer (reported), which ran and reported every phase 3 trial cited here [3,5,6] (verified). The trials are large, registered and published in major journals, which is a different tier of evidence from anything else in this library. Some of the most quoted figures, such as ACHIEVE-4's mortality result and the on-treatment weight figure, come first from company releases, so the peer-reviewed versions are the ones to check (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["US: approved on 1 April 2026 for chronic weight management, label revised July 2026 [2,7] (verified). EU: the CHMP adopted a positive opinion at its 12 to 15 October 2026 meeting for weight management and type 2 diabetes; the European Commission decision follows [8] (verified).","Sport: GLP-1 agonists are not named anywhere on the 2026 Prohibited List, and as an approved drug it falls outside the catch-all non-approved class [9] (verified for the list contents; inferred for its application)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is the thyroid warning relevant to a drug rodents cannot respond to? Only human follow-up can say.","Does the mortality signal in ACHIEVE-4 hold in a trial designed to test it?","How much of the tolerability gap against oral semaglutide is dose escalation speed, and how much is the molecule?","What happens to weight after stopping a daily pill, compared with weekly injections? Not yet reported."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Kawai et al., PNAS, structural basis for GLP-1 receptor activation by LY3502970 (PDB 6XOX)","url":"https://rcsb.org/structure/6xox","year":2020},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, Foundayo (orforglipron) prescribing information, revised July 2026","url":"https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/220934s003lbl.pdf","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Wharton et al., N Engl J Med, ATTAIN-1 (ACC journal scan)","url":"https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/09/24/16/48/attain-1","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Rosenstock et al., N Engl J Med, ACHIEVE-1 in early type 2 diabetes (HCPLive summary)","url":"https://www.hcplive.com/view/achieve-1-oral-glp-1-agonist-orforglipron-shows-strong-hba1c-weight-reductions","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"Eli Lilly, ACHIEVE-3 results against oral semaglutide","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/node/53961","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Eli Lilly, ACHIEVE-4 cardiovascular safety results","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/node/54996","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Eli Lilly, FDA approval of Foundayo for weight management","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/node/54076","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"chemXplore, CHMP October 2026 meeting highlights","url":"https://chemxplore.com/news/chmp-meeting-highlights-october-2026","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025}]},"pe-22-28":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where it came from, and who is behind every result","where":"lab","body":["The story starts with a receptor protein rather than a drug. Sortilin, also called neurotensin receptor 3, is made with a short leading piece, a propeptide, that is cut off as the protein matures. In 2010 a group at the Institute of Molecular and Cellular Pharmacology near Nice showed that a 17-residue stretch of that propeptide, which they named spadin, blocked the TREK-1 potassium channel and behaved like an antidepressant in mice [1] (verified). The idea behind it came from the same group's 2006 finding that mice lacking TREK-1 entirely were resistant to depression-like behaviour across several tests [2] (verified).","PE-22-28 came next, in 2017. The group watched spadin break down in mouse blood, saw that almost all of it was gone within 30 minutes, and designed a shorter seven-residue peptide, GVSWGLR, based on the fragments that survived [3] (verified). The product sold today is that seven-residue molecule.","What is missing from the story is a second laboratory. Every primary result on spadin and its short analogues found in this review traces to the same French academic network, funded by national research agencies, with authors declaring no commercial conflicts [3] (verified). One of the authors held an industrial doctoral fellowship of the French CIFRE type, which by design pairs a student with a company, but no company is named as a developer (reported). Independent replication, which is the normal check on a single group's findings, has not appeared in print as far as could be found (inferred from searches; PubMed itself was not reachable for this review)."]},{"h":"What the two famous numbers actually mean","where":"cells","body":["Two figures travel with this peptide: a potency about 400 times higher than spadin, and a roughly 23-hour half-life. Both come from the 2017 paper, and both say less than they appear to.","The potency is real but specific. In human TREK-1 channels expressed in cultured kidney cells and measured by patch clamp, the concentration that blocked half the current was 0.12 nanomolar for PE-22-28 against 40 to 60 nanomolar for spadin [3] (verified, in cells). That is a measure of how tightly it acts on one channel in a dish. It says nothing about how much reaches the brain after an injection or a nasal spray.","The 23 hours is not a half-life. No one has measured the peptide in blood or tissue over time. What the paper measured was how long the behavioural effect in mice lasted after one dose, and it reported half-effect times of 14, 17, 21 and 23 hours across four analogues, against about 6 hours for spadin [3] (verified). The 23-hour figure belongs to a modified analogue, not necessarily to the plain sequence in a vial. There is no pharmacokinetic data for PE-22-28 in any species: no absorption, distribution, clearance or brain penetration figures (verified as absent).","The mouse doses are worth knowing for scale, not as guidance. Behavioural effects were seen at 3 to 4 micrograms per kilogram by intraperitoneal injection, and in one four-day arm at 1 milligram per kilogram by stomach tube [3] (verified). The paper's own figure caption and main text disagree on one of those doses. There is no established human dose, and no study has tried to derive one."]},{"h":"Mechanism, one step at a time, with the model each step came from","where":"mixed","body":["Step one, the target. TREK-1 is a background potassium channel: when it is open, potassium leaks out and the neuron is harder to fire. Mice bred without it showed less despair-like behaviour, and their serotonin neurons in the brainstem fired more [2] (verified, in animals). That knockout result is the whole rationale. It shows that removing the channel from birth changes behaviour; it does not by itself show that blocking it in an adult does the same thing safely.","Step two, selectivity. At 100 nanomolar, roughly a thousand times its blocking concentration, PE-22-28 did not change currents through the closest relatives TREK-2 and TRAAK, nor through TRESK or TASK-1. At 10 micromolar the analogues did not change the cardiac hERG current, the channel most often behind drug-induced rhythm problems [3] (verified, in cells). That is a clean panel for five channels. It is not a broad screen, and no receptor or enzyme panel was reported.","Step three, behaviour. In male C57BL/6J mice, single doses shortened immobility in the forced swim test, and four days of dosing shortened the delay to eat in a novel environment, a test that usually needs weeks of a conventional antidepressant [3] (verified, in animals). Only male mice of one strain were used.","Step four, new neurons and synapses. Four days of dosing increased markers of new neuron formation in the hippocampus, and in cultured mouse cortical neurons the analogues raised PSD-95, a scaffold protein of excitatory synapses [3] (verified, in animals and cells). The speed is the interesting part, since conventional antidepressants take weeks to do this in mice. The size and durability of the effect beyond the short window studied is unknown."]},{"h":"Safety: the channel does other jobs","where":"animals","body":["There are no toxicology studies of PE-22-28: no acute, no repeat-dose, no genotoxicity, no reproductive studies, in any species (verified as absent from the primary paper and the patent). The patent text describes the peptides as lacking side effects, but reports no measured toxicity to back that [4] (verified).","The more important point is biological. TREK-1 is not a depression switch; it is part of how neurons protect themselves. In the group's own 2004 work, mice lacking TREK-1 were more vulnerable to seizures and to brain damage from reduced blood flow, and less sensitive to some general anaesthetics [5] (verified, in animals). A drug that blocks the channel is, in principle, pushing in the same direction as that knockout. The 2017 paper cites earlier spadin work reporting no harmful effects on pain, ischaemia or the heart, and states the authors could expect the analogues to behave similarly [3]; that is an expectation for this molecule, not a test of it (reported).","So the honest summary is not that it looked safe, but that the specific questions its own biology raises, seizure threshold, stroke outcome and interaction with anaesthesia, have not been asked of this peptide (inferred)."]},{"h":"Product reality and the human record","where":"people","body":["There is no human study of any kind: no trial, no case series, no pharmacokinetic measurement (verified as absent from the literature found; trial registries could not be opened directly for this review). Intranasal use, which is common in practice, has never been tested in any species. The patent lists intravenous, intraperitoneal and oral routes as possibilities, and its examples used injection into the abdomen of mice only [4] (verified).","The patent family is also a reminder that the molecule in commerce is not the one the inventors chose to protect. The US claims cover an alanine-substituted version, AVSWGLR, and its biotinylated form, not the native GVSWGLR sequence [4] (verified). In the 2017 paper that modified version gave the strongest neurogenesis signal [3]. The commercial product is therefore the unprotected, cheaper-to-copy sibling of the lead candidate (inferred).","A seven-residue peptide containing tryptophan is vulnerable to oxidation and light, and spadin itself was shown to be cut apart in blood within half an hour [3]. No stability data for the short analogue in solution, in a vial or in a nasal spray has been published (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Not approved anywhere, not in any registered clinical programme found, and not nominated for or reviewed under the US compounding lists (verified as of October 2026). It is not named on the 2026 WADA Prohibited List, which means it falls under the catch-all S0 class for pharmacological substances with no government approval for human use, prohibited at all times [6] (inferred from the class wording).","Does blocking TREK-1 in an adult human change mood at all? No blocker of this channel has been tested in people for mood.","How much reaches the brain, by any route? Without a single pharmacokinetic measurement, the dose question cannot even be framed.","Does it lower seizure threshold or worsen stroke outcome? The knockout biology says those are the right questions; nobody has asked them of this molecule.","Does anyone outside the original group get the same results? One independent replication of the four-day neurogenesis and behavioural effects would change the weight of the whole file."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Mazella et al., PLoS Biology, spadin, a sortilin-derived peptide targeting TREK-1","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.1000355","year":2010},{"n":2,"t":"Heurteaux et al., Nature Neuroscience, TREK-1 deletion gives a depression-resistant phenotype","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/nn1749","year":2006},{"n":3,"t":"Djillani et al., Frontiers in Pharmacology, shortened spadin analogs with better TREK-1 inhibition and stability","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2017.00643/full","year":2017},{"n":4,"t":"US Patent 11,220,527, peptides derived from the NTSR3 propeptide for treating depression","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11220527","year":2022},{"n":5,"t":"Heurteaux et al., EMBO Journal, TREK-1 in neuroprotection and general anesthesia","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC449762","year":2004},{"n":6,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"survodutide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Why a glucagon arm was added on purpose","where":"lab","body":["Glucagon is the hormone that raises blood sugar, so putting a glucagon agonist in a diabetes drug looks like a contradiction. The logic is that glucagon does two other things as well: it tells the liver to burn stored fat, and it raises resting energy expenditure. Pair it with a GLP-1 agonist strong enough to override the glucose-raising effect, and in principle you get GLP-1 appetite suppression plus a liver-directed fat-burning signal that GLP-1 alone does not have (verified as the stated design rationale).","That is the mechanistic bet, and the trials below are what it bought. The clearest evidence it is a real second mechanism rather than marketing is the liver data: liver fat fell much further than the weight loss alone would predict, and histology changed in a 48-week biopsy trial [1] (verified, in people).","The cost of the bet shows up in the tolerability columns of every trial, and in the fact that the glucagon arm has no long-term human safety precedent. No glucagon receptor agonist has ever been approved for chronic use in people (inferred)."]},{"h":"The liver trial, which is the most interesting dataset","where":"people","body":["A 48-week phase 2 trial randomised 293 adults with biopsy-confirmed MASH to 2.4, 4.8 or 6.0 mg weekly or placebo, with biopsies at the end [1] (verified).","Histological improvement in MASH without worsening fibrosis reached 47%, 62% and 43% across the three doses against 14% on placebo [1]. Fibrosis improved by at least one stage in 34%, 36% and 34% against 22% on placebo [1]. At least a 30% reduction in liver fat by MRI was reached by 63%, 67% and 57% against 14% [1] (verified).","Two features of that table deserve attention. First, the response was not dose-ordered: the middle dose did best on the primary endpoint and the highest dose did worst, which is what tolerability-driven dropout tends to look like rather than a clean dose-response. Second, fibrosis, the endpoint that actually predicts liver outcomes, moved much less than the inflammation and fat endpoints did, and placebo moved too. A 34% versus 22% difference on fibrosis is a far weaker result than 62% versus 14% on MASH activity, and it is the fibrosis number that matters for whether livers fail (inferred).","Gastrointestinal effects in that trial were heavy: nausea 66% against 23%, diarrhoea 49% against 23%, vomiting 41% against 4%, with serious adverse events similar at 8% against 7% [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The two phase 3 obesity trials, and the two sets of numbers each reports","where":"people","body":["SYNCHRONIZE-1 ran 76 weeks across 116 sites in 14 countries, randomising 725 adults with obesity, or overweight with a complication, and no diabetes, 1:1:1 to weekly 3.6 mg, 6.0 mg or placebo, all with lifestyle counselling. Mean age was 47, mean BMI 37.9, mean weight 108.8 kg [2] (verified).","At 76 weeks, mean weight change was minus 12% on 3.6 mg, minus 13% on 6.0 mg and minus 5% on placebo, with at least 5% loss in 73%, 72% and 46% respectively, p at or below 0.0001 for both doses [2] (verified). Note that the higher dose added almost nothing over the lower one, and that the placebo arm lost 5% on lifestyle counselling alone.","SYNCHRONIZE-2 enrolled 752 adults with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of at least 27, same doses, same 76 weeks [3]. Here the reporting difference matters more than the result. Under the treatment-regimen estimand, which counts everyone as randomised including those who stopped, weight change was minus 8.2% and minus 9.8% against minus 3.9% on placebo. Under the efficacy estimand, which asks what happens in people who stay on treatment, the same trial reads minus 10.4% and minus 13.1% against minus 3.1% [3] (verified). Both are correct; the first describes a treatment decision, the second describes a drug. The larger figure is the one that circulates.","HbA1c fell 0.9 and 0.8 percentage points against 0.2 on placebo, at least 5% weight loss was reached by 57.6% and 64.5% against 35.1%, and hsCRP, a general inflammation marker, fell about half against 17% [3] (verified). Weight loss in people with diabetes was smaller than in those without, which is the usual pattern for this class.","Both trials were funded by the manufacturer, published in a major journal and presented at a diabetes meeting [2,3] (verified). One caution for anyone chasing the primary sources: the trial report and its own reference list carry different DOIs for the same paper, so citation details should be checked at the journal [3] (verified as an inconsistency in the reporting)."]},{"h":"Tolerability, in the numbers that decide whether people stay on it","where":"people","body":["In SYNCHRONIZE-1, gastrointestinal adverse events affected 81% on 3.6 mg and 90% on 6.0 mg against 48% on placebo, mostly mild to moderate, with no deaths [2] (verified).","SYNCHRONIZE-2 reported the discontinuation figure that the obesity trial's summary did not: gastrointestinal events alone caused 18% of the 3.6 mg arm and 17.9% of the 6.0 mg arm to stop, against 1.2% on placebo [3] (verified). Roughly one person in six left the trial because of side effects, at both doses. That is high for this class and consistent with the dual mechanism: the glucagon arm does not reduce nausea, and the dose that maximised liver histology in phase 2 was not the top dose.","Serious adverse events were 9.2% and 8.4% against 10.8% on placebo, so no excess there. There was one case of severe hypoglycaemia, in the lower-dose arm, and two major adverse cardiovascular events, both in the lower-dose arm; one case of acute pancreatitis occurred on placebo [3] (verified).","What has not been published is the thing the glucagon arm most warrants: a cardiovascular outcome trial. Glucagon agonism raises energy expenditure and heart rate is a known concern across this class, and the definitive trial has not reported (verified as pending; registry pages could not be opened directly for this review)."]},{"h":"Product reality, and what an investigational compound means here","where":"mixed","body":["This is a molecule with no approved product anywhere, which makes its position unlike liraglutide's and unlike BPC-157's. There is no approved label, no pharmacopoeial monograph, no reference standard in general circulation, and no generic. Everything in the trials above was manufactured, filled and titrated under a sponsor's control, over a titration schedule of roughly twenty weeks [2,3].","A powder sold under this name is therefore not a cheaper version of a known product; there is no known product. Nothing in the evidence above transfers to a vial nobody has characterised, and a 29-residue acylated peptide with a fatty diacid tail has formulation requirements, including concentration-dependent self-association, that are part of how the trial material behaved (inferred).","The structural detail that makes weekly dosing possible is the same one that makes it a formulation-sensitive molecule: an aminocyclobutane carboxylic acid substitution at position 2 protects it from the DPP-4 enzyme, and a C18 fatty diacid binds albumin to slow clearance (verified as described in the card's structural sourcing)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Investigational everywhere as of October 2026. Phase 3 obesity and diabetes trials have reported, a breakthrough designation exists for the liver indication, liver outcome trials continue, and no regulatory filing has been announced [2,3] (verified). It is not named on the 2026 WADA Prohibited List; as a substance without government approval for human therapeutic use it falls in the catch-all class prohibited at all times [4] (inferred from the class wording).","Does the fibrosis signal hold up? The phase 2 liver trial moved inflammation strongly and fibrosis weakly, and fibrosis is the endpoint tied to real liver outcomes.","Is the glucagon arm safe for years? Raising energy expenditure through the glucagon receptor has no long-term human precedent, and the cardiovascular outcome data are not yet public.","Why does the top dose underperform? In the liver trial the middle dose won, and in the obesity trials 6.0 mg added almost nothing over 3.6 mg while worsening tolerability.","How much of the effect survives real-world use rather than trial conditions? With one in six stopping for gastrointestinal effects inside a supervised trial, the gap between the two estimands in SYNCHRONIZE-2 is a preview of that question [3].","Does it beat the dual and triple incretin drugs already approved? No head-to-head trial against tirzepatide or semaglutide has been reported, which leaves cross-trial comparison, the least reliable kind."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Sanyal et al., New England Journal of Medicine, phase 2 randomized trial of survodutide in MASH and fibrosis (summary of results)","url":"https://gutsandgrowth.com/2024/06/25/survodutide-dual-glucagon-receptor-glp-1-receptor-agonist-for-mash-phase-ii-trial/","year":2024},{"n":2,"t":"American College of Cardiology, SYNCHRONIZE-1 trial summary, survodutide in adults with obesity","url":"https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2026/06/15/17/15/synchronize-1","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Healio, SYNCHRONIZE-2 results presented at the European Association for the Study of Diabetes meeting","url":"https://www.healio.com/news/endocrinology/20261001/survodutide-induces-impressive-amount-of-weight-loss-in-type-2-diabetes-and-obesity","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"thymalin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What is actually in the vial, and why that is the whole problem","where":"lab","body":["Everything else in this library is a defined molecule with a sequence. This is not. It is an acid extract of calf thymus, standardised only by total weight of low-molecular-weight peptides per vial, not by any identified active component (verified as the product's own basis of standardisation).","That has three consequences worth stating plainly. There is no sequence to test a batch against, so identity testing in the usual sense cannot be done; the content is a mixture whose composition can shift with the source tissue and the extraction run; and the named candidate actives, the dipeptides Glu-Trp and Lys-Glu, are sold as separate products in their own right, which means the extract's effects cannot be attributed to them without measuring them (inferred).","It is also a bovine tissue product given by injection. Animal-derived injectables carry a category of risk that synthetic peptides do not, around viral and transmissible agent clearance, and nothing in the accessible record describes what clearance steps the extraction includes (verified as absent from sources reachable for this review). This is the single biggest gap between how the product is discussed and how any regulator outside its home market would assess it."]},{"h":"The longevity study, read line by line","where":"people","body":["The claim that this extract extends human life rests on one 2003 paper reporting two cohorts, 266 people in total, run at institutes in St Petersburg and Kiev [1]. It is worth reading closely, because it is cited far more often than it is examined.","In the St Petersburg arm, 94 women aged 66 to 94 in a war veterans' home were allocated by what the paper calls stratification randomisation to placebo, Thymalin, Epithalamin, or both, described as double-blind, with 10 mg intramuscularly daily for 10 days, repeated in a second year. Six-year mortality was 81.8% in the control group of 22, against 41.7% on Thymalin, 45.8% on Epithalamin, 33.3% on both, and 20.0% in a separate 20-person group dosed annually for six years [1] (verified as reported).","In the Kiev arm, 152 people were allocated to placebo, Thymalin or Epithalamin, with eight-year mortality of 13.6%, 6.6% and 8.5% in groups of 48, 58 and 46 [1] (verified as reported).","Now the arithmetic that should stop a reader. The control group in one cohort died at 81.8% over six years; the control group in the other died at 13.6% over eight. Those are not the same kind of population, and no analysis reconciles them. The treatment effect in the first cohort rests on a control arm of 22 people in a care home, where an 82% six-year mortality means a handful of deaths either way moves the percentage by double digits (inferred).","What the paper does not report is as important as what it does: no causes of death, no dropouts or loss to follow-up, no confidence intervals, no mapping of the six listed statistical tests to individual results, no group sizes for the 94 women, no placebo composition, no allocation concealment, no blinding description for the Kiev arm, and no funding or conflict statement [1] (verified as absent). Its own abstract and tables disagree about whether the observation ran six or eight years and whether treatment ran two or three [1].","None of that makes the result false. It makes it uncheckable, which is a different thing, and it is why this paper has not moved practice outside the research tradition that produced it."]},{"h":"The COVID-19 trial, which is the best-designed study in the file","where":"people","body":["A 2021 trial at a hospital in Chita, Russia, is genuinely a controlled trial and should be given its due. Eighty adults with PCR-confirmed critical COVID-19 were randomised single-blind to standard care plus 10 mg of Thymalin intramuscularly daily for 10 days, or standard care plus 2 mL of saline on the same schedule [2] (verified).","In-hospital mortality was 18 of 44 (40.9%) in the control arm against 7 of 36 (19.4%) on drug, p=0.039, and time from randomisation to death differed, 7.22 against 11.2 days, p=0.005 [2] (verified).","That is a real randomised result with a placebo arm, and it is the strongest single piece of evidence this compound has. Its limits are structural rather than suspicious: a single centre, single-blind rather than double, 80 patients total, and a mortality difference that turns on 11 deaths. In critical COVID-19 in 2020 and 2021, single-centre trials of immunomodulators repeatedly produced large effects that larger trials did not reproduce, which is the base rate this result sits inside (inferred).","A second 2021 report in a Russian-language clinical journal compared 38 treated patients with 50 controls and described large fold-changes in interleukin-6, lymphocyte and CD4 counts and in antibody levels at day 200. It does not describe randomisation or blinding, does not state the dose, and reports no absolute values or statistical tests in the accessible text [3] (verified as reported, with those omissions)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"mixed","body":["This is the part a reader cannot skip. The extract was isolated in 1974 by Morozov and Khavinson; the longevity paper is by Khavinson and Morozov; the COVID-19 trial lists Khavinson among its authors alongside the institute he heads; and the broader bioregulator theory the results are interpreted through is his [1,2] (verified). The COVID-19 trial declares no conflicts of interest [2], which is formally accurate for salary and equity but sits oddly with an author's five-decade authorship of the compound itself (inferred).","No independent group outside that network was found to have replicated either the mortality findings or the proposed mechanism in this review. The claim that these short peptides enter the nucleus and tune gene expression is the group's own proposal and has not been independently confirmed (verified as the state of the record found).","That is not an accusation of bad faith. It is the same structural problem BPC-157 has: a founding group dominating the literature on its own discovery, so that reading many papers is not the same as reading many independent assessments."]},{"h":"What the body does with it, and safety","where":"mixed","body":["There is no pharmacokinetic data: no half-life, no bioavailability, no distribution, in any species (verified as absent). For a mixture rather than a molecule, conventional pharmacokinetics is not even well defined, since there is no single analyte to measure (inferred).","On safety, the 2003 paper states that no allergic or other side effects were seen, but that is a general claim about decades of use rather than a reported adverse-event analysis from the trials, and the paper reports no trial-specific adverse events at all [1] (verified). The COVID-19 trial's published account focuses on efficacy and immune markers [2].","What is absent: no genotoxicity, no carcinogenicity, no reproductive toxicology, and no long-term controlled safety data accessible in this review. The direction of the intended effect, stimulating lymphocyte populations, raises the obvious questions about autoimmunity and about use in people with lymphoproliferative disease, and neither has been studied (inferred).","Decades of use in one health system is a form of evidence, and it is the weakest form, because spontaneous reporting from that era and that market is not a surveillance system anyone can audit (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["It is a registered prescription immunostimulant in Russia, with registration renewed in June 2024, and is sold in parts of the former Soviet sphere (verified as stated in the card's label sourcing). It is not approved by the FDA or EMA, has not been nominated for or reviewed under the US compounding lists, and is not named on the 2026 WADA Prohibited List, which leaves it in the catch-all class for substances without approval for human therapeutic use in the relevant jurisdictions, prohibited at all times [4] (inferred from the class wording, complicated by the fact that it does hold approval in one country).","An approval in one jurisdiction based on a national dossier is not interchangeable with approval elsewhere, particularly for an animal-derived injectable, where tissue sourcing and viral clearance documentation are the first thing other regulators ask for (inferred).","What is the active component? Until that is identified and measured, every mechanistic claim is about a mixture, and no batch can be compared with another.","Does the COVID-19 mortality result replicate? It is the one finding here that a multicentre trial could confirm or refute, and nobody has tried.","Can the 2003 mortality claim be reconstructed? With no causes of death, no dropouts and two control arms that behaved six-fold differently, probably not from the published record.","What is in the extract that is not peptide? For a tissue extract given by injection, this is a manufacturing question with a safety answer, and the accessible record does not contain it.","Does stimulating T-cell populations in older people carry a cost? The theory says it restores; nothing tests whether it also drives autoimmunity or affects malignancy risk."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Khavinson and Morozov, Neuroendocrinology Letters, peptides of pineal gland and thymus prolong human life","url":"https://khavinson.info/assets/files/skan/2003-khavinson_morozov1.pdf","year":2003},{"n":2,"t":"Kuznik et al., Advances in Gerontology, Thymalin regulates immune status in severe COVID-19 older patients","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8654498","year":2021},{"n":3,"t":"Khavinson et al., Vrach 32(6), thymalin and cellular and humoral immunity in elderly COVID-19 patients (Russian)","url":"https://journals.eco-vector.com/0236-3054/article/view/114441","year":2021},{"n":4,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"bpc-157-tb-500":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"The one study that tested the pair, read properly","where":"animals","body":["A 2026 study in a Turkish orthopaedic journal is the only published work to test these two together, and because it is the entire direct evidence base for the blend it is worth reading in full rather than by its conclusion [1].","Thirty-two male Sprague-Dawley rats, about 330 grams, were randomised into four groups of eight: vehicle, one peptide, the other peptide, and both. Each animal had a standardised full transection of the right Achilles tendon with a Kessler repair on day zero. Dosing began on day one and ran for 30 days, all by intraperitoneal injection: the gastric peptide at 10 micrograms per kilogram per day, the thymosin component at 60 micrograms per kilogram per day [1] (verified). Within each group of eight, four tendons went to mechanical testing and four to histology.","Maximum load to failure: vehicle 26.91 newtons, gastric peptide alone 37.16, thymosin alone 37.41, the combination 34.54 [1]. The overall test was significant, and the only surviving pairwise comparison after correction was the thymosin component against vehicle [1] (verified).","On histology, the Bonar total improved significantly only for the thymosin component against vehicle. The Movin total improved significantly for the thymosin component and for the combination, but not for the gastric peptide alone [1] (verified).","The authors state the result directly: combining the two conferred no additional benefit over either agent alone. They set out three candidate explanations, overlapping downstream pathways, a ceiling effect, or doses that were not optimal for a tendon model, and tested none of them [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"Three things in that study that cut against the usual framing","where":"animals","body":["First, the gastric peptide alone did not reach significance on the mechanical measure, the one that speaks to whether a tendon can take load [1]. In a product whose reputation rests largely on that component, the single controlled tendon experiment did not confirm it, while the component usually treated as the junior partner did.","Second, the combination's mechanical result was numerically below both single agents, 34.54 against 37.16 and 37.41 [1]. With four tendons per group this is well within noise and should not be read as interference. It is, equally, the opposite of the additive pattern the pairing is sold on, and it is the only direct observation anyone has (verified, with the caveat stated).","Third, a collagen finding that reads as positive may not be. Type III collagen was higher with the thymosin component, and the authors explicitly cautioned that elevated type III should not be assumed favourable, because type III is characteristic of early repair rather than mature tendon [1] (verified). More early-repair collagen at four weeks could mean faster healing or could mean healing that has not matured.","The authors' own limitations are extensive and worth respecting: a single four-week timepoint so later maturation was not assessed, healthy young male rats only, no functional testing, no molecular or pharmacokinetic work, a thymosin dose not optimised for tendon, histology subsets of four with no a priori power calculation, no blinding of surgery or dosing, and mechanics limited to maximum load with no stiffness or cross-sectional area [1]. They describe the work as exploratory and hypothesis-generating. That is the correct reading, and it is also the strongest direct evidence this blend has."]},{"h":"The route the study used is not the route people use","where":"animals","body":["Dosing was intraperitoneal, into the abdominal cavity [1]. That is standard practice in rodent work and it is not comparable to subcutaneous injection in a person: absorption, first-pass exposure through the portal circulation and peak concentration all differ.","This matters for the blend specifically because the pairing's rationale is systemic repair signalling reaching an injured tendon. A study that delivers both peptides into the peritoneum of a rat tells you something about systemic effect in that species and nothing about what reaches a human tendon (inferred).","It also compounds a gap already present for both components separately: neither has human pharmacokinetic data by the routes in use. For the gastric peptide, regulators found no human pharmacokinetics by any proposed route, and in the human studies where plasma was measured it was undetectable or mostly below the limit of quantification [2]. For the thymosin fragment, there is no human pharmacokinetic data at all, and the only animal data is a single horse study [3] (verified). So there is no exposure figure for either component to connect a dose to a tissue."]},{"h":"Which molecule is the second component","where":"lab","body":["Regulators stated in 2026 that the seven-residue fragment and the 43-residue parent protein are not the same substance [3] (verified). On the common retail version of this blend the second component is the full protein, under the fragment's trade name.","That single fact breaks the chain of inference the product relies on in both directions. The animal study above used material called by the fragment's name; the wound and eye data people cite belongs to the full protein; and the formal regulatory assessment of effectiveness and safety was carried out on the fragment [1,3]. These are three different molecules' worth of evidence being pooled under one label.","Neither component has an approved generic name, and for each one regulators found nominators had been inconsistent about whether they meant a free base or an acetate salt, in at least one case submitting a certificate for one form under the name of the other [2,3] (verified). A two-component vial inherits both identity problems at once.","Independent batch testing of the blend is the weakest of the blends tracked: across eight classifiable results from May to September 2026, none passed the testing service's purity standard (reported, as logged in the card record and not re-verified in this pass)."]},{"h":"What combining adds, and what it costs","where":"mixed","body":["In evidence terms, combining adds nothing measurable. The one study designed to detect an additive effect did not find one [1]. No study has tested any other ratio, any other route, or any other timepoint.","What combining costs is attribution, and there is a concrete illustration of that cost in the regulatory record. Both components' adverse-event reviews turned up cases involving blended products where causality could not be assigned to either ingredient: a report of injection-site redness and swelling in someone also injecting the thymosin component, and a report of hyperpigmentation with gum darkening that recurred on re-exposure to a two-peptide product [2,3] (verified). Each of those is an observation that a single-agent vial would have made interpretable and a blend made permanently ambiguous.","The same applies to benefit. A person who improves on a blend has learned that the blend did something, not which half, and therefore cannot narrow what to continue. Given that the one controlled experiment found the two components performing comparably alone and no better together, that lost information has a real cost (inferred).","There is one piece of human safety data touching either component, and it is very small: an uncontrolled pilot infusing the gastric peptide intravenously in two healthy adults at up to 20 mg, reporting no measurable change in cardiac, liver, kidney or thyroid markers and no adverse effects [4] (reported). Two people, no control, no efficacy endpoint."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product contains either component, and none contains the combination. Both left the US do-not-compound category in April 2026 because nominations were withdrawn, not because of a revised safety finding, which left them outside any recognised category rather than cleared [5] (verified).","On 23 July 2026 the advisory committee recommended both for the 503A bulks list, each by 8 to 6 with one abstention, against agency staff who had proposed adding neither [2,3,6]. Staff had assessed the gastric peptide for ulcerative colitis and the thymosin fragment for wound healing, and found the evidence weighed against both.","Those recommendations are advisory and change nothing by themselves: addition requires formal notice-and-comment rulemaking, which has not begun and has no deadline [7] (verified). An \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\" went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [7].","Nothing in any of this addresses combinations. A substance being eligible for compounding individually would not make a premixed two-peptide injectable an assessed product (inferred).","In sport, both components are prohibited at all times."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does any ratio or schedule produce an additive effect? The single experiment tested one ratio, one route, one duration, and found none. The question is open rather than answered.","Would the result hold at twelve weeks instead of four? Tendon maturation continues well past the single timepoint used, and the authors name this as their main limitation.","Why did the component with the larger reputation fail to reach significance on load to failure? Dose, route, or genuine absence of effect in this model are all live explanations.","Is elevated early-repair collagen good? The study's own authors declined to assume so, and nobody has followed the tissue out to maturity.","Which thymosin molecule is in a given vial, and does it matter? Regulators say the two are different substances; no routine identity test distinguishes them in grey-market supply.","What reaches an injured tendon in a person? No human pharmacokinetic data exists for either component by any route in use.","Why does independent purity testing of this blend fail more often than for the single agents? Testing could answer this; it needs no trial."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Biçer et al., Joint Dis Relat Surg, BPC-157 and TB-500 in rat Achilles tendon healing","url":"https://www.jointdrs.org/full-text/1851","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Lee and Burgess, Altern Ther Health Med, safety of intravenous BPC-157 pilot (PMID 40131143)","url":"https://irvine.health/blogs/bpc157-update/","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"cjc-ipamorelin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"A solubility problem that undercuts the product itself","where":"lab","body":["This is the most concrete finding in the regulatory record for this blend, and it is a chemistry problem rather than an evidence problem.","Reviewing the ghrelin-receptor half for the US compounding list, regulators recorded the free base's water solubility as 0.0032 milligrams per millilitre, against a nominated product strength of 2 milligrams per millilitre, and questioned whether that strength is achievable at all [1] (verified). The acetate salt's solubility is 5 milligrams per millilitre [1].","That is a gap of roughly six hundred-fold between what the free base dissolves to in water and what the proposed product claims to contain. The implication is direct: a product at that strength is either made from the acetate salt, or formulated with something other than water, or not fully in solution (inferred from the cited figures).","It also means the two salt forms are not interchangeable here in any practical sense, and regulators found the nominators inconsistent about which one they meant, with certificates and stated identifiers not matching the named substance [1] (verified). Neither form has an approved generic name, which regulators treat as a safety issue because there is no reference standard to check a batch against.","Storage differs between the forms too: the free base is described as stable at room temperature for about three weeks and recommended below minus 18 degrees Celsius, while the acetate is listed at 2 to 8 degrees with stability up to four years at minus 20 [1]."]},{"h":"The one controlled trial of the ghrelin-receptor half, which failed","where":"people","body":["There is a controlled human efficacy trial here, which is more than most peptides in this catalog have, and it did not succeed.","A Phase 2 randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in postoperative ileus, the slowed gut that follows abdominal surgery, enrolled 117 and randomised 114: 56 to the peptide at 0.03 mg per kilogram intravenously twice daily, 58 to placebo [1] (verified). Median time to first tolerated meal was 25.3 hours against 32.6 hours.","That difference was not statistically significant, and the secondary endpoints showed no difference [1] (verified). Development was subsequently discontinued [1].","A seven-hour numerical advantage that fails significance in a 114-person trial is the classic shape of either a small real effect or noise, and the sponsor's decision to stop tells you how they read it. Either way, the only controlled test of this molecule for any indication did not meet its endpoint.","Regulators also found no clinical trials at all for growth hormone deficiency, the other indication they evaluated [1] (verified). So the compound most associated with raising growth hormone has never been tested in a trial for a growth hormone condition."]},{"h":"What is known about exposure, and what that implies about route","where":"mixed","body":["Human pharmacokinetics exist for the ghrelin-receptor half, from a 1999 study in 48 healthy males across five intravenous dose groups of six, with two placebo subjects per group, spanning roughly a thirty-fold dose range. Half-life was about two hours, clearance 0.078 litres per hour per kilogram, volume of distribution 0.22 litres per kilogram. No effectiveness data and no adverse events were reported [1] (verified).","The important absence: there is no subcutaneous pharmacokinetic data, in any species [1] (verified). Rat intravenous data gives a half-life of about 27 minutes with 60 to 80% excreted unchanged [1]. So the only route with human exposure data is intravenous, and the only route in use is subcutaneous.","For the growth-hormone-releasing half, the identity question shapes what evidence applies. The human data people cite, a several-fold rise in growth hormone and a 1.5 to 3-fold rise in insulin-like growth factor 1 lasting days, belongs to the version carrying an albumin-binding complex. The material sold under that name without the complex is a different molecule with a short duration of action and a different dosing logic entirely (verified, as the card's naming note records). Regulators evaluated five separate substances under this name family, covering both the complexed and uncomplexed forms as free bases and salts [2] (verified).","The synergy premise, that a releasing-hormone signal and a ghrelin-receptor signal together produce a larger growth hormone pulse than either alone, was demonstrated in humans in 1990 using a different ghrelin-receptor peptide from the one in this blend (verified, as the card records). Whether it holds for this specific pair at the amounts used has not been tested (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"Two concerns regulators raised that are specific to this mechanism","where":"mixed","body":["First, abuse potential. Ghrelin receptors are present in reward-related brain regions, and regulators explicitly raised the possibility that the ghrelin-receptor half may have reinforcing properties [1] (verified). This is not a concern that applies to the repair peptides, and it is rarely mentioned in discussion of this blend. It has not been studied in humans.","Second, reproductive effects. No reproductive or developmental studies of the compound itself exist. Regulators cited a mouse study of ghrelin and a ghrelin antagonist showing effects on fertilisation and embryofetal development, and stated this may apply here [1] (verified, as a read-across rather than a direct finding).","Beyond those, the toxicology is empty: no acute toxicity, no repeat-dose toxicity, no genotoxicity and no carcinogenicity studies were found for the ghrelin-receptor half [1] (verified). Regulators concluded the nonclinical data were too limited to assess safety for clinical use.","Adverse event reporting amounts to two non-serious cases involving compounded products, one a nasal spray with headache and increased tearing, the other a subcutaneous combination with joint pain [1] (verified). As with every blend in this catalog, the combination case cannot be attributed to either ingredient.","Immunogenicity could not be ruled out from unidentified impurities or aggregates, and regulators noted injectable routes present a particular risk for both forms [1]."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026, and why this blend sits differently from the others","where":"people","body":["This is the one blend in the catalog whose components have been through the advisory process and lost.","Regulators proposed that neither form of the ghrelin-receptor half be added to the 503A bulks list, on all four criteria: neither is well characterised, with critical data on impurities, aggregates and endotoxins missing; effectiveness evidence is limited for any route with nothing supporting the subcutaneous route for either indication reviewed; professional guidelines do not mention it for either condition; approved alternatives exist for both; and the nonclinical data cannot support a safety assessment [1] (verified).","The committee agreed. It voted against the ghrelin-receptor half in October 2024 and against the releasing-hormone half in December 2024, in both cases unanimously or near-unanimously (verified, as the card records the tallies). The December 2024 session covered five substances in the releasing-hormone name family, with the agency proposing that none be included [2] (verified).","Neither component was among the twelve peptides removed from the do-not-compound category in April 2026, and both remain on it (verified, as the card records). That is a materially different position from the repair peptides, which left that category and then received favourable advisory recommendations in July 2026.","So while the broader regulatory direction in 2026 has been toward opening access, these two moved the other way and stayed there. Any interim access policy would have to cover substances the agency and its committee have both already declined (inferred).","In sport, releasing-hormone analogues and growth hormone secretagogues are prohibited."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Can the nominated strength even be made from the free base? The cited solubility figure and the claimed concentration differ by roughly six hundred-fold, which makes this a question about what is physically in the vial.","Which molecule is the releasing-hormone half, complexed or not? The dosing logic and duration differ completely, and the human growth hormone data belongs to the complexed version.","Does the 1990 synergy finding hold for this specific pair? It was shown with a different ghrelin-receptor peptide, and the premise of the whole product rests on it transferring.","What does subcutaneous dosing actually produce? There is no subcutaneous pharmacokinetic data in any species for the ghrelin-receptor half, and that is the only route in use.","Is there reinforcing potential? Regulators raised it from receptor distribution, and nobody has looked.","What are the reproductive effects? None have been studied; the concern is a read-across from ghrelin biology.","Does raising growth hormone and insulin-like growth factor 1 over months do anything measurable to body composition in healthy adults? No trial has measured it with this pair, and the reported effects are sequenced exactly as expectation would predict."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, ipamorelin bulk drug substances (October 2024 meeting)","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/182088/download","year":2024},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC voting questions, December 2024 meeting, CJC-1295 substance family","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/184043/download","year":2024}]},"ghk-cu-bpc-157":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Where the pairing came from","where":"mixed","body":["Neither compound was developed with the other in mind. The copper tripeptide comes out of 1970s plasma research and a cosmetic and wound-care programme [1]; the gastric peptide comes out of a Zagreb laboratory's gut and injury work from 1993 onward [2]. The two-peptide vial is a retail descendant of larger multi-peptide repair blends, built by subtraction rather than by any study showing that these two belong together (inferred from the order in which the products appeared; no scientific paper proposes this pair).","The rationale offered for it is division of labour: one for deep tissue, one for skin and matrix. That is a reasonable way to describe what each has been studied for. It is not evidence that combining them does more than either alone, and nobody has tested that (verified as absent; no study of the combination in cells, animals or people was found)."]},{"h":"The two halves have different kinds of evidence, by different routes","where":"mixed","body":["The gastric peptide's evidence is almost entirely rodent injury models from one founding group, with one controlled human trial: a 2005 meeting abstract in ulcerative colitis using an enema, whose result regulators judged inadequate [2] (verified). No human pharmacokinetic data exist for injection, and in the rectal studies where plasma was measured, levels were undetectable or below quantification [2].","The copper tripeptide's human evidence is topical, small and short, and its one large test failed. Two phase 3 trials in diabetic foot ulcers, 511 patients across 30 centres at 0.5% and 2% against placebo, found it no better than placebo [3,4] (verified). The injected route has never been studied in people.","So the blend is injected, while the human data that exist were gathered rectally for one component and on the skin for the other. In route terms, the vial has no human evidence behind either half (inferred from the above, and the most important thing to understand about it)."]},{"h":"What the copper adds that a plain peptide blend does not","where":"lab","body":["The copper tripeptide is roughly 16% copper by mass, from its formula (inferred, calculated). A 50 mg share of copper peptide therefore carries about 8 mg of elemental copper across the whole vial. For scale, the adult dietary requirement is 0.9 mg a day and the upper intake limit is 10 mg a day, a limit set on liver damage [5] (verified).","The comparison is imperfect in a way that matters. The body controls copper mainly at the gut, by adjusting how much it absorbs, and at the liver, by excreting it into bile [5] (verified). An injection skips the first of those controls. Whether the copper stays bound to its peptide long enough to be handled differently from free copper, and where it goes once the peptide is broken down, has not been studied for injection (verified as absent).","The chemistry of the bound complex is the best-established part of the copper story: held by the tripeptide, copper's ability to drive damaging redox reactions is largely silenced [1] (verified, in vitro). That protection depends on the copper staying bound. Anything in the solution that competes for copper or reduces it works against that, which is the underlying reason the mixing rules on the card exist (inferred)."]},{"h":"Mixing and stability: what is known and what is assumed","where":"lab","body":["For the gastric peptide, regulators recorded that the lyophilised powder should be kept below minus 18 degrees and that reconstituted solution is stable for two to three weeks refrigerated [2] (verified). That is the shortest documented window of the two components and, in a combined vial, it sets the realistic life of the whole solution once mixed (inferred).","No stability study of the two together exists. Whether the gastric peptide, which carries two neighbouring aspartates and a lysine, takes up any copper from the complex at the concentrations in a vial has not been measured; the card's statement that it has no strong copper site is a reasonable reading of its sequence, not a tested result (inferred).","The quality problems recorded for the gastric peptide on its own, unidentified impurities and no endotoxin, bioburden or aggregate testing on the certificates regulators reviewed [2], carry over unchanged into a blend. Injectable copper tripeptide has had no equivalent public review at all [6] (verified)."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product contains either component, alone or together. In the US the gastric peptide was removed from the do-not-compound category in April 2026 after nominations were withdrawn, then recommended for the 503A bulks list by an advisory committee in July by 8 to 6 with one abstention, against the agency's own staff recommendation [2,7]. That vote is advisory and requires rulemaking that has not begun [8] (verified).","Injectable copper tripeptide is in a different position: the topical form was restored to Category 1 in May 2026, the injectable route was left unclassified, and its advisory review is expected before the end of February 2027 [6,8] (verified). So a combined injectable draws on one substance recommended but not yet permitted and one whose injectable form has not been assessed.","In sport, the gastric peptide is prohibited at all times under WADA's non-approved substances class [2]; the copper tripeptide is not named, but injected use of an unapproved substance falls under the same catch-all (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the pair do anything the components do not? Untested.","Where does injected copper go, and does repeated dosing shift copper balance? Unstudied for either the single agent or the blend, and it is the larger component by mass.","Does the gastric peptide interact with the copper complex in solution? Answerable with routine analytical chemistry, never published.","If someone responds, to which half? A fixed-ratio vial makes that impossible to tell."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Encyclopedia reference article, copper peptide GHK-Cu, with primary citations indexed","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_peptide_GHK-Cu","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"BioCentury, peptide-copper gel completed two Phase III trials in diabetic foot ulcers","url":"https://biocentury.com/article/111229/iamin-gel-peptide-copper-compound-completed-two-phase-iii-trials","year":1994},{"n":4,"t":"The Pharma Letter, lead product no better than placebo in pivotal trials","url":"https://www.thepharmaletter.com/procyte-s-lead-product-no-better-than-placebo","year":1994},{"n":5,"t":"NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, Copper fact sheet for health professionals","url":"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/copper-healthProfessional/","year":2022},{"n":6,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"glow":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What combining these three adds in evidence terms","where":"mixed","body":["Nothing has been measured. The blend has never been tested in an animal or a person, and no study of any three-component ratio exists (verified as absent).","What has been tested is two of the three, together, in one experiment. A 2026 rat Achilles transection study ran four groups of eight: vehicle, the gastric peptide alone, the thymosin component alone, and both, dosed intraperitoneally for 30 days after a Kessler repair [1]. The authors hypothesised an additive benefit and found none, stating that combining the two conferred no additional benefit over either agent alone [1] (verified).","Two details from that study bear on this blend specifically. On load to failure, the gastric peptide alone did not reach significance against vehicle, while the thymosin component alone did [1]. And the combination's mechanical result sat numerically below both single agents, which with four tendons per group is within noise but is the opposite of the pattern the pairing is sold on [1].","So the deeper two-thirds of this product rests on one exploratory experiment that looked for addition and did not find it. The authors offered overlapping downstream pathways, a ceiling effect, or non-optimal dosing as explanations, and tested none [1] (verified).","The copper component was not in that study. Its interaction with either of the others has never been examined in any system."]},{"h":"The three components do not have comparable evidence","where":"mixed","body":["A blend inherits the reputation of its best-regarded ingredient. Read separately, the three sit at very different places.","The copper tripeptide has the most human data and the most discouraging large result: a pivotal programme in diabetic foot ulcers enrolling 511 patients across 30 centres at two strengths against placebo found the drug no better than placebo [2,3] (verified). Its positive human evidence is topical, small and short. The injected route, which is what a blend uses, has never been studied in humans.","The gastric peptide has one controlled human trial, a 2005 meeting abstract in ulcerative colitis whose between-group confidence interval crossed zero, and which regulators judged inadequate for lacking endpoint definitions, inclusion criteria and statistics [4] (verified).","The thymosin component is the thinnest and the most often assumed strongest. Regulators found no clinical studies, no human exposure data, no human pharmacokinetic data by any route, and no toxicology studies of any kind for the marketed fragment [5] (verified). When that fragment was tested directly in a fibroblast wound-closure assay at 50 micrograms per millilitre, it did not significantly promote closure [5].","On the common retail version the thymosin component is the full 43-residue protein rather than the fragment. Regulators stated in 2026 that these are not the same substance [5] (verified), so the component list cannot be used to look up which evidence applies."]},{"h":"The chemistry three components have to satisfy at once","where":"lab","body":["This is where documented facts about the separate components say something concrete about the mixture, and it is the most practically useful part of this entry.","The components prefer different acidity. The copper tripeptide binds its copper ion very tightly at neutral pH and more weakly as pH falls, while the two repair peptides have different preferred ranges from each other, one toward neutral-to-slightly-alkaline and one toward mildly acidic (as the card records). A single solution sits at one pH. It therefore cannot sit at all three optima, and whichever compromise is struck, at least one component is held outside its preferred range (inferred).","The specific concern that follows is copper. If the solution is held toward the acidic end to suit the peptide that prefers it, the copper complex's binding is weaker there, which is the condition under which copper is less fully held (inferred). The reason bound copper is tolerable in the first place is that complexation silences its reactivity; a documented 1981 study showed that redox activity is effectively silenced when the metal is held by this tripeptide (verified, in vitro, as indexed in the copper peptide reference literature). Weaker binding is therefore the one chemical variable in this product with a clear mechanism for harm, and no study has measured free copper in any multi-peptide blend (verified as absent).","Storage requirements also conflict. The gastric peptide's lyophilised powder is recommended below minus 18 degrees Celsius, with reconstituted solution documented as stable only two to three weeks refrigerated [4]. The thymosin fragment is stored below minus 20 as free base and below minus 15 as acetate [5]. A single vial can only be stored one way, and after reconstitution the shortest-lived component sets the real shelf life of the whole product (inferred).","Release certificates on the powder show components near label with drift, including the gastric peptide running about 17% over in one batch, and independent testing is thin: one of two classifiable results passed between July and September 2026 (reported, as logged in the card record and not re-verified in this pass)."]},{"h":"What the copper share means that the other components do not","where":"lab","body":["At the common composition the copper tripeptide is about five parts to one of each other component, which makes this, in mass terms, mostly a copper peptide product with two additions. Nothing in the literature establishes that this ratio is right for any purpose, because no ratio has been studied (verified as absent).","An injected copper complex raises questions a plain peptide does not: how much copper is delivered per dose, where it goes once the peptide is degraded, and what repeated dosing does to copper handling over weeks. Nothing in the available record addresses this for the single agent, let alone in a mixture (verified as absent).","There is also a regulatory asymmetry that bears on this. For the gastric peptide and the thymosin fragment, regulators published detailed 2026 assessments covering identity, certificates, impurities, endotoxin, aggregates, toxicology and immunogenicity [4,5]. For injectable copper tripeptide, no such document exists, because its advisory review was deferred to a later meeting [6] (verified). So the component making up most of the mass is the one with no published agency assessment of what is in it.","The stinging and welts users describe at injection sites are attributed to the copper share and are consistent with a reactive metal complex meeting tissue (reported). They are also the clearest signal in the whole product that something is happening locally that nobody has characterised."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product contains this combination anywhere, and the three components sit in three different regulatory positions.","The gastric peptide and the thymosin fragment left the US do-not-compound category in April 2026 because nominations were withdrawn, not because of a revised safety finding, leaving them outside any recognised category rather than cleared [6] (verified). On 23 July 2026 the advisory committee recommended both for the 503A bulks list, each by 8 to 6 with one abstention, against agency staff who had proposed adding neither [4,5,7].","The copper tripeptide is placed differently again. Its non-injectable form was restored to Category 1 on 5 May 2026 after a nominator clarified it had meant to withdraw only the injectable nomination, and it sits there under temporary enforcement discretion. The injectable route was left unclassified and has not been reviewed by the committee at all; that review is expected before the end of February 2027 [6] (verified).","So an injectable blend of all three draws on two substances recommended but not yet permitted, plus one whose injectable form has never been assessed. Addition to the list requires formal rulemaking, which has not begun [8]. An interim enforcement-discretion policy went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [8] (verified). Nothing in any of this addresses premixed combinations.","In sport, two of the three are prohibited at all times."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does any three-way combination add anything? The only pairwise test among these components looked for addition and found none, and the copper component has never been combined with either in a study.","How tightly is the copper held in a multi-peptide solution at whatever pH the product sits at, and is any of it free? This is the single most consequential unmeasured variable, and it is answerable by analytical chemistry rather than by a trial.","What happens to delivered copper over a multi-week course? Unstudied for the single agent and unexamined in combination, in the component that makes up most of the mass.","What is the real shelf life after reconstitution? The components' documented stability windows differ and the shortest governs, but nobody has measured the mixture.","Which thymosin molecule is present? Regulators say the fragment and the full protein are different substances, and no routine identity test distinguishes them in grey-market supply.","If a user responds, to what? Three simultaneous components at a vendor-fixed ratio make attribution impossible by design.","Why does the copper component sting, and is the local reaction telling us something about the chemistry? Nobody has looked."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Biçer et al., Joint Dis Relat Surg, BPC-157 and TB-500 in rat Achilles tendon healing","url":"https://www.jointdrs.org/full-text/1851","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"BioCentury, peptide-copper gel completed two Phase III trials in diabetic foot ulcers","url":"https://biocentury.com/article/111229/iamin-gel-peptide-copper-compound-completed-two-phase-iii-trials","year":1994},{"n":3,"t":"The Pharma Letter, lead product no better than placebo in pivotal trials","url":"https://www.thepharmaletter.com/procyte-s-lead-product-no-better-than-placebo","year":1994},{"n":4,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"klow":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What \"never tested as a blend\" actually means","where":"mixed","body":["The phrase gets used as a mild caveat. It carries more than that, and it is worth unpacking into the three separate things that have not been examined.","First, pharmacology. Nobody has measured whether the four do the same things together that they do apart. Two of them act partly through the same pathway, nitric oxide signalling in the case of the gastric peptide, and matrix remodelling in the case of the copper tripeptide, so additive, redundant and opposing outcomes are all possible and none has been tested (verified as absent).","Second, chemistry in solution. The four sit in one vial, dissolved together, for the life of that vial. No stability study of the combination exists. What is known is that each component has its own documented degradation behaviour and its own storage requirement, and those requirements do not match (see below).","Third, safety attribution. Each injected component separately drew the same concern from US regulators in 2026: that peptide-related impurities and aggregates can drive immune responses, and that no immunogenicity studies exist for any of them [1,2,3] (verified). Combining four peptides in one solution multiplies the impurity and aggregate surface without adding any data about it (inferred).","None of that says the blend is dangerous. It says the blend is, in evidence terms, a fifth product with no file of its own."]},{"h":"The four components do not have comparable evidence","where":"mixed","body":["A blend tends to inherit the reputation of its best-regarded ingredient. Read component by component, the evidence here is not just thin, it is unevenly thin in ways that matter.","The copper tripeptide has the most human data of the four, and the most discouraging large result. Its pivotal programme in diabetic foot ulcers enrolled 511 patients across 30 centres at two strengths against placebo, and the drug was no better than placebo [4,5] (verified). Its positive human evidence is topical, small and short. The injected route, which is what a blend uses, has never been studied in humans at all.","The gastric peptide has exactly one controlled human trial, reported as a 2005 meeting abstract: 53 people with ulcerative colitis, a daily 80 mg enema against placebo for two weeks, with a between-group difference whose confidence interval crossed zero. Regulators found the abstract inadequate for lacking endpoint definitions, inclusion criteria and statistics [1] (verified). Everything else human is uncontrolled.","The thymosin fragment is the weakest of the four and the one most often assumed strongest. Regulators found no clinical studies, no human exposure data and no human pharmacokinetic data by any route, and no toxicology studies of any kind: no acute, no repeat-dose, no genotoxicity, no reproductive, no carcinogenicity [2] (verified). When the marketed fragment was tested directly in a fibroblast wound-closure assay at 50 micrograms per millilitre, it did not significantly promote closure [2].","The tripeptide from alpha-MSH has no human studies, no pharmacokinetic data in any species and no toxicology studies [3]. Its nomination cited nine references, eight animal studies of related compounds and one cadaver-skin study, none testing it in a living human [3] (verified). It was nominated only as a 0.1% topical cream, not for injection.","So the blend combines one component with a failed pivotal trial, one with a single inadequate and non-significant controlled trial, and two with no human data whatsoever."]},{"h":"The identity problem, multiplied by four","where":"lab","body":["Each of these substances exists in at least two forms, a free base and an acetate salt, with different molecular weights and, in two cases, different solubility and storage requirements. For each one, regulators found that nominators had been inconsistent about which form they meant, in some cases submitting a certificate of analysis for one form under the name of the other [1,2,3] (verified). None of the four has an approved generic name, so there is no reference standard to test a batch against, which regulators flagged as a patient safety issue in its own right.","One mismatch is specific to this blend and worth stating plainly. Regulators stated that the marketed seven-residue fragment and the full 43-residue protein are not the same substance [2] (verified). Retail versions of this blend commonly contain the full protein under the fragment's name. That is not a minor labelling quirk: it means the vial contains a molecule with a different evidence base, different stability and a different regulatory record from the one on the label, and that the component list cannot be used to look up what is actually present.","Independent batch testing of the blend is better than for most, and still mixed. Across one public testing service's classifiable results from May to September 2026, 9 of 15 passed. Powder certificates from two laboratories in July and August 2026 showed each component near its label figure, with the copper tripeptide running as high as 120% of label. One certificate from an unnamed laboratory failed its own stated specifications while recording the conclusion \"complies\" (verified, as logged in the card record).","The copper component being the one that runs over label is the least convenient result, since it is the largest share of the powder by a wide margin and the component carrying a metal."]},{"h":"The chemistry one vial has to satisfy at once","where":"lab","body":["This is where documented facts about the separate components say something concrete about the combination.","Storage requirements conflict. The gastric peptide's lyophilised powder is recommended below minus 18 degrees Celsius, with reconstituted solution documented as stable only two to three weeks refrigerated [1]. The thymosin fragment is stored below minus 20 as free base and below minus 15 as acetate [2]. The alpha-MSH tripeptide is reported stable up to three years at minus 20 as free base, while its acetate's stated storage is ordinary refrigeration at 2 to 8 degrees [3] (verified). A single blended vial can only be stored one way, so at least one component is being held outside its documented condition, and after reconstitution the shortest-lived component sets the real shelf life of the whole vial (inferred).","Solubility sets a constraint that can be checked with arithmetic. At the common composition, 10 mg of the alpha-MSH tripeptide in a 3 mL reconstitution is about 3.3 mg per millilitre. The documented water solubility of its free base is about 0.70 mg per millilitre; its acetate dissolves to about 5 mg per millilitre [3]. On those figures the free base could not fully dissolve at that concentration and the acetate could, which means a blend that reconstitutes clearly at 3 mL is almost certainly built on the acetate salt (inferred, assuming the cited water-solubility figures carry over into the blended solution).","Degradation is predictable for at least one component: under forced conditions the alpha-MSH tripeptide acetate cyclises to a diketopiperazine [3] (verified). A degraded vial therefore does not simply contain less of that component, it contains a different molecule, and nothing in the record describes what that does alongside three other peptides and a copper complex.","The copper itself is the variable with no file. An injected copper complex raises questions a plain peptide does not: how much copper is delivered, where it goes once the peptide is degraded, and what repeated dosing does to copper handling. Nothing in the available record addresses this for the single agent, let alone in a mixture (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"What a blend costs you in information","where":"mixed","body":["There is a practical argument for blends, which is convenience, and a practical cost, which is attribution. Both deserve stating honestly.","If something improves, four components could be responsible, and the two with no human data at all are as likely candidates as the two with some. If something goes wrong, the same ambiguity applies in reverse. The reported adverse effects of this blend map neatly onto single components, stinging and welts to the copper share, nausea to the gastric peptide, with first-week tiredness and headache unattributed (reported), which suggests the ingredients are not masking each other, but it also means nobody using the blend learns which component they actually respond to.","That matters more here than it would for a fixed-dose approved combination, because the ratio is fixed by the vendor rather than by evidence. At the common composition the copper tripeptide is roughly five parts to one of each other component, which makes this, in mass terms, mostly a copper peptide product with three additions. Nothing in the literature establishes that this ratio is the right one for any purpose; no study of any ratio exists (verified as absent).","A regulator's framing of the general problem fits here: for each of these substances individually, the agency's difficulty was identity before efficacy, because these peptides lack a universally accepted chemical definition, which makes evaluation very hard [6]. A four-component mixture of four such substances inherits all four of those problems simultaneously."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product contains this combination anywhere, and the four components sit in three different regulatory positions, which is unusual and makes blanket statements wrong.","The gastric peptide, the thymosin fragment and the alpha-MSH tripeptide were each removed from the US do-not-compound category in April 2026, because nominations were withdrawn rather than because of any revised safety finding, which left them outside any recognised category rather than cleared [7] (verified). On 23 July 2026 the advisory committee recommended all three for the 503A bulks list by 8 to 6 with one abstention each, against the agency's own staff, who had proposed not adding any of them [1,2,3,8]. Those recommendations are advisory, require formal rulemaking that has not begun, and carry no deadline [9].","The copper tripeptide is in a different position again. Its non-injectable form was restored to Category 1 on 5 May 2026 after a nominator clarified it had meant to withdraw only the injectable nomination, and it sits there under temporary enforcement discretion. The injectable route was left unclassified, and has not yet been reviewed by the committee at all; that review is expected before the end of February 2027 [7,9] (verified).","So an injectable blend of all four draws on three substances recommended but not yet permitted, plus one whose injectable form has never been assessed. An \"Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides\" went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and was unpublished as of mid-October, with its covered substances and conditions not public [9] (verified). Until it appears, nobody can say whether a blend of this kind would fall inside it.","In sport, two of the four are prohibited at all times, and the other two fall under catch-all provisions rather than outside the rules."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the combination do anything the components do not? No study of any kind has addressed this, which makes the central premise of the product untested.","Which salt forms are present, and does the vial contain the fragment or the full protein? Regulators state those are different substances. Without identity testing that distinguishes them, the label cannot answer this.","What is the real shelf life after reconstitution? The components' documented stability windows differ and the shortest one governs, but nobody has measured the mixture.","What happens to the copper over repeated dosing? Unstudied for the single agent and unexamined in combination, and it is the largest component by mass.","Why is the copper component the one that runs over label in independent testing, and is that a weighing, a filling or a measurement artefact? This is answerable by testing.","If a user responds, to what? The fixed ratio and four simultaneous components make individual attribution impossible by design, which is the quiet cost of every blend.","Would a blend fall within the interim compounding policy once published? Unknowable until it is published, and the four components' differing positions make it genuinely uncertain."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, KPV bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193346/download","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"BioCentury, peptide-copper gel completed two Phase III trials in diabetic foot ulcers","url":"https://biocentury.com/article/111229/iamin-gel-peptide-copper-compound-completed-two-phase-iii-trials","year":1994},{"n":5,"t":"The Pharma Letter, lead product no better than placebo in pivotal trials","url":"https://www.thepharmaletter.com/procyte-s-lead-product-no-better-than-placebo","year":1994},{"n":6,"t":"Time, FDA committee votes in favour of peptides","url":"https://time.com/article/2026/07/23/fda-committee-peptides/","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"DJ Holt Law, regulatory alert on the April 2026 compounding category changes","url":"https://djholtlaw.com/regulatory-alert-fdas-april-2026-update-on-bpc-157-and-ghk-cu/","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"Pharmaceutical Executive, FDA panel votes to loosen restrictions on four peptides","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/fda-votes-loosen-restrictions-four-peptides","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026}]},"lipo-c":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What \"never tested as a blend\" means here, in the maker's own words","where":"mixed","body":["Most blends in this library have to be assessed from outside. This one is unusual: a licensed compounding pharmacy publishes its own product page, and that page says the quiet part out loud. The evidence for the product is described as largely extrapolated from the individual components' pharmacology rather than from randomised trials of the combination; safety and efficacy for the formulation have not been evaluated by the FDA; and there is no single approved dosing schedule [1] (verified).","That is the honest baseline. Three separate things have not been studied: whether the components do anything together that they do not do apart, whether the mixture is stable and compatible as formulated beyond its dating, and what the combination's adverse-event profile is as opposed to each ingredient's.","The deeper problem specific to this product is that the name is not a formula. Published recipes under this label run from about 120 to 526 mg per mL, with different components and different ratios. A study of one would not describe another. There is no plausible reading in which a trial of this product could exist, because there is no single product to trial (inferred)."]},{"h":"The components do not have comparable evidence, and the biggest one has the least","where":"people","body":["L-carnitine is the only component with weight-loss trials, and they are oral. A 2016 meta-analysis in a peer-reviewed obesity journal pooled nine randomised trials judged methodologically adequate, totalling 911 adults, and found a mean difference of minus 1.33 kg (95% CI minus 2.09 to minus 0.57) and minus 0.47 kg/m2 of BMI (95% CI minus 0.88 to minus 0.05) [2] (verified).","Two features of that result are usually dropped. The effect is about one and a third kilograms, which is small, and meta-regression found it diminished significantly with longer supplementation, p=0.002 [2] (verified). So the best-evidenced ingredient has a small effect that fades, measured by mouth at doses typically far above what a millilitre of this blend delivers.","Choline deficiency causes fatty liver, and choline is genuinely required to assemble the particles the liver uses to export fat; methionine genuinely supplies methyl groups through S-adenosylmethionine; thiamine and pantothenate are genuinely coenzymes for fuel use. All of that is real biochemistry and none of it is evidence of fat loss in people who are not deficient. No trial of injected choline, methionine, inositol or dexpanthenol for fat loss was found in this review (verified as absent).","Inositol's best human evidence sits in polycystic ovary syndrome, not obesity, and it is oral. It is also the component most often absent from the formula altogether, including from the four-part pharmacy version [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The mechanism argument has a hole where the injection goes","where":"mixed","body":["Carnitine's job is to carry long-chain fatty acids across the inner mitochondrial membrane. The limiting step for that in muscle is not how much carnitine is in the blood, it is how much gets into the muscle cell, which depends on the OCTN2 transporter and is stimulated by insulin. Raising plasma carnitine does not reliably raise muscle carnitine, which is why oral loading studies have generally needed carbohydrate co-ingestion to move muscle content at all (inferred from the transport biology).","The same logic applies to the liver components. Choline and methionine support fat export in someone short of them. In someone whose intake is adequate, adding more does not create a shortage to correct. The rationale is a deficiency-repletion argument being applied to people who have not been shown to be deficient (inferred).","Nothing here contradicts the biochemistry on the card. The gap is between \"this molecule is required for fat oxidation\" and \"adding this molecule increases fat oxidation,\" and no human study of this product has crossed it."]},{"h":"The TMAO question, and the one argument that favours injection","where":"mixed","body":["The two largest components by mass in most versions, carnitine and choline, are also the two best-characterised dietary precursors of trimethylamine N-oxide, a metabolite associated with cardiovascular disease. This is worth laying out properly because it is the strongest safety-adjacent argument in the file and it cuts in an unexpected direction.","In a 2013 Nature Medicine study, gut bacteria converted dietary carnitine to trimethylamine and then TMAO: a week of oral antibiotics made labelled TMAO nearly undetectable in five omnivores, with production returning after at least three weeks off; vegans and vegetarians had much lower fasting TMAO and a markedly reduced capacity to produce it after a carnitine challenge; mice on 1.3% carnitine in drinking water had roughly double the aortic plaque area with plasma TMAO around 114 micromolar against 17, and antibiotics blocked the effect [3] (verified, in people and animals). In a clinical cohort of 2,595 patients undergoing cardiac evaluation, high carnitine predicted events after adjustment for traditional risk factors, but the association disappeared once TMAO was accounted for [3] (verified).","The pivotal detail for an injection is that all those challenges were oral, and the gut microbiota are the required step. The authors themselves note that parenteral administration would bypass the microbiota, and did not test it [3] (verified as their inference, not their result).","So the fair reading is: the TMAO pathway is a real and well-evidenced concern for swallowed carnitine and choline, and an injected route plausibly sidesteps the main generating step. That is an argument for the route, and it is untested. The product page's listing of transient fishy body odour attributed to trimethylamine [1] suggests the pathway is not entirely bypassed in practice (inferred)."]},{"h":"Product reality, and the parts of the label worth reading","where":"lab","body":["This is a ready-made aqueous solution in a multi-dose vial, not a lyophilised powder, which changes the handling questions entirely. The published four-part pharmacy formula is methionine 15, choline chloride 50, L-carnitine 50 and dexpanthenol 5 mg per mL in water for injection, supplied in 10 and 30 mL multi-dose vials, for intramuscular use, with a beyond-use date of 28 days from compounding under the relevant sterile compounding standard [1] (verified).","Twenty-eight days is a formulation and sterility limit on a multi-dose vial, not a suggestion. Storage is specified as either room temperature or refrigerated depending on the exact formulation, protected from light in the original carton, not frozen, not shaken vigorously, and inspected for particulates or discolouration before use [1] (verified). The B vitamins in versions that contain them are light-sensitive, which is why the carton matters.","The contraindication list is more specific than the product's reputation suggests, and one entry is absolute: inborn errors of sulfur amino acid metabolism such as homocystinuria. Also listed are hypersensitivity to any component and severe hepatic failure. Cautions cover advanced kidney impairment or dialysis because of carnitine accumulation, active peptic ulcer disease, levodopa use, severe heart failure or arrhythmia, and pregnancy and breastfeeding [1] (verified).","The interaction list is the part most likely to be missed. Carnitine may potentiate warfarin, with reports of raised INR. Levodopa-carbidopa effect may be reduced. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil warrant monitoring for cramping or slow heart rate. Cysteine or N-acetylcysteine may alter paracetamol metabolism [1] (verified).","Documented adverse effects are mostly local: injection-site pain, redness and warmth, with nausea and loose stools in a minority resolving within a day or two, and the transient fishy odour. Uncommon but serious entries are immediate hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis, and delayed eosinophilic myositis, with systemic hypersensitivity cited below 0.1% in one retrospective series [1] (verified as reported by the pharmacy).","One honest note about the source: that page cites several commercial clinic blogs alongside its pharmacology, and two of its regulatory citations use placeholder identifiers [1] (verified). It is the best single document available for this product and it is not a peer-reviewed one."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["In the US this is a 503A compounded preparation, made for an individual patient on prescription, explicitly not FDA-approved and not evaluated by the agency for safety or efficacy [1] (verified). That is a different legal position from the injectable peptides elsewhere in this library: the components are established substances with their own histories, and levocarnitine itself is approved, but only for carnitine deficiency. Research-labelled vials sold under the same name sit outside the pharmacy framework entirely.","In sport, no component is named on the 2026 Prohibited List. The applicable rule is the method prohibition on intravenous infusions totalling more than 100 mL per 12 hours outside hospital care, which does not bite on a one-millilitre intramuscular injection [4] (verified).","Does the combination do anything the components do not? The maker says this has not been tested, which makes the premise of the product unexamined rather than disputed [1].","Does injected carnitine reach muscle? Plasma carnitine and muscle carnitine are different things, and the transporter step is the one nobody has measured for this route.","Does the injected route genuinely avoid the TMAO pathway? The gut step is required for the oral effect and plausibly bypassed, but the fishy-odour reports suggest trimethylamine still appears, and nobody has measured TMAO after an injection [1,3].","What does a repeated methionine load do to homocysteine? Methionine is the direct precursor, and no study of this product has measured it.","Which formula is a given vial? Published versions range from 120 to 526 mg per mL with different components, so every number in circulation, including dose conventions, is formula-specific and usually unlabelled as such."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Empower Pharmacy, Lipo-C injection product monograph, composition, 503A status, contraindications and interactions","url":"https://www.empowerpharmacy.com/drugs/lipo-c-injection","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Pooyandjoo et al., Obesity Reviews, effect of L-carnitine on weight loss in adults, systematic review and meta-analysis","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27335245/","year":2016},{"n":3,"t":"Koeth et al., Nature Medicine, intestinal microbiota metabolism of L-carnitine promotes atherosclerosis","url":"https://www.dcscience.net/Koeth-carnitine-Nature-Med-2013.pdf","year":2013},{"n":4,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List, including M2.2 intravenous infusion limit","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"reta-cagri":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What has and has not been studied as a pair","where":"mixed","body":["No human trial of retatrutide with cagrilintide has been located, and none is registered in the sources reviewed (verified as absent). The evidence people lean on is a different pairing: cagrilintide with semaglutide. In REDEFINE 1, 3,417 adults took the combination, either drug alone or placebo for 68 weeks; adherent participants lost 22.7% on the combination, 16.1% on semaglutide, 11.8% on cagrilintide alone and 2.3% on placebo [4,5] (verified). Gastrointestinal effects hit 80% on the combination against 40% on placebo [4].","That result does not transfer automatically. Semaglutide acts on one receptor; retatrutide acts on three and already produces far larger losses on its own, so there is less room left for a second drug to add, and the glucagon arm brings effects semaglutide does not have (inferred). The single 2026 rodent paper located for this read tested retatrutide alone, about 25% lower body weight than obese controls over four weeks, not the pair [9] (verified). A rodent study of the pair itself could not be traced."]},{"h":"Each component, one level deeper than the headline","where":"people","body":["Retatrutide. The phase 2 trial enrolled 338 adults for 48 weeks; at 12 mg weight fell 24.2% against 2.1% on placebo [1] (verified). Heart rate rose with dose, peaking around week 24 and easing afterwards; cardiac arrhythmia adverse events reached 14% in some dose groups against 2% on placebo, mostly mild [1] (verified). TRIUMPH-1, 2,339 adults over 80 weeks, gave 28.3% at 12 mg on treatment and 25.0% by intention to treat, against 2.2% and 3.9% on placebo [2] (verified). At 12 mg, 11.3% stopped for adverse events, and a skin-sensation disturbance called dysesthesia appeared in 12.5% against 0.9% [2] (verified), a signal the older incretin drugs did not show.","Cagrilintide. Its phase 2 trial randomised 706 adults for 26 weeks; weight fell 10.8% at 4.5 mg against 9.0% on liraglutide and 3.0% on placebo [3] (verified). Injection-site reactions rose with dose to 43%. Anti-drug antibodies appeared in 46 to 73% of participants by week 26, increasing with dose and time; most cross-reacted with the body's own amylin, few were neutralising, and they did not appear to blunt weight loss [3] (verified)."]},{"h":"The antibody finding and what it means for a vial","where":"mixed","body":["That cagrilintide provokes antibodies in most people, even as a pharmaceutical-grade product in a controlled trial, is the most under-discussed fact in this pairing (verified for the trial product). Antibodies that cross-react with native amylin are, in principle, antibodies against one of the body's own hormones. In the trial they were mostly non-neutralising and harmless over 26 weeks [3].","Material outside that supply chain adds unknown impurities and aggregates, which are the usual drivers of immune responses to peptides (inferred). A second peptide in the same solution adds its own (inferred). Nobody has measured antibody rates with research-grade cagrilintide, or with either peptide sharing a vial."]},{"h":"The fixed ratio and the titration problem","where":"mixed","body":["The common 5 to 1 mass ratio lines up roughly with the top trial doses of each, about 12 mg of retatrutide and 2.4 mg of cagrilintide (inferred from [2,4]). The trials, though, escalated each drug on its own schedule: retatrutide from 2 mg, stepping every four weeks [2]. In a blend, every step moves both at once, so the dose of the more nauseating component cannot be held while the other is raised (inferred).","Side effects stack on overlapping routes. At 12 mg, retatrutide's nausea rate was 42.4% against 14.8% on placebo [2]; cagrilintide alone gave nausea in 20 to 47% [3] (verified for each alone). Their sum in one person is unmeasured."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["The FDA states plainly that retatrutide and cagrilintide cannot be used in compounding under federal law, because neither is part of an approved drug or has been found safe and effective for any condition [6] (verified). It has sent warning letters to telehealth companies marketing retatrutide, to ingredient distributors selling it to compounders, and to outsourcing facilities repackaging it [6] (verified). In August 2026 the developer filed six lawsuits against sellers and reported more than 200 referrals to regulators and prosecutors [7] (verified).","Sport: neither is named on the 2026 Prohibited List, but with no government approval both fall in the catch-all non-approved class, prohibited at all times [8] (inferred application, verified wording). If a cagrilintide product is approved, that would change for that component (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does adding amylin to a triple agonist add meaningful weight loss, or mostly side effects? Untested in people.","Does the pair push heart rate or arrhythmia further than retatrutide alone? Unknown.","What are antibody rates with research-grade material? Unmeasured.","Is there an interaction between dysesthesia and the amylin route? Nobody has looked."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Jastreboff et al., N Engl J Med, retatrutide phase 2 trial in obesity","url":"https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2301972","year":2023},{"n":2,"t":"Eli Lilly, TRIUMPH-1 phase 3 topline results","url":"https://investor.lilly.com/node/54321","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Lau et al., Lancet, once-weekly cagrilintide dose-finding phase 2 trial","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(21)01751-7","year":2021},{"n":4,"t":"Garvey et al., N Engl J Med, REDEFINE 1 (ACC journal scan)","url":"https://www.acc.org/Latest-in-Cardiology/Journal-Scans/2025/07/02/14/43/REDEFINE-1-and-REDEFINE-2","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"TCTMD, amylin analogue boosts weight loss when added to GLP-1","url":"https://www.tctmd.com/news/amylin-analogue-boosts-weight-loss-when-added-glp1","year":2025},{"n":6,"t":"FDA, concerns with unapproved GLP-1 drugs used for weight loss","url":"https://fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":8,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025},{"n":9,"t":"Eur J Clin Exp Med, retatrutide in experimental obesity in male rats","url":"https://journals.ur.edu.pl/ejcem/article/view/12411","year":2026}]},"semax-selank":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Was the pair ever studied together?","where":"mixed","body":["Not as a combination in people. No human trial of the two given together has been located, and no registered trial exists (verified as absent in the sources reviewed). The two come from the same Moscow institute and often appear in the same papers, but side by side rather than together: in rats with Parkinson-like damage, for example, each peptide was tested on its own, neither changed motor activity, and only Selank reduced anxiety [4] (verified).","The one direct head-to-head measurement is biochemical. In human serum in a test tube, both inhibited enkephalin-degrading enzymes, Semax at an IC50 of about 10 micromolar and Selank at about 20 [3] (verified). Whether two inhibitors of the same enzymes add, overlap or simply saturate the same target has never been measured (inferred)."]},{"h":"The two licensed labels pull against each other","where":"people","body":["This is the most concrete fact about the pairing, and it rarely gets mentioned. The Russian Semax label lists anxiety disorders, acute psychosis and a history of seizures among its contraindications [1] (verified). The Russian Selank label exists for anxiety states and adjustment disorders [2] (verified).","The usual reason for pairing them, Selank to take the edge off Semax's stimulation, therefore runs into Semax's own label: in its country of approval it is not meant for people with an anxiety disorder at all (verified that the contraindication exists; what it means for the pair is inferred). Someone who feels anxious on Semax is, by that label, someone the drug was not meant for, and adding a second peptide is a workaround nobody has tested."]},{"h":"Licensed products versus the blended vial","where":"lab","body":["Both registered medicines are separate nasal drops from the same Russian manufacturer, each preserved with methylparaben: Semax at 0.1% or 1%, Selank at 0.15% [1,2] (verified). No licensed product combines them (inferred, none located).","The solubility arithmetic is worth doing. A 10 + 10 mg vial in 4 mL holds 2.5 mg/mL of each. The documented water solubility of Semax free base is about 2 mg/mL, rising to about 10 mg/mL in phosphate buffer [5] (verified). On those figures, free-base Semax would sit just above its limit in plain water, so a vial that dissolves cleanly is most likely using the salt form or a buffered diluent (inferred, assuming the figures carry over into the mixture).","Shelf life is set by the shorter-lived part. Reconstituted Semax is quoted as stable only 2 to 7 days in the fridge [5] (verified), and no comparable figure has been published for Selank. A mixed vial used over weeks is outside any documented stability window (inferred)."]},{"h":"Two peptides that are both gone in minutes","where":"mixed","body":["Selank's label gives 92.8% nasal bioavailability, plasma detection at about 30 seconds and levels falling away over 5 to 5.5 minutes [2] (verified). Semax's label gives up to 60 to 70% nasal uptake and arrival in the brain within about 4 minutes, while claiming an effect lasting 20 to 24 hours [1] (verified). In rats, peak brain Semax after nasal dosing was about 0.072% of the dose [5].","Both are cleared by the same tissue peptidases, and both inhibit enkephalin-degrading peptidases. Whether either slows the other's breakdown, lengthening exposure in a way neither label describes, is a plausible question that has never been asked (speculated). By injection, which many blends assume, neither has any human exposure data [5] (verified for Semax)."]},{"h":"The evidence is uneven between the two","where":"people","body":["Semax has had a full regulatory review. The FDA found two usable clinical references for its proposed uses, both small and uncontrolled; in the pain study the authors themselves concluded Semax has no analgesic activity by itself [5] (verified). Its rodent profile includes antithrombotic effects and potentiation of amphetamine-induced dopamine release, both unstudied in people [5].","Selank has no regulatory review in English. Its accessible human record is a handful of small Russian trials, compared against benzodiazepines or uncontrolled, none with a placebo arm (reported; see the Selank entry). So one half of the blend has been examined and found thin, and the other half has not been examined."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Semax: recommended for the US bulks list by the advisory committee on 24 July 2026, 8 to 5 with one abstention, against FDA staff advice; still needs rulemaking [5,6] (verified). Selank: not reviewed; its acetate nomination was removed from the do-not-compound category earlier because the nominator meant a different substance [7] (verified). An interim access policy for certain peptides went to White House review on 14 October 2026, peptides not yet named [8] (verified). A blend therefore pairs one substance recommended but not yet permitted with one sitting outside every category (inferred).","Sport: neither is named on the 2026 list. Both hold Russian approvals, which arguably takes them outside the catch-all non-approved class [9], but the list does not address them and athletes cannot assume either is allowed (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the pair do anything either does not do alone? Untested in any species as a combination.","Should someone who gets anxious on Semax be taking it at all? Its own label says not, and that question deserves an answer before a second peptide is added.","How long does a mixed vial stay potent? Unmeasured, with one component documented at days.","Do the two change each other's breakdown? A simple plasma study could answer it."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Semax 0.1% and 1% nasal drops, Russian product label (RLS database)","url":"https://www.rlsnet.ru/drugs/semaks-5536","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Selank 0.15% nasal drops, Russian product label (RLS database)","url":"https://www.rlsnet.ru/drugs/selank-2638","year":2025},{"n":3,"t":"Kost et al., Russ J Bioorg Chem, Semax and Selank inhibit enkephalin-degrading enzymes of human serum","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1011373002885","year":2001},{"n":4,"t":"Slominsky et al., Dokl Biol Sci, Semax and Selank in rats with 6-OHDA parkinsonism","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1134/S0012496617030048","year":2017},{"n":5,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, Semax bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193348/download","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"American Med Spa Association, FDA committee recommends six of seven peptides","url":"https://www.americanmedspa.org/news/fda-advisory-committee-recommends-adding-six-of-seven-peptides-to-compounding-list/","year":2026},{"n":7,"t":"FDA, bulk drug substances nominated under section 503A, category lists","url":"https://fda.gov/media/94155/download","year":2024},{"n":8,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, HHS and FDA actions","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":9,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List (hosted copy)","url":"https://www.asiarugby.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026list_en_final_clean_september_2025.pdf","year":2025}]},"tb4-bpc-kpv":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Two of the three were finally tested together, and the combination lost","where":"animals","body":["For once a blend's central premise has been examined, at least in part. A 2026 study in a peer-reviewed orthopaedic surgery journal took 32 male Sprague-Dawley rats, cut the Achilles tendon fully and repaired it with a Kessler technique, then split them into four groups of eight: vehicle, BPC-157 at 10 micrograms per kilogram daily, thymosin beta-4 at 60 micrograms per kilogram daily, and both together, all intraperitoneal, from the first day after surgery for 30 days [1] (verified, in animals).","Maximum load to failure at four weeks, reported as medians, was 26.91 newtons for vehicle, 37.16 for BPC-157, 37.41 for thymosin beta-4, and 34.54 for the combination [1] (verified). Only thymosin beta-4 against vehicle survived correction for multiple comparisons, adjusted p=0.0406, with BPC-157 against vehicle at adjusted p=0.389 [1].","Read the combination column again. It is not just that two plus two failed to make four; the combination arm sat numerically below both single agents on the mechanical endpoint. On the histological scores the pattern repeated: thymosin beta-4 differed from control on both total scores, the combination differed from control on one, BPC-157 on neither, and there were no significant differences among the treatment groups at all [1] (verified).","The authors' conclusion is the sentence that matters here: combined treatment did not confer additional benefits compared with either agent alone [1] (verified). They offered overlapping downstream pathways, a ceiling effect and suboptimal dosing as possible explanations and labelled all three speculative, since no molecular or pharmacokinetic analyses were done [1].","The study's own limits are stated plainly and are worth carrying: a single four-week time point, healthy young male rats, no functional testing, histology subgroups of only four tendons per group with no power calculation, no blinding of surgery or dosing, and biomechanics limited to peak load without stiffness or energy to failure [1] (verified). It received no financial support and declared no conflicts [1]. So this is not a definitive negative. It is the only direct test of the combination in existence, and it points the opposite way to the product's premise.","Note also what it did not test: the third component. No study of any kind has examined KPV alongside the other two (verified as absent)."]},{"h":"The three components have very unequal, and unevenly thin, evidence","where":"mixed","body":["A blend inherits the reputation of its best-regarded ingredient. Component by component, that reputation does not survive contact with the regulatory reviews of 2026.","BPC-157 has exactly one controlled human trial, reported only as a 2005 meeting abstract in ulcerative colitis, whose between-group difference had a confidence interval crossing zero, and which the FDA found inadequate for lacking endpoint definitions, inclusion criteria and statistical methods [2] (verified, as recorded in this library's component entry). Everything else human is uncontrolled.","Thymosin beta-4 is the component most often assumed strongest and is the weakest on paper for the marketed fragment. The FDA found no clinical studies, no human exposure data and no human pharmacokinetic data by any route for the nominated substance, and no toxicology studies of any kind: no acute, no repeat-dose, no genotoxicity, no reproductive, no carcinogenicity [3] (verified, as recorded in this library's component entry).","KPV has no human studies, no pharmacokinetic data in any species and no toxicology studies. Its nomination cited nine references, eight animal studies of related compounds and one cadaver-skin study, none testing it in a living person, and it was nominated as a 0.1% topical cream rather than for injection [4] (verified, as recorded in this library's component entry).","So the blend combines one component with a single inadequate and non-significant controlled trial and two with no human data whatsoever, one of which was never proposed for injection at all."]},{"h":"The identity problem, and the one mismatch specific to this vial","where":"lab","body":["None of the three has an approved generic name, so there is no reference standard to test a batch against, which regulators flagged as a patient safety issue in its own right. Each exists as both a free base and an acetate salt with different molecular weights, and in each case nominators were inconsistent about which form they meant [2,3,4] (verified, as recorded in this library's component entries).","One mismatch belongs specifically to any blend containing thymosin beta-4. Regulators stated that the marketed seven-residue fragment and the full 43-residue protein are not the same substance [3] (verified). Products in this family commonly contain the full protein under the fragment's name, or the reverse. That is not a labelling quirk: the two molecules have different evidence, different stability and different regulatory records, so the ingredient list cannot be used to look up what is actually present.","It also changes the chemistry of the vial. The full protein carries an oxidisable methionine; the short fragment does not. A vial built on full protein at the larger share is therefore the most oxidation-sensitive version of this blend, which is why light and cold storage matter more here than in a fragment-based mix (inferred).","KPV is the chemically quiet one: no histidine, methionine or asparagine, so it adds no new reactive site to the solution (verified as a structural observation). The pH preferences of the components do not match, with the gastric peptide's range sitting above the full protein's, so a single blended solution cannot be optimal for both (inferred).","No public certificate of analysis for either common ratio was found in this review (verified as absent), which leaves the mass of each component in a given vial unverifiable from outside."]},{"h":"The ratio is a product decision, not a finding","where":"mixed","body":["At the leading composition the thymosin share is two and a half times each of the others, so by mass this is predominantly a thymosin product with two additions. The even version is a different product in everything but name.","Nothing in the literature establishes that either ratio is right for any purpose, because no study of any ratio exists (verified as absent). The one combination study used doses in a six-to-one ratio favouring thymosin by weight, closer to the weighted vial than to the even one, and found no added benefit at that ratio [1]. That is a single data point, at one dose pair, in one species, against one injury.","The practical consequence of a fixed ratio is attribution. If something improves, three components could be responsible, and the two with no human data are as plausible as the one with a single inadequate trial. If something goes wrong, the ambiguity runs the same way. Nobody using a fixed blend learns which component they respond to, which is the quiet cost of every blend and is sharper here because the rat study suggests the components may not even be additive (inferred).","There is a real argument on the other side, which is convenience: one reconstitution, one injection, one vial to store. That is a genuine benefit and it is a handling benefit, not a pharmacological one."]},{"h":"Safety, as an absence rather than a finding","where":"mixed","body":["The three components drew the same concern from US regulators in 2026: peptide-related impurities and aggregates from synthesis can drive immune responses, and no immunogenicity studies exist for any of them [2,3,4] (verified, as recorded in this library's component entries). Dissolving three peptides in one vial multiplies the impurity and aggregate surface without adding any data about it (inferred).","Beyond immunogenicity, the absences are the story. For the thymosin fragment and for KPV there is no toxicology at all, of any kind, in any species [3,4]. For BPC-157 there are no repeat-dose studies longer than 28 days in any species, and none by the routes people actually use [2]. A combination cannot be safer than the sum of what has not been studied about its parts.","Nothing here says the blend is dangerous. It says that in evidence terms the blend is a fourth product with no file of its own, and that its three parts between them have one inadequate controlled human trial and no toxicology for two of the three."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["No approved product contains this combination anywhere, and no approved product contains any of the three components as a single agent.","The US sequence in 2026 is easy to misread. On 15 April 2026 the FDA said it would remove twelve peptides from the do-not-compound Category 2 within seven days, because the original nominations had been withdrawn; the agency was explicit that lifting that designation did not make any peptide eligible for compounding, and it simultaneously scheduled advisory committee review [5] (verified). On 29 June the agency's own staff urged that none of the seven peptides under review be added to the 503A bulks list, citing poorly characterised substances, limited or absent human data and immunogenicity risk; the same day a reconstituted advisory committee was announced, with roughly half its members reported to have ties to peptide businesses or clinics [5] (verified).","At the meeting on 23 and 24 July 2026 the committee recommended six peptides for addition, including all three components of this blend, each passing 8 to 6 with one abstention, and declined one [5] (verified). Those recommendations are advisory only. The agency must still decide whether to accept them, publish a proposed rule, take comments and issue a final rule, with no statutory deadline; for scale, a 2016 proposed rule on ten bulk substances was not finalised until 2019 [5] (verified).","The live development is an interim route. An Interim Access Policy for Compounding Using Certain Peptides went to the White House budget office on 14 October 2026 and had not been issued as of mid-October. Its expected shape is temporary enforcement discretion for state-licensed 503A pharmacies compounding for individual patients on prescription; it would approve nothing, would not cover research-use-only, supplement or outsourcing-facility channels, could be restricted by route, and could be narrowed or withdrawn without rulemaking [5] (reported). Until it is published, nobody can say whether a three-peptide blend would fall inside it.","In sport, BPC-157 is named explicitly on the 2026 Prohibited List and thymosin beta-4 and its derivatives are named under peptide hormones and growth factors, both prohibited at all times; KPV is not named and falls under the catch-all class for substances without approval for human use [6] (verified for the two named, inferred for the third)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the combination do anything the components do not? The one direct test, in two of the three, found no added benefit and a numerically lower peak load than either alone [1]. That needs repeating at other doses and time points before it is treated as settled, in either direction.","Why would two repair peptides fail to add? Overlapping downstream pathways and a ceiling effect are the obvious candidates, and the study that raised them could not test either [1].","What has KPV got to do with tendon repair? It was nominated as a topical cream, has no injectable human data, and has never been studied alongside the other two [4].","Does the vial contain the fragment or the full protein? Regulators say they are different substances [3], and no public certificate of analysis for either common ratio was found.","What is the real shelf life after reconstitution for a three-peptide solution whose components have different pH preferences and one of which oxidises? Unmeasured for the mixture.","Would a blend fall inside the interim compounding policy once published? Unknowable until it appears, and a mixture is exactly the case such a policy may not address [5]."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Biçer et al., Joint Diseases and Related Surgery 37(3), effects of BPC-157 and TB-500 on Achilles tendon healing in rats","url":"https://www.jointdrs.org/full-text/1851","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, BPC-157 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193343/download","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, TB-500 bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193349/download","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"FDA, PCAC briefing document, KPV bulk drug substances","url":"https://www.fda.gov/media/193346/download","year":2026},{"n":5,"t":"Holland and Knight, peptides at a crossroads, what wellness providers need to know","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/10/peptides-at-a-crossroads-what-wellness-providers-need-to-know","year":2026},{"n":6,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"amycretin":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"One molecule rather than two drugs, and why that is the engineering point","where":"mixed","body":["The competing approach to combining a GLP-1 drug with an amylin drug is to put two separate peptides in one pen. This one instead covalently links a GLP-1 analogue and an amylin analogue into a single molecule, codenamed NN9487 and later given the name zenagamtide [1] (verified).","That has practical consequences, which rarely get spelled out. A single molecule has one pharmacokinetic profile instead of two, so the two signals cannot drift apart between doses, and the ratio between them is fixed by chemistry rather than by formulation. It also means the ratio cannot be adjusted if the balance turns out wrong, and that any immunogenicity or impurity question applies to one new chemical entity rather than to two known ones (inferred).","The oral form uses salcaprozate sodium, the same absorption enhancer as oral semaglutide, which is why the tablet dose runs in tens of milligrams while the weekly injection runs in single-digit to tens of milligrams [1] (verified). Oral peptide delivery of this kind is inefficient by design: most of the dose does not get absorbed, which is the reason for the gap."]},{"h":"The human record, and how to read the big weight numbers","where":"people","body":["Injection, phase 1b/2a: 125 people randomised, 101 to drug and 24 to placebo, aged 18 to 55 with BMI in the overweight to mild obesity range, in five parts with single then multiple ascending weekly doses up to 60 mg [2,3] (verified). Weight change against placebo was 24.3% versus 1.1% at 36 weeks on 60 mg, 22.0% versus a 1.9% gain at 36 weeks on 20 mg, 16.2% versus a 2.3% gain at 28 weeks on 5 mg, and 9.7% versus a 2.0% gain at 20 weeks on 1.25 mg [3] (verified).","Three things temper those figures. The arms were small and of unequal length, so the headline 24% comes from one part of a dose-finding study rather than a powered comparison. One third of participants withdrew, and the investigators noted that 59% of withdrawals were for reasons unrelated to adverse events, including withdrawn consent and recreational drug use [2] (verified). And the participants were young, with BMI largely in the 25 to 35 range, not the population a phase 3 obesity trial enrols (inferred).","Tablet, first-in-human phase 1: 144 people, up to 12 weeks, highest dose two 50 mg tablets daily. Weight fell 13% against 1% on placebo at the top dose, with no sign of plateau [2] (verified).","Type 2 diabetes, phase 2: 448 adults on metformin, about 40% also on an SGLT2 inhibitor, randomised to six weekly injected doses from 0.4 to 40 mg, three daily oral doses of 6, 25 or 50 mg, or placebo, for up to 36 weeks. Injected: HbA1c down up to 1.8 points from a baseline of 7.8%, with 89.1% reaching under 7% and 76.2% under 6.5%, and weight down up to 14.5% from 99.2 kg. Oral: HbA1c down up to 1.5 points from 8%, 77.6% under 7%, weight down up to 10.1%. Placebo moved HbA1c 0.2 to 0.4 points and weight about 2.5% [4] (reported, from the sponsor's topline release rather than a paper).","Across all of it, the recurring observation is the absence of a plateau at 36 weeks [2,3,4]. That is genuinely unusual for this class and is the strongest argument that the two mechanisms are doing different work rather than overlapping."]},{"h":"Safety: what the early trials showed, and the honest caveat about rates","where":"people","body":["Oral phase 1: treatment-emergent adverse events in 62% of participants, 364 events in total, of which 180 (49%) were gastrointestinal, mostly nausea, vomiting and reduced appetite. All were mild or moderate, frequency rose with dose, and there were no deaths [2] (verified).","Injected phase 1b/2a: in parts B to E, 80 of 83 people on drug reported at least one adverse event (496 events), as did 18 of 18 on placebo (72 events) [2] (verified). The near-universal rate in both arms is why those percentages say little; the investigators themselves attributed part of the gap in event counts to a nocebo effect in a small sample [2]. Gastrointestinal events dominated, were mostly mild to moderate and had resolved by study end.","The investigators raised a point worth keeping: rapid weight loss may not deliver the same benefits as gradual loss, and the optimal rate has not been established [2] (verified as their stated caveat). An accompanying editorial argued that the field's question is now cardiovascular and comorbidity risk, and called for head-to-head outcome trials against GLP-1 drugs with proven cardiovascular benefit before judging what these combinations add [2] (verified).","What has not been studied: no trial longer than 36 weeks has reported, no cardiovascular outcome trial has read out, and nothing has been published on lean mass, bone or longer-term amylin receptor effects for this molecule. With roughly 700 people treated across all published trials, rare events remain invisible (inferred)."]},{"h":"Who produced the evidence","where":"people","body":["Every study is the developer's own. The two phase 1 trials were peer-reviewed in The Lancet in June 2025 [2]; the diabetes phase 2 result is, as of October 2026, a company release rather than a paper [4]. No independent trial exists."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, October 2026","where":"people","body":["Investigational everywhere, not approved in any country. The developer announced in June 2025 that both the weekly injection and the daily tablet would go to phase 3 in weight management, after end-of-phase-2 regulatory feedback, with the programme due to start in the first quarter of 2026 [5] (verified). A phase 3 programme in type 2 diabetes followed the November 2025 phase 2 result [4] (reported).","In sport, GLP-1 and amylin receptor agonists are not named on the WADA Prohibited List, but an unapproved investigational substance falls under the S0 class, prohibited at all times (inferred from the class definition)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Is a fused single molecule better than two peptides in one pen? Nothing has compared them head to head, and this is the central bet of the design.","Does the absence of a plateau hold past 36 weeks, and at what cost to lean mass? Phase 3 should answer the first; nobody has published on the second for this molecule.","What do the tolerability numbers mean when placebo groups report events at the same rate? Larger trials with proper titration schedules are the only way to separate drug effect from expectation.","Does it reduce cardiovascular events? The editorial accompanying the phase 1 papers made this the test that matters, and no outcome trial has reported.","How much of the oral dose is actually absorbed, and how variable is that between people? Standard for SNAC-based tablets, and not published for this one."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Wikipedia, Amycretin (zenagamtide, NN9487): structure, formulations, development history","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amycretin","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"American College of Cardiology journal scan of Gasiorek et al. and Dahl et al., Lancet 2025 (oral and subcutaneous amycretin phase 1 trials, with editorial)","url":"https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/07/02/14/43/oral-subcutaneous-amycretin","year":2025},{"n":3,"t":"HCPLive, amycretin data at ADA 2025: per-part dose and weight results","url":"https://www.hcplive.com/view/amycretin-data-at-ada-highlight-efficacy-safety-of-dual-agonist-obesity-therapy","year":2025},{"n":4,"t":"Healio, amycretin reduces weight and HbA1c in phase 2 trial in type 2 diabetes (sponsor topline)","url":"https://healio.com/news/endocrinology/20251125/amycretin-reduces-weight-hba1c-in-phase-2-trial-of-patients-with-diabetes","year":2025},{"n":5,"t":"Novo Nordisk release, advancing subcutaneous and oral amycretin into phase 3 for weight management","url":"https://www.biospace.com/press-releases/novo-nordisk-to-advance-subcutaneous-and-oral-amycretin-for-weight-management-into-phase-3-clinical-development","year":2025}]},"liraglutide":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"Why this one has a different kind of evidence file","where":"people","body":["Almost everything else in this library is held up by animal work and inference. Liraglutide is the opposite case: it has been through full regulatory review twice, carries a boxed warning, and has a cardiovascular outcome trial with nearly ten thousand participants. The useful work here is not defending it, it is reading its own trials closely, because the gap between what they measured and how it is used is wide.","The weight programme was a set of 56-week placebo-controlled trials. In the largest, 3,731 adults with overweight or obesity and a weight-related condition, the placebo-subtracted weight change was minus 5.4% (95% CI minus 5.8 to minus 4.95, p<0.0001). In 846 adults who also had type 2 diabetes it was minus 4.0% (95% CI minus 4.8 to minus 3.1), and in a 422-person maintenance trial minus 6.1% (95% CI minus 7.5 to minus 4.6) [1] (verified). The odds of losing at least 10% of body weight were roughly four to seven times placebo depending on the trial [1].","Note which population those numbers come from: people with a weight-related condition, enrolled with a lifestyle programme, in trials that lasted just over a year. The commonly quoted 8% figure is total weight change including the placebo arm's own loss; the drug's contribution is the placebo-subtracted figure above (inferred from the trial reporting)."]},{"h":"The cardiovascular trial, read as a trial rather than a headline","where":"people","body":["LEADER randomised 9,340 adults with type 2 diabetes to liraglutide or placebo and followed them a median of 3.8 years [2] (verified). The population was deliberately high-risk: 72.4% already had cardiovascular disease, 81.3% had cardiovascular disease or chronic kidney disease stage 3 or worse, mean HbA1c was 8.7% and mean diabetes duration 12.8 years [2].","The composite of cardiovascular death, non-fatal heart attack and non-fatal stroke occurred in 13.0% on drug against 14.9% on placebo, hazard ratio 0.87 (95% CI 0.78 to 0.97), superiority p=0.01 [2]. Cardiovascular death fell from 6.0% to 4.7%, hazard ratio 0.78 (0.66 to 0.93), p=0.007, and death from any cause from 9.6% to 8.2%, hazard ratio 0.85 (0.74 to 0.97), p=0.02 [2]. A kidney composite fell from 7.2% to 5.7%, hazard ratio 0.78 (0.67 to 0.92), p=0.003 [2] (verified). Stroke alone did not reach significance, hazard ratio 0.89 (0.72 to 1.11), p=0.30 [2].","The number needed to treat is the honest translation: 66 people treated for three years to prevent one primary outcome event, and 98 to prevent one death [2] (verified). The HbA1c difference was 0.40 percentage points and the weight difference 2.3 kilograms at 36 months [2], so this is not a trial where the benefit can be attributed to large weight loss.","Two limits travel with it. The population was high-risk, so the result does not transfer to a healthy person using the drug cosmetically, and the trial itself says so. And exploratory outcomes were not corrected for multiple comparisons [2] (verified). The trial was funded by the manufacturer with NIH grant support [2]."]},{"h":"What the safety file actually contains","where":"people","body":["The boxed warning is about rodent tumours, and its wording is careful in a way that is worth reading rather than paraphrasing: liraglutide causes dose-dependent and treatment-duration-dependent thyroid C-cell tumours at clinically relevant exposures in both sexes of rats and mice, and whether it does so in humans is unknown, because the human relevance of those rodent tumours has not been determined. It is contraindicated with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, and routine calcitonin monitoring or thyroid ultrasound is described as of uncertain value for early detection [3] (verified). In LEADER, there were no cases of medullary thyroid carcinoma on drug and one on placebo [2].","In LEADER the imbalances that did appear were elsewhere. Acute gallstone disease occurred in 3.1% on drug against 1.9% on placebo, p<0.001 [2] (verified), and the label notes gallbladder disease was more common than placebo even after accounting for weight loss [3]. Acute pancreatitis was slightly less frequent on drug, 18 cases against 23, p=0.44 [2], which is the opposite of the common assumption. Pancreatic cancer went the other way, 13 cases against 5, p=0.06 [2] (verified) a difference that did not reach significance and has never been resolved.","Severe hypoglycaemia was less frequent on drug, 2.4% against 3.3% [2], which reflects the glucose-dependent mechanism, though the label still warns about the combination with sulfonylureas or insulin [3].","Resting heart rate rises by about 2 to 3 beats per minute on average, and the label says to stop if the increase is sustained [3] (verified). Severe gastrointestinal reactions occurred in 4.8% against 1.4% on placebo [3]. Suicidal ideation was reported in 0.3% (9 of 3,384) on drug against 0.1% (2 of 1,941) on placebo, with one attempt in an adult and one death by suicide in a 125-patient paediatric trial; the label states there is insufficient information to establish a causal relationship, and advises against use with prior attempts or active ideation [3] (verified). Post-marketing acute kidney injury has followed dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea [3]. Because it slows stomach emptying, there are rare aspiration reports under anaesthesia or deep sedation [3].","Discontinuation for adverse events in LEADER was 9.5% against 7.3%, p<0.001 [2]; in the weight trials 6.4% stopped for gastrointestinal problems alone, with nausea in 40% and vomiting in 15% [1] (verified)."]},{"h":"The head-to-head that explains why it lost its market","where":"people","body":["One trial compared it directly with weekly semaglutide, and it is the single most informative study for anyone choosing between them. STEP 8 randomised 338 adults 3:1:3:1 across semaglutide 2.4 mg, placebo, liraglutide 3.0 mg and placebo, at 19 US sites, for 68 weeks [4] (verified).","At 68 weeks, mean weight change was minus 15.8% on semaglutide, minus 6.4% on liraglutide and minus 1.9% on pooled placebo. At least 10% loss was reached by 70.9%, 25.6% and 15.4%; at least 15% by 55.6%, 12.0% and 6.4%; at least 20% by 38.5%, 6.0% and 2.6% [4] (verified).","Tolerability split in an interesting direction. Gastrointestinal events were almost identical, 84.1% on semaglutide against 82.7% on liraglutide, but discontinuation for adverse events was 3.2% against 12.6% [4] (verified). The daily injection was not better tolerated despite the smaller effect; four times as many people stopped it.","Two caveats belong with this. The comparison between the two active drugs was open-label, since randomisation to semaglutide or liraglutide was not masked, and only each drug against its own placebo was blinded [4] (verified). And the trial was funded by the company that makes both, with several authors employed by or financially tied to it, including one who had advised on liraglutide's own development [4] (verified). The result is still the best direct evidence available, and it is consistent with what happened commercially."]},{"h":"Product reality, and why the generics change the question","where":"mixed","body":["This is one of the few entries where a legitimate, characterised product exists and is now cheap. US generic versions of the diabetes presentation arrived in December 2024 and of the weight presentation in August 2025, with more since (verified as stated in the card's own sourcing; manufacturer announcement pages were used rather than a regulatory database, which could not be opened for this review).","That matters because the usual argument for grey-market material, that the approved product is unaffordable or unavailable, is weakest here. A generic pen is a known quantity: a defined salt, a defined concentration, a sterile filled device, a lot number, and a recall mechanism. A vial of powder sold for research carries none of those, and nothing about the molecule being well studied transfers to a batch nobody has tested (inferred).","The molecule itself is unusually robust compared with most peptides in this library: a C16 fatty acid on lysine 26 through a glutamic acid spacer makes it self-associate under the skin and bind albumin, which is what turns a GLP-1 half-life of minutes into about 13 hours [3]. That same self-association is why concentration, pH and excipients are not cosmetic details in a formulation."]},{"h":"Where the law stands, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Approved in the US and widely internationally, for type 2 diabetes and separately for weight management, with paediatric indications and a cardiovascular risk-reduction indication added on the strength of LEADER [2,3] (verified). It is not named on the 2026 WADA Prohibited List, and as a government-approved medicine it does not fall into the catch-all class for non-approved substances either, so on the published text it is not prohibited in sport [5] (inferred from the class definitions).","Does the cardiovascular benefit exist in people without diabetes or established disease? LEADER answered the high-risk question only, and nobody has run the low-risk version.","What was the pancreatic cancer imbalance? Thirteen cases against five, p=0.06, in a 3.8-year trial is the kind of signal that is neither dismissible nor interpretable without longer follow-up [2].","Does the rodent thyroid tumour mechanism apply to humans? The label says the relevance is undetermined, and a decade and a half of use has not settled it.","What happens after stopping? The weight trials ran about a year on treatment, and durability is the question the whole class still answers poorly.","Why does a drug with a quarter of the effect get stopped four times as often? STEP 8 found that pattern and did not explain it; daily injection burden and slower titration tolerance are both plausible and untested (speculated)."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Oregon P&T drug review, liraglutide 3.0 mg for weight management, 56-week trial results","url":"https://www.orpdl.org/durm/meetings/meetingdocs/2016_03_31/archives/2016_03_31_Liraglutide_SaxendaAbbrevDrugRevew_ARCHIVE.pdf","year":2016},{"n":2,"t":"Marso et al., New England Journal of Medicine, LEADER, liraglutide and cardiovascular outcomes in type 2 diabetes","url":"https://lshtm-eprints-repo-prod.lshtm-eprints.cdl.cosector.com/2551429/1/nejmoa1603827.pdf","year":2016},{"n":3,"t":"Novo Nordisk, Saxenda (liraglutide) boxed warning and important safety information","url":"https://www.novocare.com/content/experience-fragments/NovoCare/sf/isi/hcp_isi_saxenda.content.html","year":2026},{"n":4,"t":"Rubino et al., JAMA, STEP 8, semaglutide 2.4 mg versus liraglutide 3.0 mg for weight loss","url":"https://www.gwern.net/doc/longevity/glp/semaglutide/2022-rubino.pdf","year":2022},{"n":5,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"tesofensine":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"It was never meant to be a diet drug","where":"people","body":["Tesofensine began as NS2330, a Danish candidate for Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease. Development for both was dropped for lack of efficacy [1] (verified). What got noticed in those trials was an adverse event: participants, especially heavier ones, kept losing weight. The obesity programme exists because of a side effect in a failed neurology drug [1].","That history explains the pharmacology that follows. This is a triple monoamine reuptake inhibitor built for the brain, not a gut hormone analogue, and it sits in the same pharmacological family as the appetite suppressants that were withdrawn over cardiovascular and psychiatric effects. Nothing about its chemistry is peptide-like; it belongs in this library only because it is traded alongside peptides (inferred).","Potency figures depend on which dataset is quoted, which is itself worth knowing. One set of inhibition values gives noradrenaline transporter 3.2 nanomolar, dopamine transporter 8.0 and serotonin transporter 11.0; a revised set gives noradrenaline 1.7, serotonin 11 and dopamine 65 [1] (verified as both reported). Those two readings tell different stories about how dopaminergic the drug is, and the difference matters for what to expect of its drive and abuse profile."]},{"h":"The famous trial, and the formal cloud over it","where":"people","body":["The 2008 phase 2 trial is the source of nearly every number quoted for this compound. Over 24 weeks, with all participants on a 300 kcal deficit diet and rising exercise, mean weight loss was 6.7 kg at 0.25 mg, 11.3 kg at 0.5 mg and 12.8 kg at 1 mg, against 2.2 kg on placebo [1] (verified). A 48-week open-label extension re-enrolled 140 completers and reported total mean loss of 13 to 14 kg in those previously on 0.5 mg [1].","In 2013 the journal that published it issued an expression of concern, and the detail matters more than the label. The Danish Health and Medicines Authority inspected the trial in November 2011 as part of a random selection of completed studies. At one of five sites, informed consent, which Danish rules require a medical practitioner to take, had been delegated to non-medical staff. Blinding integrity had been questioned in an earlier 2007 inspection and rebutted by the investigators. And the contract research organisation had recorded adverse events incompletely: recurring conditions such as headache, migraine, stress and depression were not reliably captured in participants who had them before enrolling [2] (verified).","The authority's conclusion is the sentence to carry away: the overall side-effect profile as published was not in accordance with the actual course of the trial [2] (verified). The endpoint data and serious adverse events were found to match the source records [2]. The corresponding author himself raised the concerns with the journal, agreed that under-reporting of some adverse events was the relevant finding, and drafted an erratum [2] (verified).","So the efficacy numbers above are probably sound, and the tolerability numbers from that trial are specifically the ones a regulator said were understated. Anyone quoting the 12.8 kg figure without that qualification is quoting half a finding."]},{"h":"The pharmacokinetics are the real practical problem","where":"people","body":["Half-life is about 220 hours, roughly nine days, with 90% oral bioavailability, hepatic metabolism mainly via CYP3A4 and renal clearance of about 15 to 20%. The main metabolite has a half-life of about 374 hours, roughly sixteen days, reaching 31 to 34% of parent exposure at steady state while contributing about 6% of the activity [1] (verified).","A nine-day half-life has consequences that a daily tablet hides. Steady state takes weeks, so what someone feels in the first days is not what the drug will do at plateau. A dose change takes a month or more to express itself. If an adverse effect appears, stopping does not clear it quickly; exposure decays over weeks. And any interaction that affects CYP3A4 acts on a compound that accumulates (inferred).","That profile is a poor match for a self-adjusted regimen of any kind, because the feedback loop between a change and its effect is longer than most people's attention to it (inferred)."]},{"h":"The cardiovascular arm, and the drug built to cancel it","where":"people","body":["At 0.25 and 0.5 mg, blood pressure rose 1 to 3 mmHg and heart rate rose by up to 8 beats per minute [1] (verified). Discontinuation for adverse events in the obesity trials was 13% against 6% on placebo, with dry mouth and insomnia dose-dependent alongside headache, nausea, diarrhoea and constipation [1].","The clearest evidence that the developers took the cardiovascular effect seriously is that they built a second product around it: Tesomet, a fixed combination of tesofensine with the beta-blocker metoprolol, specifically intended to blunt the heart-related effects [3] (verified). A drug that needs a beta-blocker co-formulated into it is a drug with a known cardiovascular signal, by its own sponsor's design.","That programme ran in hypothalamic obesity and Prader-Willi syndrome, obtained orphan drug designation in 2021, and was paused in April 2022. The company stated the pause was entirely due to funding limitations and not related to safety or efficacy, alongside a restructuring that cut about 30% of staff [3] (verified, as the company's own account).","The psychiatric side has never been properly characterised, and the 2013 finding is the reason. Depression and stress were among the pre-existing conditions the inspectors found were not reliably recorded [2]. For a drug that raises dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin availability, mood and sleep effects are the expected risks, and the main trial's record of them was held to be incomplete (inferred)."]},{"h":"The regulatory record, which is a refusal and not a pending formality","where":"people","body":["A Mexican phase 3 trial run by the licensee enrolled 372 patients and reported about 10% mean weight loss over 24 weeks, with more than half of participants losing over 10%, against a safety database of roughly 1,600 patients [4] (verified as the sponsor's account).","In November 2024 the regulator did not approve it. The sponsor's own statement said it appeared the decision had not been based on the full data package as submitted, and that the partner was in dialogue with the agency about a path forward [4] (verified). A resubmission followed in February 2025, and no approval has been found as of October 2026 (verified as of this review; the agency's own register could not be opened directly).","No approval exists in the US or EU, and no filing has been reported there. That is twenty-five years from first-in-human work to no approved product in any major market, across two therapeutic areas and two corporate owners (inferred)."]},{"h":"Where it sits in sport, and open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Unlike most entries in this library, this one is named explicitly on the 2026 Prohibited List. It appears under non-specified stimulants, which is prohibited in competition rather than at all times [5] (verified). The non-specified classification matters: it removes the reduced-sanction route available for specified substances. A nine-day half-life also means in-competition prohibition is effectively a long out-of-competition constraint, since a dose taken weeks earlier is still present (inferred).","What is the real adverse event profile at 0.5 mg? The dataset a regulator said understated it is still the main published source.","Does the mood and sleep risk scale with duration? Insomnia was dose-dependent over 24 weeks, and nobody has characterised a longer course with complete psychiatric reporting.","Is it dopaminergic enough to matter? The two published transporter datasets disagree eightfold on the dopamine transporter, which is exactly the parameter that governs reinforcement.","Why has it failed to reach approval anywhere? The refusal in the one market that reviewed it has not been explained publicly beyond the sponsor's account, which is the weakest kind of source for a regulator's reasoning.","What happens on stopping, given the kinetics? Weight regain after discontinuation has not been characterised for this compound, and the slow washout makes the question unusual rather than routine."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Tesofensine, development history, trial results and pharmacokinetics (encyclopedia compilation)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesofensine","year":2026},{"n":2,"t":"Retraction Watch, expression of concern on the Lancet tesofensine trial after Danish inspection","url":"https://retractionwatch.com/2013/04/09/trial-irregularities-earn-lancet-study-of-potential-weight-loss-drug-tesofensine-expression-of-concern/","year":2013},{"n":3,"t":"Prader-Willi Syndrome News, Tesomet phase 2b trials paused by the sponsor","url":"https://praderwillinews.com/news/tesomet-maker-saniona-pauses-phase-2b-clinical-trial-for-people-with-pws/","year":2022},{"n":4,"t":"Saniona company release, Mexican application for tesofensine not yet approved","url":"https://www.inderes.fi/en/releases/mexican-application-for-tesofensine-not-yet-approved","year":2024},{"n":5,"t":"WADA, 2026 Prohibited List, S6.A non-specified stimulants","url":"https://www.wada-ama.org/en/prohibited-list","year":2026}]},"collagen-peptides":{"asof":"2026-10","sections":[{"h":"What is in the scoop","where":"lab","body":["Collagen peptides are not one peptide. They are collagen, mostly type I from cow hide, pig skin or fish skin and scales, boiled to gelatin and then cut with enzymes into a mixture of fragments [11] (verified). Like Cerebrolysin, the product is defined by its source and its process rather than by a sequence, so two brands with the same label can differ in fragment size, source animal and the share of small peptides (inferred).","What matters biologically is the small fraction that survives digestion. In 2005 researchers fed volunteers gelatin hydrolysate and found prolyl-hydroxyproline (Pro-Hyp) as the dominant collagen peptide in blood, peaking within one to two hours, with smaller amounts of Ala-Hyp, Pro-Hyp-Gly and others [11] (verified). After four weeks of daily intake the blood profile shifted, with a rising share of hydroxyprolyl-glycine (Hyp-Gly), while Pro-Hyp stayed the most abundant [12] (verified). These two dipeptides are the closest thing collagen has to an active ingredient."]},{"h":"The skin evidence, and the funding problem","where":"people","body":["Early trials of specific peptides were positive: 2.5 g or 5 g a day for eight weeks improved skin elasticity against placebo in 69 women, with hydration trending better but not significant [4] (verified). Pooled analyses since then report modest gains in hydration and elasticity [3] (reported), and an updated 2026 meta-analysis put the effects at standardised mean differences of about 0.44 for hydration and 0.62 for elasticity, with no reliable effect on wrinkles and signs of publication bias [2] (reported).","The 2025 meta-analysis of 23 trials and 1,474 people cut the other way. Pooled, collagen looked effective; split by funding, trials without industry money showed no benefit, and high-quality trials showed no effect on hydration, elasticity or wrinkles [1] (reported). Industry groups disputed how funding and quality were classified [1] (reported). The honest reading is a small effect at best, measured mostly by instruments on the skin surface, in short trials, from a field where most studies are commercially linked (inferred)."]},{"h":"Joints, bone, tendon and muscle","where":"people","body":["Knee osteoarthritis: a 2023 meta-analysis of four trials and 507 patients found less pain on collagen peptides (standardised mean difference about −0.58), but every trial was rated high risk of bias and the result was heterogeneous [5] (verified).","Bone: in a one-year trial of 102 postmenopausal women with low bone density, 5 g a day raised spine and femoral neck density against placebo, with a rise in a bone formation marker [6] (reported; summarised by the manufacturer, single trial).","Tendon: with calf strengthening, 5 g a day sped recovery in 20 people with Achilles tendinopathy in a crossover trial [7] (verified), and in young men doing heavy training, 5 g a day grew the patellar tendon more than placebo without changing stiffness or strength [8] (verified). In eight men, 15 g of vitamin C enriched gelatin an hour before skipping roughly doubled a collagen synthesis marker [9] (verified).","Muscle: older men on 15 g a day plus resistance training gained more lean mass than training alone over 12 weeks [10] (reported). Collagen is a poor protein for muscle on paper, so the result is surprising and has not been widely replicated (inferred)."]},{"h":"Safety, quality and who should think twice","where":"mixed","body":["Side effects in trials are rare and mild: fullness, a taste, mild stomach upset (reported across trials). The real issues are source and quality. Hydroxyproline, abundant in collagen, is partly converted to oxalate; in a controlled feeding study gelatin raised urinary oxalate by 43% and glycolate fivefold, and 5 to 10 g loads moved them while 1 to 2 g did not [13] (verified). That matters for calcium oxalate stone formers.","Allergy depends on the animal. Fish collagen is a registered fish allergen, and some fish-allergic patients react to it without reacting to the usual fish test [14] (verified). Bovine and porcine collagen carry alpha-gal, the sugar behind tick-bite meat allergy [15] (reported).","Heavy metals: a non-profit test of 30 collagen products found measurable lead in 37% and cadmium in 17%, with 4 of 30 over its limits; industry called the report sensational and not peer reviewed [16] (reported). Look for third-party testing and a named source animal (inferred)."]},{"h":"Open questions","where":"mixed","body":["Does the benefit come from specific dipeptides, or from protein and glycine any food could supply? Few trials compare collagen with an equal dose of another protein.","Why do independent trials of skin outcomes look null while sponsored ones look positive?","Do bone density gains hold and reduce fractures? No fracture trial exists.","What dose matters? Skin trials found no clear dose-response, and duration seemed to matter more [2] (reported)."]}],"refs":[{"n":1,"t":"Myung & Park 2025, collagen and skin aging meta-analysis (Am J Med), reported by NutraIngredients","url":"https://nutraingredients.com/Article/2025/08/26/industry-reacts-to-meta-analysis-concluding-collagen-supplements-show-no-proven-benefit-for-skin-aging","year":2025},{"n":2,"t":"Batool et al. 2026, oral collagen peptides and skin, updated meta-analysis (J Cosmet Dermatol), via Dermatology Times","url":"https://www.dermatologytimes.com/view/oral-collagen-improves-skin-hydration-and-elasticity-but-benefits-for-wrinkles-remain-unproven","year":2026},{"n":3,"t":"Pu et al. 2023, oral collagen for skin anti-aging meta-analysis (Nutrients)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37432180/","year":2023},{"n":4,"t":"Proksch et al. 2014, 2.5 g and 5 g collagen peptides and skin elasticity (Skin Pharmacol Physiol)","url":"https://karger.com/spp/article/27/1/47/177051/Oral-Supplementation-of-Specific-Collagen-Peptides","year":2014},{"n":5,"t":"Lin et al. 2023, collagen peptide and knee osteoarthritis pain meta-analysis (J Orthop Surg Res)","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13018-023-04182-w","year":2023},{"n":6,"t":"König et al. 2018, 5 g collagen peptides and bone density in postmenopausal women (Nutrients)","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu10010097","year":2018},{"n":7,"t":"Praet et al. 2019, collagen peptides with calf training in Achilles tendinopathy (Nutrients)","url":"https://acuresearchbank.acu.edu.au/item/8v365/oral-supplementation-of-specific-collagen-peptides-combined-with-calf-strengthening-exercises-enhances-function-and-reduces-pain-in-achilles-tendinopathy-patients","year":2019},{"n":8,"t":"Jerger et al. 2023, 5 g collagen peptides with resistance training and patellar tendon size (Eur J Sport Sci)","url":"https://www.informahealthcare.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461391.2023.2232758","year":2023},{"n":9,"t":"Shaw et al. 2017, vitamin C enriched gelatin before exercise and collagen synthesis (Am J Clin Nutr)","url":"https://acuresearchbank.acu.edu.au/item/8724z/vitamin-c-enriched-gelatin-supplementation-before-intermittent-activity-augments-collagen-synthesis","year":2017},{"n":10,"t":"Zdzieblik et al. 2015, 15 g collagen peptides with resistance training in older men (Br J Nutr)","url":"https://acuresearchbank.acu.edu.au/item/85188/exceptional-body-composition-changes-attributed-to-collagen-peptide-supplementation-and-resistance-training-in-older-sarcopenic-men","year":2015},{"n":11,"t":"Iwai et al. 2005, collagen peptides found in human blood after gelatin hydrolysate (J Agric Food Chem)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16076145/","year":2005},{"n":12,"t":"Shigemura et al. 2018, blood Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly after 4 weeks of collagen hydrolysate (J Sci Food Agric)","url":"https://researchmap.jp/read0020328/published_papers/22672099","year":2018},{"n":13,"t":"Knight et al. 2006, hydroxyproline (gelatin) ingestion and urinary oxalate (Kidney Int)","url":"https://scholars.duke.edu/publication/1603001","year":2006},{"n":14,"t":"Kalic et al. 2020, collagen as a fish allergen (J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract)","url":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/65788/1/CollagenAn%20Important%20Fish%20Allergen%20for%20Improved%20Diagnosis_2020.pdf","year":2020},{"n":15,"t":"Alpha-gal Information: gelatin and collagen","url":"https://alphagalinformation.org/gelatin","year":null},{"n":16,"t":"Clean Label Project 2020, heavy metals in 30 collagen products, reported by Nutrition Insight","url":"https://www.nutritioninsight.com/news/urgent-need-for-collagen-codes-of-conduct-after-controversial-study-reveals-contamination.html","year":2020},{"n":17,"t":"ClinicalTrials.gov NCT07456449, collagen peptides and cellular aging (recruiting)","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07456449","year":2026}]}};
